      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-01-26_WidnesExaminer_art0009" n="art0009">
        <head>2 McDOUGAL 1:S PATENT SELF—RAISING FLOUR ONCE TRIED Lim ALWAYS USED</head>
        <p>2 McDOUGAL 1:S PATENT SELF—RAISING FLOUR ONCE TRIED Lim ALWAYS USED</p>
        <p>"WOMEN'S CHAT."</p>
        <p>Many must have been reminded in the course of the present campaign of those lines of Byron, "By Heaven, it is a splendid sight to see Por one who hath no friend, no brother there."</p>
        <p>In the case of this war so many of us have, friends or relatives at the seat thereat, that we are not in a position to regard the conflict with such equanimity,and each nominal roll of cainalties must be scanned by thou- sands with eagerness and anxiety. Looking at the matter from this aspect, one of the most distressing features of each fatal engagement is the suspense which is caused among the immediate relatives of men st?rving in the regiment concerned. Take fer example one sent- ence from a telegram received from Sir Redvers Buller recently—" Two privates Kings Rifle Corps killed." From a national and Imperial point of view, that may not save been a very serious matter, but those of us who have men folk at the front can well imagine the appre- hension which those few words occasioned in hundreds of British homes, and the painful eagerness to learn the names, which they produced. It bas often been remarked that the "Times "is never more interesting than it is in the dull season, when a large portion of its space is devoted to letters from the public. Another London paper fills a good many columns in this way and the correspondence is frequently most entertaining, not the less so on account of the fantastic views of some of the contributors. For instance one writer has suggested that chaplains shculd be sent to the seat of war provided with phonographs,in order that they may register and convey to the relatives, the last words of the dying soldiers. It is to be feared that the phonograph would not always be found available in the particular spot where it was wanted, but in any eveut it would surely be quite as acceptable to the fOOnds to receive the last message from the Chaplain, as it would be to bear it in the metallic notes on a phonograph,with no particular certainty that the words were not those of some other poor fellow who had fallen upon the field of battle. _ .</p>
        <p>Much sympathy is felt for the Duchess of York in the loss of her father. It is only a little more than two years sirce the Duchess of Tech passed away after a few hours illness only, and in the case of the Duke there was no time to summon any member of the family. The Duchess of York has ever been devoted to her father,and his broken health was a great grief to her. For months past daily bulletins have been despatched to her by the doctor in attendance, and when in town, and it was possible to spare a few hours,the Duchess has spent them at White Ledge. The Duchess of York, and Princess Adolphus of Trek —who only a few weeks ago lost her own father—the Duke of Westminster—are the closest of friends as well as slate roio-law. At the time of the Duchess of Teck's death, the Princess was the Duchess of York's right hand and now in this further bereavement, it must be a great comfort to the Duchess that her sister-in-law is at hand, to help cheer and comfort her. It was the Princess's in- tention to proceed shortly to South Africa, so as to be as near her husband as possible but the death of her father- in-law ma s make some alteration in her plans. The winter has been a very trying one, and fatal to several of the few remaining of our really great men. To the majority of people the death of Mr. Ruskin means perhaps less than that of the more popular writer Mr, R. D Blacirmore, the well-known author of "Lorna Deone." For many yparß Mr. Ro,kin had lived in seclusion, and tip., m, at influential of his works were produced when the present generation were unborn, or ,in the happy days of childhood. Mr. Blackmon, wrote for the people's amusement, and his unfailing habit of looking on the bright side of human nature made him one of the most popular, as well as one of the most accomplished of our novelists. Of his numerous books, the best known is "Lorna Doone" and strangely enough this delightful story proved anything but a success when published. It first appeared in 1869 but it was not until after the marriage of the Prince,. Lends, to the Marcsais of Lame in 1871 that it be anse popular. The story has of (mums trAthing to do wto this event, bat somehow the notion got abroad that it had some connection with the R ayal marriage, and the book was bought up very largely in ignorance of the fact that it was one of the most fascinating novels that had ever appeared. When Sir John Lubbock publiahed his valuable list of the best hundred books, somebody had the audacity to enquire whether the scholarly and versatile baronet had read all the books himself. It is quite possible that he had, but a similar inquiry certainly suggests itself with regard to the " Daily News," offer of a prize for the best list of one hundred books for children. Some of us will be disposed to ask what a child can want with a bun. sired books, but several of the competitors it appears consider that they are hampered by the smallness of the number, and that as one of them says" a list of two or three hundred would have been easier to compile." Except for driving, fur lined capes are little worn which is a move in the right direction. Although they look cosy they are really treacherous. When tbe bands are used the chest is left unprotected owing to the out- ward movement of the sides of the cape. This is the more dangerous as so many women think a silk blouse sufficient to wear under one of these seem- ingly warm garments. The ideal cape for driving or travelling is one coming to the knees, and made of care- cul lined with silk. The fur is so light and yet so warm.</p>
        <p>Be or she who desires to foretell exactly the freaks of Dame Fashion, has to keep an eye on relative matters. The milliner watches the coiffeur, the veil maker the milliner, the cloth manutacturer the couturier. Tnis manufacturer has also to watch the trend of taste in colour and contrast. And his task is distinctly a tan- talising one, as his next season's sample must be ready some menths ahead. It seuns more than probable that for some time to come he will have to give us soft fine stuff:, of neutral and sable hue.</p>
        <p>Veils are little worn. The toque is the favec rite form of headgear and frequently so fantastic is the shape as to render the donning of a veil an impossibility. That the disappearance of the veil will make matters better for our sight goes without saying. Sime of the spots and patterns indulged in from time to time must have proved trying to a degree. Hair nets are well to the fore, and a new description, which, in one, covers the front and back hair, is delightfully easy to.adjust.__</p>
        <p>" NiADGE."</p>
        <p>WARIZINGIO'S FOOTBALL CLUB.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-01-26_WidnesExaminer_art0010" n="art0010">
        <head>"LADIES' LETTER.</head>
        <p>"LADIES' LETTER.</p>
        <p>UT DEAR NELLIE,</p>
        <p>The more elaborate the bodice tho better is It considered in regard to style. A home evening- dress, suitable for the winter wear, can be made 3f an artistic-hued velveteen, the skirt long and 'tinging and the bodice cut as a pinafore with full fronts. The top and shoulder-straps should be edged with narrow fur, and the yoke and long, tight sleeves made of brocaded, printed, or plain silk, according to taste. Another nice type of pinafore-bodice suitab:e for a cloth, cashmere, or tine serge gown is cut open in a V reaching to the waist back and front, to chow a simulated under, bodice of spotted velvet. The pinafore part can be edged with fur, braiding, or passernenterie, or even with rows of stitching only. These bodices are almost without exception finished at the waist by a very narrow velvet belt, slightly folded and drawn well down in front. Boleros are still in vogue, and are usually small, with large revers forming a square collar at the back, but a newer and more stylish shape has no revers, but is cut out in a V or heart•shape at the neck in front to show the upper portion of the blouse or under. bodice.</p>
        <p>Panne it] not oniy nued on gowns, but brlchs a great part in the decoration of the hat. he satin- like appearance can be produced by ironing silk velvet on its face! Take an iron which is at once clean and moderately hot, and gently but firmly press the velvet in the direction in which the pile lies smoothest. This last.mentioned particular is easily discovered by stroking the veivet with the hand, when it will soon be seen which way ruffles the pile and which produces a smooth surface. Any old piece of velvet one is already possessed of can be utilised in this way, but before doing so the pile must be evenly raised all over by first damp- ing the velvet and then passing the wrong side over the face of a hot iron, and finally, after It is quite dry, ironing it on the face to make panne of It. The large feathers still hold their own.</p>
        <p>Furs are comforting when the east wind is about, and it will be due before very long. Fur coats that are more than half worn have a knack of looking shabby when tha early spring sunshine reveals all defects, and it is well to have thorn freshened up at once. An old sealskin coat cuts up conveniently into a sleeved Eton jacket or bolero; or if too short for that, will make yoke and revers for a smart cloth coat. Astrakhan or earacul can be utilised in the same way; and even chinchilla, which looks such a wreck after a few weeks' wear, can be freshened up. Sometimes a flat boa may be made of the Liest parts, and this is always a convenient little article to have at hand In our variable climate. A collar for a theatre coat may often be provided from the wreck. Er- mine wears better than chinchilla, but even to its firm whiteness comes the day of rejection by the fastidious. For it, too, may be recommended re- organisation as collar and cuffs of an evening coat composed of cloth, black satin, or brocade in white, black or any colour. To it ermine would give distinction.</p>
        <p>Perhaps you would like to hear of a com- fortable .Liad convenient c.)sturne of the newest design, suitable for girls who are still under seven- teen. The material is nut-bcov-a cloth. The skirt is pleaten as far down as the knees, where the pleats fall loosely outwards. The short coat, double-breasted, avoids a streight line by being cub out in wide scallops all round the edge. It is trimmed with black and white chenille, and the collar and cuffs ars Oorthred narrow band of ermine. Velveteen is rather in favour with fond mothers as a material for girls' outdoor dresses, but It is cold stuff, consisting entirely of cotton. Vel- veteen bodices and blouses are open to the same objection. Real velvet is warm and light. Its cotton imitations are suitable for party Locks, or for summer wear, but they are not to be lightly chosen for winter walking or school dresses. A friend of mine has purchased a smart black and white gown. it is of fine black cloth with a tunic skirt, and slightly poached bodice. The latter is worn over a tucked yoke of white satin, and has a double shoulder collar, the first of black cloth only, the second with a border about three inches wide of white satin overlaid with black guipure lace, a strip of the same bordering, centre- log the front of the bodice from beneath the collars. The sleeve and edge of tunic are both finished by the border of white satin overlaid with the black lace, and the waist encircled by a narrow black velvet band with tie on the left side, the effect of the whole being thoroughly smart, and in the best possible taste.</p>
        <p>Your loving, JEI(ITIE.</p>
        <p>THE MOST NUTRITIOUS.</p>
