      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-01-26_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>"SONS OF THE EMPIRE."</head>
        <p>"SONS OF THE EMPIRE."</p>
        <p>We have received from the celebrated art. pub- lishers, Messrs. Raphael Tuck and Sons, a signed proof of the photogravure, "Sons of the Empire," published by them for the benefit of the Transvaal Widows and Orphans Fund. Mr. Harry Payne's striking picture is reproduced in superb style, the vigour and delicacy of the work being equally well brought out. For the benefit of those who may not; be acquainted with the character of "Sons of the Empire," which is absolutely correct as to detail, we may say that it de- picts four typical representatives of English Scotch, Irish and Welsh Regiments, of whom an Officer of the Guards, holding aloft the Union Jack, occupies the centre. Ranged round in its defence, with weapons "at the ready," are stern stalwart men representing the various contingents contri- buted by the differrnt Colonies to the fighting forces in South Africa, the other Branches of the Imperial Service, Artillery, Engineers and Infantry being separately represented, while the Blue-jackets and Marines also occupy a deserved position, the whole forming a group which, in its picturesque- ness, its power and its concentrated force, well typifies the vastness and solidarity of the Empire Messrs. Tuck and Sons are issuing a limited num- ber of double and single remarque proofs, and artist's proofs, on India, at the following prices : £5 5s ; ; : and 5s each. They may be had from the publishers, Raphael House, Moorfields City.</p>
      </div>
