      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-01-26_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
        <head>EXAMINER, FRIDAY, JANUARY 26, 1900.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, FRIDAY, JANUARY 26, 1900.</p>
        <p>CoPYligU• )</p>
        <p>NOBLER THAN REITENGi</p>
        <p>BY ESME STUART,</p>
        <p>Author of "In the Dark," "Inscrutable," "A Woman of Forty," "Sara's Mother," &amp;a.</p>
        <p>CHAPTER XXII.</p>
        <p>Several hours after Alec had fled back to London, feeling that the whole world was laid with snares set specially for him, James Felton and Sir Harry arrived at Yewdale. as.</p>
        <p>Bob was at the station to ineet them, and a more stupid-looking, boorish young fellow could not easily have been imagined. James scanned Bob's face as he entered the cart, but the stupidity was not overdone and it took him in. Sir Harry did not even suspect the lad; his conscience was too busy about other matters to have much time to spare for scanning the boy's face. Besides, this same Bob had won golden opinions from Rudd, and Rudd was an individual who had to be kept in good temper. It would be most difficult to replace such a sober man, who never interfered with what did not concern him, nor wished to know more than he was told. But all Sir Harry's thoughts were gloomy, and he wrapped a cloak of silence round them. It was left to James to ask a few questions which he thought safe to ask. "Has anything happened, Bob, since we went sway? Has Miss Ruby returned ? " "Nothing as we know on," said Bob, touching his forelock. He did not even look interested in the question which he could not answer. James scowled. He did not know what to think about the news he had received. If Beauworth had managed to escape it was not very safe to be about, for he might prove inconvenient. Then there was all the fiasco about Ruby. He used the strongest language of his most choice vocabulary when he thought of her; but he could not see how one could have had to do with the other. He had not been mistaken. He had seen Ruby in the cab, and so had his father, and they had only missed her by a few moments. He set his teeth closely, wishing he had the girl in his power; but he did not yet despair of accomplishing this. London was big, but the police were an astute body, and surely they would find a runaway girl for a broken- hearted lover and an over fond father.</p>
        <p>On the way, however, James stopped the dog- cart and went off to seek his informant, telling the squire that he would soon follow him. Sir Harry knew nothing but that Alec had been seen in the neighbourhood; but he guessed that somehow or other the Feltons had managed to detain him and keep him out of Ruby's way. The weight of circumstances hung heavy on the squire's mind. He was thinking of Ruby every moment of the day. Where was she? She, so ignorant of the dangers of the world and so unequal to cope with the ways of men. He loved Ruby, and he had tried to save her till the choice lay between them, but that she was saved was not owing to him but to the innate purity of the girl herself. When the squire got home without having said a word to Bob, this latter felt considerably relieved. He wanted to know so much if Mr. Alec had got home without being seen, and he wondered when the squire would find out about the room. On entering the kitchen, Nancy, who would not stay upstairs now that her child was gone, and who was always moaning for her, met him with the question: "Bob, do you know anything about the key of the spare room? Jane says she doesn't know where it is."</p>
        <p>"The key!" said Bob, looking stupid. "Eh, but the squire's come back, and best not tell him nothing about it. He looks that black I'm afraid." "That's what I say to Jane, and she does nothing but cry." "Let me go and see," said Bob, with great assumption of good nature. "Go up, then, sharp," put in Rudd. "I told Jane the squire will be mighty displeased." Bob ran up, and sure enough there was Jane with her apron up to her eyes. "0 Bob! what have you done with the key? I didn't let out on you; but, goodness sake alive, go find it quick!" Bob was rather puzzled himself what to do, but he wished to shield poor Jane. "I locked it. It must have dropped out. Shouldn't wonder now if there is a,nother key as fits it."</p>
        <p>He hurried to the passage beyond and took out all the other keys which he could find. At last he discovered one that would fit, and Jane and he hurried in.</p>
        <p>"Oh, lor I " said Jane, "what a mess! I didn't leave it so. What ever have you been a-doing, Bob?"</p>
        <p>"It was the ghost, girl. Look alive and put it straight, or there'll be words." Jane did hurry, but she looked much puzzled. "The ghost hey quite spoilt this ere sheet, Bob, Why, it's right cut a-two." "He was mighty strong." "Nancy'll never think it can be a spirit, Bob." "Anyways you'd better keep quiet. There won't be another visitor just yet, and you can darn them, Jane." "And here's the carpet all awry." "I had to tussle with him, you see. If I Ir.:* hammer and nails now I'd put it down straight." "You'd be heard, Bob. No, that won't do ; but Nancy is so took up she won't notice much if I just make it tidy." "Whatever you do, Jane, don't you leave these sheets to be seen."</p>
        <p>"Whatever be I to do ? " said poor Jane, who foresaw terrible questions.</p>
        <p>"Ain't there -another bed with sheets? We might shift them." "Lor, so there is Mr. Felton's sheets ain't gone to the wash yet, and Nancy is taking on so she won't come round and look too particular." They so arranged it between them. Bob cared nothing about Nancy. He was thinking how best to delude James Felton. He would be sure to come and see how his victim escaped and he must not find out that he was helped from the outside. Had Alec escaped alone he would have put back the carpet, but by no possibility could he have got hold of the sheets.</p>
        <p>At last all was settled according to Bob's mind, and not a minute too soon, for almost before he and Jane had closed the door they heard footsteps and Nancy's voice bemoaning herself. Jane hurried away, but Bob stood firm. "The key's all right, Nancy, and Jane put the room quite tidy if the squire or Mr. James comes sound." . _ _</p>
        <p>"They won't find my lamb there I Oh, the wicked men to drive the poor child to drown herself."</p>
        <p>"There weren't nothing to drown herself for," said Bob, scornfully. "She wasn't a-going to marry Mr. James, and I've heard the Beauworths never could be made to do what they wouldn't." "You don't understand a woman, Bob. The poor thing's took a fancy for Mr. Alec, and she wasn't to be made to love anyone else." "Mr. Alec is just worth caring about. He's my friend, too." "Your friend. Bob! That is an idea!" "Well, anyways, Mr. James ain't my friend, and I wouldn't mind choking of him if it came in my way. Hist! Nancy, I hear him coming. We had better make ourselves scarce. He is just in a awful temper at not having had his own way." A little later James Felton had locked himself into the room. He looked round and saw a tidy room. The bed was made and nothing was dis- arranged. He looked carefully about to see how it was that his well-contrived plan had failed in such an extraordinary manner. Then he seized the carpet and lifted it from the trap-door. This was the way the prisoner had escaped. He had gone below and had made sure that no one had tampered with the doors, but James wa., almost dumfounded to find that all his precautions had been in vain., It was just possible to have raised the door, but it wanted strength. The whole thing puzzled him, and what was worse he clearly saw his own danger. Alec Beauworth would, of course, hunt down his enemy. He would make an outcry and publicity would be fatal.to James's plans. He must find Beauworth, or he must escape. This was the problem which presonted itself to him, and the contemplation of which did not improve his temper. All his plans had failed, but he was not yet going to own himself beaten. "Who could have helped Beauworth 1 No, the fool helped himself, and all the time I thought it was as safe as could be. Accident or a fall through an old trap-door, not discovered till too late. By Jingo 1 Could that old Rudd be in the secret? Had he-"</p>
        <p>James went out, locked the door behind him as he had found it, and went in search of Rudd, but that individual was so perfectly open and ignorant that even James could not imagine that he had had anything to do with giving a helping hand. Again .Bob occurred to his mind. But how could Bob</p>
        <p>do anything witnout being round out? Besides, ha was a mere country lout. At last he came to al* unpleasant conclusion that Alec had managed hi own escape, that he was ae large and would, mos likely, prove very troublesome. He had been foile&amp; in all particulars, and he vented his feelings in un- measured language. Even Ruby had outwitted him, that innocent, sly minx. Presently he sought out the squire. He found him pacing the hall, looking altogether cast down. "What the dickens do you put on such a long face for? " growled James. "I can't get her out of my head. Where is Ruby? If I knew-"</p>
        <p>"If you knew! Perhaps you do know. I think it is all a plant to outwit me and the governor." "I wish it were."</p>
        <p>"Oh, you do? Thanks; but we didn't come over from Australia to be made fools of. It would serve you right if I washed my hands of you and the precious Ruby." "1 have done all you asked me." "Who's to know? If you had not been such a fool she would have been here, or rather, I should not have been here, but with her on the high seas." "What is up now, James? You are most un- reasonable."</p>
        <p>"On the contrary, I reason only too well. I must go back to town, and I must hunt down that fool of a Beauworth."</p>
        <p>"Then he is about still? I thought--" "You thought! I wish you had acted instead of thinking." More words followed, till both men had worked each other up to white heat. At last James, remembering that words butter no parsnips, be- thought himself of the business in hand. "Look here! I'm off to London to find Beau- worth. He must be hunted down for good and all this time. Give me the fellow's address."</p>
        <p>The squire went to a desk, and took out a letter from Alec, which he had received before his arrival at Yewdale.</p>
        <p>"This is a lodging. He may think fit to keep clear of them. Where's his office address ? "</p>
        <p>The squire gave it to ISni. "That's more the thing I vant. Now it will be a race for life." "For life?"</p>
        <p>"Yes, I suppose he will try to do something. He won't exactly love me. Our last interview wasn't quite pleasant." "You are a fiend incarnate," said the squire. "I mean to find both the strayed sheep. And, by, Jove! when I do, they won't find me a lamb." "I feel sure that Ruby Is dead." "Dead! Don't you believe that! She is a cute young woman, and you pretend to think her innocentl," James laughed. James ordered the dog-cart, and as Bob kept out of the way, the coachman took him to the station. Left alone, the squire felt utterly lost and forlorn. At every step he missed Ruby's face, Ruby's yoke singing her songs; worst of all, he missed Ruby's love. "She did love me, whatever James may say. What a fool I have been; I ought never to have promised. Ruby, Ruby, my poor girl, you arf avenged." He could not eat or steep. He wandered about trying to discover how she had been able to escape, but too much cast down even to vent his anger on Nancy, who lived in dread and expectation of the storm. One day-passed, then another. The squire went about as if he did not see or hear what was going on around him, and every day he mounted to Ruby's rooms. He told Nancy to leave every- thing as Ruby had left it. At other times, he would go into the woods and look everywhere for her, as if she could be there. "She will come back," he thought, "when she thinks that James has gone. Poor child, she trusted me—me! If she had known! " He went to Babbitts-a inn, when it was dark, to drown thought in play, but his talk always came back to Ruby. One evening he heard some news which made him silent.</p>
        <p>"There's a stranger sleeping here to-night, sir, as has been asking after you." "What is he like? " said Sir Harry, quickly. "He brings me news of Ruby, I feel sure of it." "Don't know, sir. He didn't say nothing about that. He was very anxious to know when you would be willing to be seen. / said that you didn't care to see strangers, and he says that he knew that." "What was he like ? " said Sir Harry, looking up quickly. "He's a doctor, he says." "I don't want to see anybody unless they bring me news of Ruby." But the squire looked uneasy. "You'll not be wanting Bob, sir, any more if you haven't got company." "Bob! Oh, I don't want the boy, but he had better stay. He may help find Ruby." The morning post brought him no news from James, and not a word about Ruby from Scotland Yard, except reports of several visits to Rubys who turned out to be entirely different girls to the one wanted.</p>
        <p>When the squire went out to give orders to Rudd as usual, he added: "Has anything been heard of Miss Ruby? " "Well, no, Sir Harry. It veems to be that the young lady has made away with herself. It is the way with young females as is thwarted." "I am sure she would not hurt herself, but I think that she has lost herself in the big city. Why did I not watch her better Nancy was a fool. When I have time I will sift the matter thoroughly." "Nancy, she couldn't help it, Sir Harry; she has made herself quite ill ever since Miss Ruby's been lost."</p>
        <p>"If anyone calls to-day, Rudd, say that I am too much engaged to see them, but you can ask their business and tell me. Remember I don't want to see anyone." Rudd assented without taking much notice. Sir Harry never did like strangers. Soon after this Bob, who was rolling the gravel in the front of the house, saw a gentleman walking towards him. "Is this Yewdale, where Sir Harry Beauworth lives ? " said the stranger. "Yes, sir, do you want to see him? Have you come about his daughter ? " asked Bob, looking the stranger full in the face. The doctor was taken by surprise for one moment, and hesitated. Bob drew his own conclusions. "He knows about Miss Ruby, but he ain't going to let it out." "No, I have not come about Sir Harry's daughter. Has he got one? " "Mighty fine," thought Bob, "but you knew just now, my fine gentleman." "Yes, sir, he has and he has not a daughter, but he'll tell you himself." "I come on other business," said Dr. Murray. "If you ring that door then, Rudd will come to you, if so be you ring loud enough, for he's hard of hearing." The doctor smiled. "You can trust me to get in where I want to, my young man." Rudd did hear the decided peal that was rung. "Is Sir Harry Beauworth at home?" "He don't see anyone, sir, to-day but you might tell me your business." "Will you tell him that an old Australian friend wishes to see him." _ _ _</p>
        <p>• Rudd went away and the doctor waited in the hall. He looked around to see what the house of his old friend was like, for he had found out that it was the same Sir Harry that had come back from Australia fo take possession of an inheritance which had come to him on the death of a young nephew. As his eyes wandered round thinking of Ruby in this quaint old place, he noticed a girl's hat and gloves lying in a chair. But Rudd came back immediately. "Sir Harry's compliments, sir, but he is sorry that he can't see no one."</p>
        <p>Did you mention that I am old friend he knew in Australia ? "</p>
        <p>"Yes, sir, but he said that it was a long time since he had been there, and could see no one now."</p>
        <p>"It is strange that he does not care to talk of old times," thought the doctor. "I shall be much obliged if you will give him this card and ask him to let me see him if only for a few minutes. He will remember my name, I am sure."</p>
        <p>Again Rudd went off, and when he returned the doctor saw by his face that his old friend was obdurate.</p>
        <p>"The squire sends his compliments, but he is very much occupied, and doesn't want to see any old friends." "Surely he remembers my name." "He didn't say he didn't, sir, but he wont see anyone. He has had a lot of trouble lately." "I am sorry for that. I might perhaps have been able to help him." Rudd grunted. "The squire don't need anyone's help, 10,astways I never saw him asking for it."</p>
        <p>The doctor turned- away. lie was deeply dis- ppoinLed not to see his old friend. lie returned to the inn and packed up his hand-bag, tiiing Itabbitts to send a boy with his bag to the station, thinking to himself that he would .explare the %wag</p>
        <p>dna go ny lewclale once more in the hope or seeing Sir Harry. His old affection had much revived, and he felt a longing to hear his friend's wonderful adventures. Why had he given up his theories nien and returned to live in Erand, but this moroseness was like him, only he might have made an exception in his favour, the one man he had often said who suited him.</p>
        <p>As deep in thought Arthur Murray was passing the road which skirted the park, he heard footsteps approaching down a side path. Perhaps it might be Sir Harry. He stepped aside and hid himself behind a great tree trunk close by. He would see if he were much changesl4, though he thought that he should know him under any disguise. If he dared, he would speak to him about Ruby. Of course, he was in trouble about her, and this made him disinclined to see strangers, but on the other hand, the doctor was bound not to reveal Ruby's whereabouts.</p>
        <p>A gentleman came down the path, paused to open the gate, and in that pause Arthur Murray had a full view of his old friend. He looked long and fully at him before he turned sharply away, and walked towards the village. "Sir Harry Bea,uworth," he murmured. "No wonder Ruby fears him, but how changed he is; how changed! What has happened to him. I should not have known him. No, I cannot speak to him about his daughter. I must write."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-01-26_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <head>CHAP IER XXIII.</head>
        <p>CHAP IER XXIII.</p>
        <p>The squire Ns as sitting in his lonely hall two mornings after Dr. Murray's fruitless visit. As he had done every day since the fatal morning of Ruby's disappearance, he looked across to the table where she had always sat. Since her disappearance life had changed very much for the owner of Yewdale. He sat brooding most of the day ani. could not rouse himself to action. If she hae, married, he thought, I should equally have beer without her, but I should have known where she was. Where is she, what is she doing? " His meditation was interrupted by the entrance of Rudd with the letters. Sir Harry seized them , he dreaded and longed for the post at the same time. What might it not tell him, but on the other hand it might inform him that Ruby was found. There were two letters for him to-day. One of them was from James, telling him that he was hunting down Beauworth, who had not returned to his old lodgings. The firm only knew that he was ill, and that he had begged for exten- sion of leave, but that after that he hoped to return to his work. James added that he expected to track him down on his return, but at present he could not find out where he was lodging. Was be with Ruby? That was the question. In any case, the rat must be trapped and got out of the way. It was positiVely necessary ! The squire had never had any qualms of con- science about Alec Beauworth, but to-day he had some compunction. "If I could be sure that Ruby was safe, I would save him—but no, it is im- possible." The other letter was written in an unfamiliar handwriting. The squire opened ic, then he exclaimed: "Good heavens ! " The letter ran thus:</p>
        <p>"Dear Sir Harry—You refused to see me the other day, but I mean to try my luck again. I hear you are in trouble. For the sake of old times may I not see you? I think I could help you. You certainly cannot have forgotten our past friend- ship. Do you ever think of our long talks and of the schemes we made for the good of humanity? You were not very hopeful about poor human nature in those days. You thought that men were ungrateful, and that there was no dependence to be placed on anyone. You made me an exception to prove your rule. Why did you never tell me that you had come back from the wilds, and that you had come back to England? I stayed a very short time in Australia after saying good-bye to you. I went to America, and tried to forget the girl I loved, and who had died in such sad circumstances. You remember that I told you that she was burnt on the eve of our wedding. In America I wished to forget all my sorrow, but I was only pleasing myself, and I could not find ease. Then I had a fortune left to me, and my mother begged me to return to her. I faced the question, and saw that it was nobler to work among the ungrateful than to disappear altogether from the world. I came back, and I have found in work for others the comfort which I could not find in working for myself. How has it fared with you, my friend? I want to see you, to talk to you, to hear you denounce men again, and then to twit you with helping good-for-nothings, as I did in old days! Have you been too much disabused about common humanity? Let me have a line to say that you will see me and that we shall renew our old friend- ship. I think I can tell you something to your advantage, as the papers put it; but at present I may not speak out. Ever, dear Beauworth, your old friend, —ARTHUR. MURRAY."</p>
        <p>The squire read and re-read this letter till he seemed to know it by heart. "Before I went into the wilds," he said to him- self. "No, I will not see him. How shall I avoid it? This has never happened to me before. How could I guess that it would come from the other side of the world? Happily, the Feltons are not here. What can he have to tell me to my advantage? All these years I have avoided publicity. Never has an old,friend turned up before, and now I am in trouble, why should this happen? Is everybody in league against me ? " He got up and went out. There was a spring feeling in the air, and he looked round him as if be were expecting to see Ruby running towards him with flowers in her hand; but he only saw Bob, who at once put on a stupid look. "Come here, Bob, did you see the gentleman who came two days ago ? " "Yes, sir." Bob was longing to hear if Mss Rubyhad been heard of, for Nancy told them every day that there was no news.</p>
        <p>"Well, if he comes again, say that I have gone away. Do you understand? "Yes, sir." . .</p>
        <p>"I won't have any strangers coming about the place. I won't see anybody." "Yes, sir," repeated Bob. The squire strode on as if he were in a great rage, and Bob wondered what he was going to do. "If strangers knew the squire, they wouldn't be in a mighty hurry to come here," thought Bob. "When I knows if Miss Ruby is found, I shan't be in a mighty hula.; to stay neither. My stars! If Mr. James co=,es back again, I think I shall just see if he likes a rat-trap I" When the squire returned from his walk, he shut himself up in his bedroom, and unlocking a box that was always under his bed, he took out the contents and examined them carefully. There was a strange medley of things—old letters, old papers, old mathematical instruments, and a few old books. He opened one of them, and read the name, "Harry Bmuworth." There were even specimens of nuggets and old negatives. There was one bundle of letters which he took up, and which was fastened with a piece of tape, on which was written: "From Arthur Murray." "Here they are," said the squire. He opened a letter, and read with eager atten- tion. They were very private letters, and deeply interesting. One was a professional letter. "From what you tell me, I think that you should take the greatest care of your health. Some day you may develop heart complaint, and any shock will be injurious to you. The wild life you hunger for is very good for you, as long as you have no worries." The squire smiled a little. "I have not avoided worries, and I am alive." When he had seen that all the things were exactly as he had left them the last time, he looked at them again and replaced them in the box, and doubly locked it. Then he corded it, and looked about for some safe place to put it. He went downstairs into the passage which the Felton had used, and the old rooms which had before that Ow so long remained empty. As he was hunting about, he came to the door which James had so securely fastened. The squire tried the door, and could not find a key. This made him angry. He called Rudd, and asked where the key was. It was nowhere to be found; but Rudd said that he had seen Mr. James going in and out of that door. _</p>
        <p>"It only leads-to a- rubbish cupboard, where no one ever goes, sir."</p>
        <p>The sTure sent for some tools andl unpicked the lock. Ile was surprised to see signs of recent occupation. Then he went on to the next room, which was the great cupboard. It was here that he wished to put away the box he had just locked. This lo,lc had also to be picked, and when it was openod be saw—for he had barricaded himself in— all that had formed Alec's tower of Babel. He looked up, and the truth flashed upon him. "This is what James did, and of course, Beau- worth has now got free. This is the end of it then, for, of course, now he is only biding his time. I am lost. Hew was I such a fool as to trust that James? No wonder he has gone in pursuit, but you won't catch an Englishman twice " It was no use puttinc, his box here, and the squire next had recourse '''to the cellar; but it was</p>
        <p>a long time betore tie coula make up his mine what to do with his old treasures. Then he meditated:</p>
        <p>"If I only knew where Ruby was, and if sht would give up everythiniz for my sake I would gc with her, and we 14, ould bury ourselves somewhere but no, it is impossible. She is lost, and Beau- worth will follow up James." He walked up and down the dreary hall, seeking for a solution of his difficulties, but finding none. Should he cut the %hole thing, and fling himself into the ‘ril is of America, as Arthur Murray had done? But the one word, "Ruby," recalled him. Perhaps she would come back, and find no one here. She had loved him, the only being in the world now that loved him, and thought him good —the only one! When evening came he was no nearer to a decision. He would wait till morning, and see if James had any more news; in the meanwhile, he sent off a telegram to Arthur Murray: "Sorry to disi.ppoint you, but quite a recluse now, and see no one." _ _ _</p>
        <p>That would, he hoped, stop all further persecu- tion from his old friend. The next day he felt more cheerful. He received no letter from James, and this ili itself was more than he had hoped. Everything about him went on as usual, except that no Ruby was near him. He hul never realised how much he should miss her. Now the quietness of the place seemed to be almost unbearable. He went to bed that night and slept better than he had done for many days. He felt a presenti- ment that Ruby was not dead, but that somone had befriended her, and that when she thought it safe she would return to him.</p>
        <p>The following day he went out into the woods, and plunged into the most lonely copses, so as to feel unobserved even by the servants. James had made no sign. What if Alec Beauworth had got hold of him It might prove inconvenient—but also, it might rid him of James! The old man could be squared, but James—never. A smile parted his lips as he thought what a relief this would be to him. Suddenly he stumbled on something. He stooped down to examine it. He knew it in a moment. It was some of Alec's luggage. Good heavens I James had, indeed, gone beyond his province. It was safer to bury the whole thing. He must bring a spade here and do the work him- self. He wished James anywhere out of the world at this moment. What he had stumbled over someone else might also find. There were a good many poachers about, and no one loved the squire. They were all willing to do him a bad turn. (lo be continued.)</p>
      </div>
