      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-01-26_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>FRIeJ4I CHURCHES.</head>
        <p>THE FRIeJ4I CHURCHES.</p>
        <p>There is an alarming amount of illness among prominent Free Church people. The Rev. Thomas Law has, we are glad to say, almost entirely recovered from his serious illness, and is now in the midst of his responsible duties at the Memorial Hall and his journeys throughout the country; but the Rev. W. J. Woods, the secretary of the Congregational Union, is still in a serious condition. Mr. Woods' progress has been more seeming than real, and months at least will elapse before his genial face will again be seen in the Memorial Hall. The Rev. S. Vincent, ex-Chairman of the Bap- tist Union, has had influenza. Mrs. Spurgeon, the widow of the great preacher, is recovering from illness; and Mr. Thomas Olney, the well- known official of the Metropolitan Tabernacle, has succumbed to a painful illness. Mr. Archibald Brown, well known for his work in the East of London, has also to be added to the list of influenza victims. This is, indeed, a heavy sick-list.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-01-26_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <head>REV. SILAS K. HOCKING.</head>
        <p>REV. SILAS K. HOCKING.</p>
        <p>Mr. Hocking, who has long been well known as a popular preacher, lecturer and novelist, is now taking a prominent position as a political leader. Mr. Hocking is among the foremost, and at present the most vigorous of those who are against the present war. He has beau mainly instrumental in forming a committee whose avowed purpose is to get up an agita- tion for stopping the war. We understand that an attempt is being made to organise public meetings with this object all over the country. Mr. Hocking is also a member of the committee that Mr. Courtney has organised for dissemi- nating information with regard to the factors of the problem in South Africa. Mr. Hocking is a sanguine man, and he has begun his enter- prise of seeking to stop the war with hopes that very few find themselves able to share. Mr. Hocking is now "a minister without charge "in the Methodist Free Church, and is tile Liberal candidate for the Parliamentary - representa- tion of the Camborne division of Cornwall. An excellent full-page portrait of Mr. Hocking appears in the February Free Churchman.</p>
        <p>FREE CHURCH GPRLSI FRIENDLY SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>We understand that when next the General Committee of the 3,Taiional Council of Evangeli- cal Free Churches meets a s2heme of a Girls' Friendly Society on a Free Church basis will be submitted. There is evidence enough that the Girls' Friendly Society is too much used in some places by the Anglicans for proselytising purposes. In many rural districts it is practically impossible for a young woman belonging to a Free Church family to obtain a situation as a domestic servant, unless she con- sents to abandon the faith of her fathers and become a communicant of the Anglican Church. The main object of the proposed Society will be to secure for such girls mistresses who will respect freedom of conscience. Of course the society wri not be a mere':servants registry. There will probably be a branch in connection with all the 600 Free Church Councils throughout the country, and provision will be made for social intercourse and friendly communication among the members. The details of the scheme will be submitted to the National Council which meets at Sheffield in March.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-01-26_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>TRUE STORY OF DR. PARKER.</head>
        <p>A TRUE STORY OF DR. PARKER.</p>
        <p>Dr. Parker has warned us not to believe the anecdotes that are told about him, but a writer in the February ±ree Church Chronicle tells a new and true one. In a certain provincial town Dr. Parker preached a sermon which contained so many startling statements that at the end of the service one of his hearers solemnly re- monstrated with him. Dr. Parker listened with the utmost patience to the remarks of his critical hearer, and at the end only remarked in a tone that was childlike and bland, "Now, what more of the sermon do you remember Some years ago Dr. Parker preached a sermon which greatly impressed the writer of the article,llllncl on a recent Tuesday Dr. Parker was asked by him to preach it again. On the Thursday Dr. Parker delivered the discourse in words which were almost the same as those employed on the former occasion. No notes were used either time, and Dr. Parker had not given the sermon a thought in the interval.</p>
        <p>REV. J. G. GREENHOUGH, M.A.,</p>
        <p>of Leicester, who has been nominated as Presi- dent ot the National Free Church Council for 1901, has already been distinguished by the greatest honour which Ins own denomination can confer upon him, namely, the Chairmanship of the Baptist Union. Mr. Greenhough exer- cises a great influence in Leicester, and is almost as well known in London. At his best he has no superior as a platform orator. He speaks always without a single note, and his fluency, con- centration of thought, and felicity of language are amazing. Mr. Greenhough has lectured for Free Church Councils in every part of England. Though Mr. Greenhough is rather subject to bronchitis he works with tireless energy, and his speeches are always fresh and piquant.</p>
        <p>THE "INDEPENDENT,"</p>
        <p>which was founded by Edward Miall, and has continued to this day with varying fortune has now -passed, we believe, into a syndicate of various eminent Congregational ministers. Mr. Arthur 'Porritt has quitted the editorial chair, and has joined the staff of the Christian World. We understand that the Rev. W. B. Seib, M.A., of Highgate, has been invited to take charge of the journal, but as we write his decision has not yet been announced. A feature of the journal for some years has been two or three columns of notes on current events by Dr. Guinness Rogers. Dr. Rogers retires at the end of this month from his pastorate at Clapham, but of 'course he will give his services as freely as ever to the Free Churches.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-01-26_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>NICE POINT OF LAW.</head>
        <p>A NICE POINT OF LAW.</p>
        <p>The Rev. G. B. Berry, an Anglican clergy- man at Plymouth, arranged with Dr. Chapman of Western Independent College, that Dr. Chapman should preach in his (Mr. Berry's) church, and that he (Mr. Berry) should preach in the Methodist Free Church. The Bishop of the diocese forbade this arrangement. The law of the Church of England in the matter is obscure. A well-known London clergyman has written to the Times, pointing out that a lay- man may read the lessons, and that the same provision (or absence of pro- vision) which allows this should also in law allow the layman to preach the evening sermon. A Free Church minister in the legal point of view is a layman, and if this London clergyman is right then Dr. Chapman might have preached the evening sermon in Mr. Berry's church. Free Churchmen believe that the words cleric and lay represent a false distinction, but it is hardly likely that Dr. Chapman will consent to enter an Anglican pulpit except as a recognised minister of Jesus Christ.</p>
        <p>EVERY WOMAN Should send two stamps for our 32 page Illustrated Book containing valuable information of how all IrregnlaritieF and Obstructions may be entirely avoided or removeci by simple means. Recommended by eminent Physician the only Safe, Sure and Genuine Remedy. Neve]. Thousands of genuine unsolicited Testimonials from all over the World.—P. BLANCIIMII Forest-rcaO, Dalston, London.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-01-26_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>14:XAM1NE11, FRIDAY, JANUARY 26, 1900.</head>
        <p>THE 14:XAM1NE11, FRIDAY, JANUARY 26, 1900.</p>
        <p>firmary. He and Dr.T.torpe,junior house surgeon at the Intitmary,subsequently math, a post mnrtem examination of the boly on January 1511. tie described the marks externally. The heart was not hoalthy and there were signs of oneurnonia in both lungs—septic pneumonia the result of gangrene. The cause of death was primarily the injury; secundly,the extravasation of urine; and thirdly, ganrene I-, would require a fairly hard kick to have cauncd the injury A kick from either of the clogs pro- ouced would easily cause the injury, and it might be causA more easily from a kick behind. Th.ny concluded fr on the post-morant that the chance of recovery was very roman. He should say that the de;easei was a strong man. Dr • Thorpe, junior house surgeon at the Infirmary said the deceased was admitted to the Infirmary on the 7th. He was suffering from rupture of the urethra and extravasatinn of the urine. He attended him until his doath on the morning of the 15th. He assisted Dr. Curtin at the post-mortem exa:nination, and gave the re</p>
        <p>This conclud,Kl thtl cale for the prosecution.</p>
        <p>For the Defence.</p>
        <p>Mr. Browne then addressed the court for the &amp;Fenn. He said that the magistrates had to decile whether they would commit the prisoner for trial on the most serious crime of wilful murder or oe the minor crime of man- slaughter. As prefacing his remarks let him say this, that that Court was not in any way bound by what the Coroner and his jury had done. The Bench were not entitled to take inl.o consideration that the jury found a verdict of "Wilful murder" and it did not bind them in any way, Teey had to put themselves in the position of a jury and ask themselves whether it was possible or probable that this man would be convicted of wilful murder *pen the evidence given that morning,. What was wilful murder ? It was wllfully and maliciously killing or slaying a man with malice aforethetry,ht, expressed or implied. There must be some intention to commit murder. What wOre the facts? He had not disputed them so far as they had gone that morning. They had proved an 3 proved beyond all doubt, that this man and his wife went to the h,u4Q for the shawl. There was no contra- diction of that. Was the man's in'ention in going there to eommir grievous bodily harm ? N. H sd he a weapon ? N. N) uelayeful weapoe was uced,and he th,refore ven- tured to suggest that there was no case of wilful murder. Suprosine, the kick had happened to a'ight on the de. ceased to inches from where it did no possible injury would have taken place, except perhsps a mere mark. Such a kick was a common occurrence in Warrington, but in this case the man happened to receive it on the most vital part, without any intention on the part of the priso»ei.. It was then said that he most be guilty of wilful murder. Such could not be so. There could be no wilful murder in this case.</p>
        <p>Mr. Birchall : The man said: "I have not killed you, but I wil! do so." Mr. Browne said there was no threat in the first in- stance. He said I..othi'ag until the injury was com- mitted. The threat referred to was never exnuted. Supposing the man went to the bcuse for the purpose of chastising &amp;ceased, he only went there for that pur- pose and not to infliat grievous bodily harm. He ven- tured to suggest that the case was not °us of wilful murder, and he was sure the magistrates woull net cnmmit the man on that charge unless they absolutely found that he had an intention when he weut t 3 the</p>
        <p>puie 'o do some unlawful act or _unlawful injury. If</p>
        <p>they found tint he went there only for the purpoie of chas'ising the deceased, they must commit for man- slaughter.</p>
        <p>Decision of the Bench,</p>
        <p>The magistrates retired, and on their return to curt, The Chairman said they 134 d come to the decision that they were fully justified on the evidence is committing the prisoner for wilful murder. He would be committed on tht charge to the Liverpool Assizes. Prisoner, who reserved hi i defence, was then re- moved.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-01-26_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>ALLEGED MURDER AT WARRINGTON. Prisoner Committed to the Assizes.</head>
        <p>ALLEGED MURDER AT WARRINGTON. Prisoner Committed to the Assizes.</p>
        <p>Thomas Walsh again entered the dock at the Warrington Borough Police Court on Wednesday morning to answer the grave charge of having mur- dered Joseph Swift on the 4th of January last. He was brought from Strangeways Gaol by a warder, just before the sitting of the Court. and when he entered by the prisoner's staircase he looked con- t.iderably weaker than when he appeared a week ego. An explanation of his altered condition was forthcoming, when the Chief Conßtable in asking for permission to provide the prisoner with a chair in the dock, said that the man had been suffer- ing from illness.—Mr. Arthur Browne, solicitor, now appearad to defend the prisoner. The magis- trates on the beach were Mr. T. Birchall, J.P., and Alderman Sutton. The Chief Constable's Statement.</p>
        <p>The Chief Constable said that Walsh WaS charged with the serious offence of wilful murder. He had already been committed for trial on the Coroner's warrant on the gravest of all charges. The facts of the cue had been detailed once or twice before so he needed only to briefly recapitulate them that morning. The dee ased, Joseph Swift, was a forge labourer as was a!so the p-isoner, 1)11,7 they were unknown to each other, and had never met before the occasion which terminated so very seriously. The wives, however, were acquainted and on Wednee- day, the 3rd of January, they appeared to have met ea ,ually in the town and gone to some public-house and had some driek. They subsequently repaired to the residence of the deceased man in Ruseell-so., which was off Winevick-eoad,and there they had more drink. Tney sat chatting together for a time and no doubt got con- siderably the worse for their potations. A quarrel arose between them on account of Mrs. Swift's unwillingnes3 to send for more beer for Mrs. Walsh. The quarrel led to blows and a fight took place between the women. It resulted in Mrs. Walsh being violently ejected into the street and the door was secure"d.When she was turn,d out Mrs. Walsh broke a number of windows in Swift's house and went home. Trio prisoner was working on a night turn at the forge and when he came home shortly beforo seven o'clock in the morning he appeared to have fouud his wife sitting downstairs suffer- ing from injuries alleged to have been inflicte t on her by Mrs. Swift. She made a complaint to him and it appeased to have enraged him for they immediately set off for the residence of the deceased a little over three-quarters of a mile distant. Swift and his wife were in bed when a knock came to the door. Swift went downstairs partially dressed to see who was there. When he opened the door it was alleged that prieoner and his wife rushed in. The wife attacked Mrs. Swift and Walsh made an onslaught upon the deceased. They came in at the back door and the deceased ran away from Walsh through the seullsry to the living room in the front and took refuge upon a sofa. It was alleged that Walsh thumped him several times in spite of his begging for mercy and shouting " For Geel's sake man don't kill me" and "Foe God's sake man don't kick me. It was thee alleged that Swift cot up and tried to run away from the prisoner, and whilst he was doing so the prisoner gave him a kick from behind which alighted between his legs and injured him , in such a way that the injuries subsequently terminated fatally. Swift then went through the scullery and made his way upstairs into the bedroom and secured the deor. Prisoner followed him and tried to force the door and made use of the expression" You old pig, if I have not finished you now I will finish you." Prisoner then came downstairs and said to Swift's wife " If I have not finished him ROW I will go on the stand to-night and finish him." The bolt of the bedroom doer was nearly shot away from the fastens as Walsh used such violence to endeevour to force the door. Prisoner and his wife went away. Swift never left the room again until he was taken te the Infirmary. He complained immediately of serious injuries and great pain and Dr. Curtis was called in towards night. Two days afterwards at Dr. Curtis's instigation he was removed to the Infirmary where he lay in very great pain untll his death on the 15th of the present moreth. His dying deposition was taken in which he stated practically all that he (the Chief Constable) had told their worships that morning.</p>
        <p>The Evidence. The first witness called was Mr: Sutcliffe. assistant borough surveyor, who said that he visited the house No. 2, Russell-street, on the 18.1 a January in company with Superintendent Mona- ghan. He made an examination ot Swift's house which wss the end house of a block. He prepared the plan which he now produced. The house was an ordinary cottage, two rooms upstairs and two downstairs. He examined the thickness of the wall and found it to be a half brick wall. It was possible for one person to hear another speaking in the next house. He examined the door of the front room and found it was a light one. There was a bolt on the inside of the doer hut there was no socket. It was impossible to tell whether there had ever been one. All the plaster about the door was knocked away. The distance between the prisoner's house and the house of the deceased was 80 yards over threequarters of a mile. Mary Swift, deceased's widow, detailed the evidenee which she gave at the inquest. She commenced to nar- rate what took place between deceased's wife and her- self, when Mr. Browne objected to the evidence being tendered as it was no evidence whatever against the prisoner. The Chief Constable : It is leading up to It. The Clerk said that it could be given so long as the details of the conversation were not entered into. Witness then proceeded with her evidence and told all the details of the fight between her husband and Walsh, as before reported in the "Examiner" By Mr. Browns : The previous night her husband had a quantity of drink. She had heard that prisoner had been working all night: Her husband should have cone to work at six o'clock in the morning. Prisoner and his wife were admitted to the house about two minutes after they knocked. Mr Browne : The prisoner said it was something be- tween ten minutes and a quarter of an hour before you let them in. Witness aJhercd to her statement that the dear was opened at once. Further questioned she said that Mrs Swift was not wounded on the head the previous night. So far as she knew deceased did not hit Walsh back. Thomas Hodgett, next-door neighbour to the deceased spoke to hearing a quarrel in the heuse of Swift. He heard Swift shout "Oh man don't kill me !" and all went quiet. George Mullis, a boy, said he was passing at the back of Swift's house at ten minutes to nine on Thursday he 4th inst., when he noticed a woman in the back yard. He heard Swift shout " Oh, don't kick me." The voice came frem the kitchen. By Mr Browne: He wag quite sure deceased said the word "kick" and not "kill." Superintendent Monaghan said he received the prisoner into custody from the Present Relics on Mon- day, the Bth inst. Prisoner asked him how was the "old man" and he replied that he had not seen him. Prisoner said "My wife told me about them having some bother, and also that they would net give her her shawl, and I went down with her on Thnrsday morning to get it, and he ordered me out of the house. I hit him twice, once about the nose and once on the jaw, and his wife hit me with a shovel." He brought him to War- rington and charged him with wounding Swift with intent to kill him, and he replied "He ordered me out of the hoiret and I hit him three times, once about the ribs, once on the nose and once on the jaw. I never kicked a man in my life." Whea arrested the prisoner was eating the clogs produced. He was present when the dying depositions of Swift were taken. The Magistrates' Clerk read the depositions which were as follows :—" On Thursday morning. the 4th January, 1900, prisoner, whom they call Tom, tame in, knocked me down, hit me in the face and kicked me from the back. After I was kicked I reel upstairs and prisoner followed me and said if he hadn't finished me he would do so. I fastened the bedroom door. Prisoner tried to push it open,but I held it. I never left my bed- room till I was taken to the Infirmary, where I have lain ever since. I had had no previous quarrel with the prisoner, and showed him no violence. Prisoner's wife and mine had had a quarrel the night previous, and pri- soner's wife had broken some windows. Cross-examined by prisoner, Thomas Walsh : I sent for two or three pints of beer for our wives on the previous night. lam not aware that there was any bother between me and your wife about a boy named Lennon. You r wife did not kick me on Wednesday night,but she did on Thurs- day morning about the ribs. I did not strike you in the face. I was knocked down three or four times. Re- examined : I know lam very ill and not likely to re- cover." Continuing, Superintendent Monaghan said that on the 16th hest he charged the prisoner with the wilful murder of Joseph Swift on the 4th inst. He replied "I am innocent of the crime." He (Superintendent Monaghan) knew the prisoner and his wife lived at 37, Scotland-road. Dr. Curtis said he was called on the 4th of January to see the deceased. Be found him in bed in the front room upstairs. Be described his injuries. Ho continued to attend him at the house until Sunday. His condition be- came worse, and he insisted on his removal to the In.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-01-26_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>THEY REACH THE KIDNEYS. Dodd's Kidney Pills Cure When all Other Means Fail.</head>
