      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-01-26_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <head>__..4.-. WYCLIFFE BAND OF HOPE.</head>
        <p>__..4.-. WYCLIFFE BAND OF HOPE.</p>
        <p>Successful Entertainment.</p>
        <p>That the Wycliffe Band of Hope Society is doing a good work in the district for the advancement of the temperance cause was evident on Monday evening, when Wycliffe Hall was crowded on the occasion of an entertainment given by the members. The Mayoress presided, and amongst others present were the Rev. John Yonge, the Rev. J. F. Matthews, of Latchford,aud Mr. F. Morrell, the secretary. The audience included a large number of juveniles. The proceedings commenced with the hearty singing of the National Anthem, after which the Rev. J. Yongs engaged in prayer. The MAYORESS, in the course of her introductory remarks, said it was quite an inspiration to see that room, and she was delighted to see so many present. She loved Band of Hope meetings and she knew,that be- fore the programme was finished they would have spent a very pleasant evening. That was not the first time she had been at a meeting of their Band of Hope. When she was a little girl twelve years old she signed a Wycliffe pledge and she had net broken it yet. She meant to go to her grave a total abstainer. She wanted to ask those dear girls and brave boys who had signed the pledge, to keep it even when they grew older, and did not consider themselves to be children. Let them go throuelo their life's work total absteheers, and what a blessing it would be for Warrington. (Aoplause.) An interesting programme was then submitted, con- sisting of songs by Misses Lucy Travis, and Kershaw, and Mr. William Wools, recitations by Misses Furness and E. Bell, a violin solo by Miss B. Bouchier, a dia- logue entitled "The fiewer Queen's court "by a number of little girls, Miss Lucy Evans taking the part of the flower Queeii, and a humorous dialogue " Meta ser• vantm "by Misses Annie Furness, Ada Furness, Lily Wilcock, and Eva Wilcock, Mr C Moarey and Master Bert Foster. During the evening The Rev. J. F. MATTHEWS delivered an address. He said that during the last ten years he had been to a great number of Band of Hope meetings but he did not think he had boon to eine quite so large as that within that time. He was delighted to see the hearty interest the boys and girls in that part of the town took in the work of the Band of Hope. The members of the Bend of Hope were bouad together to work for a threefold ob- ject. First, they wanted boys and girls to keep their pledgee not only whilst they were boys and girls but when they had left school they should be true and staunch teetotalers. What he wanted to impress upon them was, that when they left school and went to work they should learn whilst still young, when the power they posse.sed was fresh upon them, to say" No" when anyone asked them to take any kind of drink whatever that was connected with aleehol. Their second hope was, that by their example, and by their pledge-keeping they were doing the very best thing they could to kill the treffic in strong drink. His hope for the future did not lie in those big places in London called the Houses of Parliament where they sent men to pass Bills into Acts of Parliament. His hope was Rot that they would make laws to repress the drink traffic, but his hope lay in the boys and girls they were training in their Bands of Hope to-day, so that in years to come they should not have to legislate in those great houses to reduce the drink traffic, but there would be sober men and women who had begun their sobriety as boys and girls in the Band of Hope, and by that means the drink traffic to a large extent would die a natural death. He would ereently ask the fathers and mothers not to send their children to the public-house. They did not know the evil that they saw. If they had seen the sights that be had seem in some of the gin palaces in London they would not want to send their children there, but to help them truly and readily to maintain their pledges. They hop,cl for a third thing, that the boys and girls in their Bands of Hope who had been trained to sobriety would help in the future to maintain sobriety. What a bright and happy thing it would be if all the boys in the Bands of Hope kept their pledges and in some years time they would be on the list of voters as men who had Dower, and could use that power against the drink traffic and there would be a righteous and godly England. (Ap- plause.)</p>
        <p>Local Receiving Order.</p>
        <p>A receiving order has bean made in the Warring- ton County Court, on a creditor's petition, against George H Toft, who resides at "Lonsdale," Victoria- road, Fearnhead, near Warrington, and carries on business as a baker and confectioner at 5, Butter- market-street, Warrington, and at Old London-road Stockton Heath.</p>
        <p>When r 'king for Cocoa, insist on haying CAD)I3I7RYS sold only in Packets and Tins as other Cones axe often substi - tuted icr the sake of extra profit.—Advt (1)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-01-26_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <head>WARRINGTON SOCIETY. ANNUAL MEETING. Dinner at the Academy.</head>
        <p>THE WARRINGTON SOCIETY. ANNUAL MEETING. Dinner at the Academy.</p>
        <p>Tile Warrington Sociiry, whose lyq.dquarters are at the Old Academy—a building whicb the founders of the Society deserve every credit for having preserved celebrated the first year of its exisence on Monday with a dinner, followed by toasts and speeches of an interesting nature. Prior to the dinner, however, the first anaual business meeting was held under the chairmanship of Mr. William Long, J.P. (president) and there were also present the Roy W El Drummond, Messrs F Moaks, J.P., F W Monks, WII J Jenkins, Dr Guest Gornall, W U Wood- cock. C Madeley, J Longland, E Osborne, E Bradshaw, T J Lee, W Crompton, J A E wart, J Dann, J H Smet- hurst, H S Page, F Taylor, E V Walker, J Birtles, R Garnett (Wilderspool) ;A Bennett and E J Hall Om secretaries).</p>
        <p>SECRETARIES' REPORT. THE REPORT AND FINANCIAL STATEMENT. The annual report for the year ended 31st December stated that the Warrington Ssciety was established at a meeting held at Market-gate Chambers, Warrington, on Monday, the 7th November, 1899. at which there were present Mr Arthur Bennett, Mr E James Hall, Mr William Long, Mr Frederick Monks, and Mr William Owes, who, together with Mr A H Crosfield, Mr John Crosfteld, Mr J 514.1 in Eielsten, Mr Henry Greenall, Mr W D Houehton, Mr F W Honks, Mr Andrew Rethbone, and Mr J Rymer Young, agreed to guarentee the rent of the Old Academy, and take it from the Corporation for an experimental term of five years, cemmencingeni the Ist January 1899. At a further meeting, held at the same place on Monday, the 12:h December, 1898, the objects of the society were thus defined :—"(a) The preservation of the Old Academy, and of other ancient buildings and local monuments ; (b) The collection of books, manuscripts, autographs, pictures, portraits, sil- houettes, and other articles aseeciated with the Academy or with tbe general history of the town ; (c) the main- tenance of the intellsctual and artistic traditions of the town and neighbourhood ; (d) the encouragement of everything which is calculated to improve the appear- mica and enhance the reputation of the town ; (e) the pursuit and enjoyment of science, literature, and arts ; (f) the establishment of subsidiary societies, clubs and other organizations with kiadred aims ; (g) the promo- tion of any other • objects consistent therewith or incidental thereto." At the same meeting, a code of rules was adopted for the regu- lation of the affairs of the society, but it did not f ermally take possession of the Academy until the Ist January, and the work of repairing, renevating,and appreprietely furnishing the premises, which had been previously commenced, was not completed until the month of April. Great pains were taken to preserve, as far as pessible, the original features of the building. The removal of some of the grates, which had become un- serviceable,and of the lead cistern over the kitchen win- dow,were considered necessary;but structural alterations were avoided as far as possible, and the whole of the work was done under the advice and superintendence of Mr. William Owen (whose services the executive desire to very cordially acknowledge), and executed be Mr. Thomas Davies and Messrs El and S Chandley. In fur- nishing the rooms, the style of the house and the period which it represented were carefully kept in view,Mr.H. Stuart Page materially assisting is the selection, and the greater portion of the work being executed by Messrs G and J Weaver. Mr and Mrs john Booth were unani- mously selected (out of some forty applicants) for the post of caretakers, and experience has more than justi- fied the choice. The Academy was formally opened on Ithe sth April, when the President and Mrs. Long gave an inaugural reeept;oe, which was attended Iby a representative assembly, to whom the aims of the society were carefully set forth. Steps were immediately taken to provide for the cenvenience of the members, and the daily dinners which were insti- tuted have already proved a great success. It is pleasant to bs able to add that the excellent catering of Mra. Booth has given every satisfaction and materially con- tribut,cl to the -success of the society. An immediate attempt was made to start a library of local literature. This is graduallY growing, and the committee will be glad to hear of any book which is written by a local author, printed in Warrington, or otherwise of local in- terest, which may not be in their collection, and, if pos- sible, to acquire it either by gift, loan, or purchase. A loan collection comprising from four to five hundred items, and housed in a book case from the residence of Dr. Aikin, Buttermarket-street (the gift of Mr. Robert Garnett) has in the meantime,kept the society well sup- plied with local literature, and it is indebted to various donors for an increasing collection of local pictures, prints, and portraits. It is hoped that every member will at once acquaint the responsible officials with any object of this character of which he may chance to hear, as the society especially desire to illestrate the salient features of "Ye Ancient Warringten," as well as to keep green the memory of the numer- ous " Warrington Worthies" who, in every age, have added to the old town's fame. The Smoke Room Committee have provided chess, draughts, and dominoes for the amusement of the lovers of the fragrant weed, sad have endeavoured to preserve the literary flavour of the place by introducing various ap- propriate smoke room booklets and other literature. "In order to assist in making the Old Academy a plea- sant rendezvous for people of intellectual tastes," and to appeal to all the members in their turn, the Meeting Room Commictee have organised a series of "club nights," which it is intended to hold, as far as practic- able, during the winter, on the second and fourth Fri- days in every month. Lectures are to be agreeably alternated with scientific, artistic, musical, literary and social evenings, and everything in the shape of stiffness and formality is carefully avoided. Owing to the inade- quecy of the accommodation at the disposal of the society, it has not been possible, as yet to elect ladies as members; but each member is at liberty to introduce a friend, and the executive are glad to note that many ladies have availed themselves of the privileges thus affarded them. The " opening" night took the shape of a pleasant social evening, which was agreeably diver- sified with music and with song, the programme including readings of local interest, and rooms being rendered even more attractive by the exhibition of a number of pictures by George Sheffield and Oswald Garside (kindly lent • by Mr. Thomas Birtles and Mr. Adam Webster). The first half of the session ter- minated on Friday, the 22nd December, with a most successful "Christmas Night." The Academy was season- ably decorated, and adorned with mottoes of local re- levance, and a numerous company kept Christmas in the true Bohemian style till midnight. These "club nights," which have proved extremely popular, will be resumed in the New Year, and it is confidently hoped, will lead to the development ot local talent, and to the formation of the subsidiary clubs or circles which it is one of the society's chief intentions to promote. In , order to still further carry out the objects described ' above, the executive have accepted as tenants the Poly- glot Society, who meet each Wednesday evening, and the Philatelic Society, who meet monthly during the winter. A Book Club has been started by a number of the members, an Arts Club has been suggeeted, and other similar developments are confidently anticipated in due coarse. The erection in front of the Academy of the Cromwell statue, which has been generously pre- sented to the town by Mr. Frederick Monks in commem- oration of the tercentenary of "the Great Protector." and of his close connection with the town, deserves to be recorded; and whatever may be thought of it upon [esthetic grounds, the site selected by the Cerporation is historically most a ppropriate,as the battle of Preston mined at the bridge,and there the Duke of Hamilton's Sceteh infantry surrendered. The original gates of the Academy have been rescued from the rubbish-heap, and re-erected at the back of the house ; and the executive desire to ackaowledge gifts of an iron knocker and three fine old grates from the recently demolished students' quarters in Academy-street. The Academy has already attracted a considerable number of visitors, and amongst others, the Liverpool branch of the Institute of Journalists the Provincial Assembly of Lancashire and Cheshireead the Historic Society of Lancashire and Cheshire. One hundred and twenty have entered their names in the visitors' book, but the actual numbers are considerably in excess of this, and it is hoped that members intro- ducing friends will make it a practice to ask them to sign, in order that a proper record may be kept. From this:hrief outline slits first year's work.the members wit perceive that the society is carefully adhering to its pro- gramme, and is already more than vindicating its ex- istence. Increasing resources will enable it to much extend its work, but it may already claim that it has not only prevented " the most interesting house in War- rington "from being turned to inappropriate uses, but has made it at once a monument of the brightest period of our past, and an eloquent incentive to renewed exertion in the future. WILLIAM LONG (president), • ARTHUR BENNETT and E JAMES HALL, hon. secretaries. The financial statement showed a balance in hand on the nine month's working of £l7 153 sid. The PRESIDENT, in moving the adoption of the report and financial statement expressed his gratification that the society which they formed nearly twelve months ago was being found a service to the more studious people of Warrington who were in want of such a place to meet. He had occasionally made inquiries from those in the habit of attending there, and found that it answered their expectations in every respect, and he could only record his great pleasure that they were able at the time they did to rescue that building and turn it to really useful account in the town. He was glad to see spring- ing up other societies whose members proposed to make the Academy their home. They desired the institution to rest upon as broad a basis as possible with all the members having the same object in using as their home the historic building. (Applause).</p>
        <p>-To MOTHER/J.—Mrs Winslow's Be3thlng Syrup has been usci for over fifty years by millions of mothers for their 2hildren while teething, with perfect success. It will relieve the powsufferer immediately. It is pleisant to taste ; and produces natural quiet sleep, by relieving the child from pain and the little cherub awakes "as bght as a ' ' i.”Of all Chemists is lfd per bottle.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-01-26_WidnesExaminer_art0037" n="art0037">
        <p>The R's'. W. H. DRUMMOND seconded, and asked the secretaries waether any calculation had been made of tee income th .y would r Squire to carry ore the society satisfa ;torily. It wJuld, he said, be als o interesting to know hew much the roams wera used as a club. Per- haps he came there at hours which were inconvenient to the general body of members, but he very seldom saw more than one or two m?mbers there. He suggested the registration of members as they wen': in. Councillor BIiNNETr said the subscriptions had been taken as the ordinary income, no portion of the gua.ran- tors' donations being credited to the income account. The subscriptions:formed the working income and if they retained the present number of members they could pay their way. Dr. GUEST Gowsem. alluded to the expenses of the Reading Room Committee, and hoped no attempt would be made to reduce the outlay there. As a member of the Committee he was able t 3 say that the general opinion was in favour of encouraging the higher forms of literature and discouraging the " snippy" type of reaTng which could hardly be called literature. They must endeavour t maintain a high ideal of lirerature. (Hear, hear). Councillor BENNETT said that during the dinner hour the rooms were particularly well patronizei. Sugges- tions had been coasidered with the view to bringing more of the members together in the evening. The report was adopted. ELECTION OF COMMITTEE AND AUDITOR.</p>
        <p>The following General Purposes Committee was appointed :—Meetiag-room Sub-Committee, Messrs H M Ashton, F R Roberts, W H Woodcock; Reading-room Sub-Committee. Rev E C E Carleton, Dr J Guest Gornall Mr II Stuart Page (librarian) ; Dining-room Sub-Corn-</p>
        <p>rnittee, Messrs James Dunn; t Jackson Lee R Garnett (VVilderspool) ; Smoke-roorn Sub-Committee, F G Fraser, CII Grumly, WH J Jenkins. The only change was the substitution of Mr Garnett for Mr H Woods,who had resigned. Mr. JOHN TUNSTALL was appointed hon. auditor f.l. the ensuing year, and a vote of thanks was passed to Mr Bradshaw, wick) gave his services last year. S-meral suggestions were made with regard to the annual composition of the Comnaittee,and it was thought advisable to consider the adoption of a rule wipraby members would enter and retire from office by rotation A number of new osembers were proposed and elected. THANKS TO THE PRESIDENT.</p>
        <p>On the motion of Mr. LONGLAND, sEc)nded by Mr. BIRTLES, a c)rdial vote of thanks WAS passed to the President. _ _</p>
        <p>_ _ Mr. LONG, in returning think said he was pleased 0 see 83 much life in the society, and anvrhin,g he could d 9 for it he should not be slow to do. (Heir, hear.) After the meeting, the members adjourned to another room where dinner WAS served by the caretaker of the</p>
        <p>Academy, Mrs. Boot% In the meantime the numbers had loc!en greatly augmented, and a large gathering sat down to the go )1 :hings prwilel. To' President (Mr. Long) took the head of the table, and was, later in the evening joined by the Mayor, Nino rec'ived a hearty welcome. A toast list of moderate length was gon3 through during the evening. ThF3 FRESIDVAT prop )sei the toast ef "nse Qaoen, remarking that Englishmen were bound to her by every tia of du:y as well as affection. They all grieved that at the end of her reign they should be embroiled in so frightful a war, but they would be unworthy of their name did they not stand up ia the interests of truth and of justice against a narr3W an oligarchy as ever existed (Applause.) The toast was drunk with musical honours.</p>
        <p>Mr: CHARLES PLINSTON proposed "The Empire." He said there was no Engliihman, whatever part of their dominions he might be in, who did not feel proud to acknowled -re the nerne of England, and considering the crisis through whicn they were passing the sans of their empire all ever the wlrld were proving themselv-s worthy of the old country. (A.pplause.) Mr. W. H. J. JENKINS in.responding hoped that the</p>
        <p>growth of their empire would lead to the development of a higher spirit among them, making them mere tolerant and capable of seeing and recognizing the good qualities of their fellow man (A polanee.) Song " I fear no foe," ear Greenhalgto. Mr. MAY proposal the toast of '• Chrivendom" dwell- ing upon the spirit of self-sacrifice which should be shown by all true Christians. The Rev. F. E. Powste. responded. He said he looks] forward to the time when the Church would be one, a secicty embracing all that was stable in catholicity, all that was of historic order, all that was of evangelical earnestness and siinnlicity,anci all that was of philosophic breath and philanthropic endeavour. That was not yet, but it was that towards wnich they were all yearning. Although there were surface differences they were all at one in their endeavour to rescue ship-wrecked humanity in its many unhappy forms and raise it tea higher level. That was the spirit actuating the whole of Christendom. (Applause.) Song" The Soldier's Tear," Mr. W. H. J. Jenkins. Mr. F. MIAs was compelled to leave before the close and in his absence, Dr. GUEST GORNALL Drl2l3ed " Tie Good Old Town," remarking that it was the coincidence of circurnstaaces and the small pox epidemic that trouelet him back to Warrington, and ieto Warrington's public service. The Warrington Society, he said as well as conserving the memory of all that was best of men in the past, endeavoured in a worthy manner to serve the best interests of the town. Re hoped it would always teach the yoang men of the town a high ideal of service. (Applause.) fhe Mason responded. He said that institution was one of the institutions which caused those who were natives to be prou i of the town of their birth. Its associations with the world of letters and art in itself was a factor of which they need not be ashamed. (Hear, hear.) Part of the programme of the society was to cultivate a spirit of local patriotism, which would bring out man ready to help in the civic government ot the town. They had a right to espeet chit men of ability and leisure, and those Will had not leisure but bad brains and a certain ameunt of commercial ability to do so, as far as they could, all they could for the town. They were at the present time going through a somewhat critical period. They were rapidly emerging from a com- paratively small town to a comparatively lerge one, and with the growth of population there was a correspond- ing growth of neele of various kinds. To wet that properly, expeditiously, and effectually they would need all the ability and energy of which the town was possessed. Councillor Bennett thongh he was occasion- ally and deservedly laughed at—(laughter)--had proved himself to be a very apostle. Ideas which he had thrown out occasionally, and persistently and consistently supported, had come to be adopted as a guide in regard to certain street improvements. (Hear, hear,) So far as the material condition of things was concerned they had reason to be satisfied. There had been an abundance of trade, and it it had not been for the misfortune which had fallen the whole upon the whole country in connection with the war, which took away a large amount of labour upon the calling out of the reserve forces, there would have been every reason for satisfaction. But they had got through such periods before, and they would be the better for the trial, The whole world would be the better for the fact that they had realise I that there were other men and races with rights and conscientious convictions,and who were prepared to die for them. (Hear, hear.) He was as red-hot a Radical as ever he was, butlhe was con- vinced that it was for the world's good that Britain should conquer in this war whatever might be the true cause of it. (Applause.) After a reference to the local spirit of patriotism both in the matter of the supply of money and men, he said he was utterly unable to ex- press the regard in which he held the Warrington Volunteers, and he might say that on Thursday evening a town's meeting would be held to decide in what way their patriotism should receive recognition. (Hear, hear.) Speaking, in conclusion, of the institution, he said that if the spirit which had animetei the men of old would animate the y °wiz people of to-day, they would have no fear for the future of Warrington. (Ap- plause). Song,</p>
        <p>" Even Bravest Heart " (Gounod). Mr. Bar-</p>
        <p>greavea Hudson. Mr. H. S. Page proposed "The Fighting Fortieth in very fitting terms. He referred to Warrington' annient martial spirit,and then alluded more particularly to the famous local regiment who was now forming part of Sir Charles Warren's brigade in South Africa. Be mentioned the interesting f Let that no regiment in the British Army had more records of conquest and strife emblazoned on its colours than had the "Fighting For- tieth," and he expressed a hope that the gallant men forming the batten°, might win further honours and return home in safety. Mr. G. VesTez respendel, Mr. Greenhalgh following 1 with the song " Ho ! Jolly Jenkin." Mr. H. M. ASHT3N was called upon to propose the health of " The Lancashire Witches." which be did in his custoneary humorous fashion His remarks about "the ladies" were responded to by Mr E. J. HALL. Mr. Jenkins thee sang " Good-bye Sweetheart, good- bye." The Rev. W. U. DRUMMOND, in proposing" The War_ rington Society" said that perhaps they had been a little too retrospective. People liviag in Warrington in the past had no doubt done great thine's, bat it would not make them great simply to think of them Their example should be an inspiration to them to do their best for the town in the future. (Applause.) Councillor BENNETT in resoending, thought that the contemplation of the past helped them to do their duty in the present. Did they think that the action of War- rinetoa's first Volunteers a hundred years ago had no effect with the men who lately stepped out co promptly to offer for active service ; that scientiste derived no en- couragement from the grand achievements of Dr. Priestley over a hundred sears ago; or that those who followed literature gained no inspiration from such lwriters as Mrs Barbauld, Dr Aiken, and Sarah Grand ? He repeated that they derived their best inspirations from a contemplation of the past. (Applause.) The proceedings concluded with the singing of the : National Anthem.</p>
        <p>LINSERD COMPOUND' for Couzns and Co Ids allays irritation. Gives immediate relief.—Advt.</p>
