      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-01-26_WidnesExaminer_art0039" n="art0039">
        <head>LATE DR. MARTINEAU.</head>
        <p>THE LATE DR. MARTINEAU.</p>
        <p>Local References.</p>
        <p>The Rev. W. H. Drummond preached a sermon on the late Rev. Dr. Mertineau, in Cairo-street Chapel on Sun- day morning. He took for his text the words, " Death is swallowed up in viceory "(I. Cor. xv. 54). He said he must mingle his voice with the great chorus of thanks- giving and regret at the passing of the great thinker and saint into the unseen. Calmly he passed, full of years, crowned with honour and affection of the wise and good followed by the imperisheb:e gratituie of those, the students whom he trained for the work of the ministry. They had listened so long to Dr. Mer.ineau's voice, they moved so consteney in tee atmosphere of his thought, he bore the burdee of his more than 90 years so lightly, that they hardly needed toe warnings of time. But at length tne sell cenee ; after a few months' decline of physical power, with mind still undimmed. and all the pieties and affectioas of his tang life fresh in his heart, he gently fell asleep. It was not a dirge which they would sing that day, as tnose we° stood sad and per- plexed before the mysteries of death, out a "To Deum " for the victorious soul, who had been one of the century's great prophets bringing life and im- mortality to light, and now iunetited the king- dom and fellowship of the saints. And yet alike in the g:eat world of men and in the more private ways of personal friendship the death of Dr. Martineau left a gap which cull toot be filled. With the solitary exceptioa of Jeha ituskei he was the last sur- vivor of that race of intellectual giants, who, born in the earlier years of the eeetury, and et, ugeling wite the great pre blems of science, peilosophy and politics which marked: its middle period, had 13Nucathed te us the most cheractnestic ideas ot the asedern world. His mind was as rem irke.ble for its massiveness as for its pene- tration. He had none of the nerrevreees of the specialist. He was a ripe scholar in mane fields, arid he knew how to bring the various departments of knewledge into helpful relation,and to make them c mtribute to the life and thought of religian,wh'ch was alweye the dominant interest of his mind. His intellectual gifts were used with the modesty of the Cnristian disciple. He regarded them simply as enabling him te reader wider service to the spiritual needs of his age ; and he never blinded himself either to the greatness of the need or to the reality of the conflict between faii:h and unbelief. On the one hand were men who were using the weapons of science and criticism to un der.nine the foundations of theistic belief ; on the other was a group of teachers who sought to rescue the ship- wrecked by a fresh insistence upon the authority of the Church and a tightening of the bonds of discipline. Dr. Martineau stood abet from both these camps, and bide men find Gal in tee conscience and the heart. He seldom argue I with them, bet in the measured eloquence wince was an inseparable part of his personality he made them feel that the life of religion in the soul and all the graces and harmonies of the Christian character were imperish- ableitnat these were the things which lay deeper than the divisions of the sects and the drift of human thought. His influence was unlike anything else that they had known, as of one who lived far off from the meaner thoughts of men, who was untouched by the coarser ambitions of the world. As age only added dignity to the bearing and sweetness tO the face, he movea among them like some prophet-saint of the older time, a living witness which none could gainsay to the spiritual splendours of our humanity. He was the last of the greet Puritans, not in the narrow and one-sided sense in which the word was so often used, but as Milton was a Puritan. With his wide aultivation of mind, his delicate and discriminating taste for inusic and art, his gracious dignity of manner, his loee for all things human, there was cernprieed a simplLity of life, a regularity of habit, a pervading tone of morel severity ane‘of direct personal responsibility for every thought and word to, the great S.' archer of hearts. In his presence it was en_ possible to take their (elite of Olf indulgence lightly, or to be content with anythieg less difficult than the braced will and the holy and consecrated mind. By ancestry and by long and unshaken conviction Dr. Martineau belonged to the small greet) of churches which formed the extreme left wing of English Noncon- formity. He loved their hoionrable traditions, he loved their congregations as in a peculiar sense his own people, he lovel above all the simplicity of their wor- ship and the freedom of religious thought. In the hymn books which ho edited for releir use, in the Pro/Mete:a words of his prayers, and lit e‘gica/ forms of worship he had left them a great reegious literature, which was already becoming coasecret:ed by long habit and intimate religious associations. Bu; undoubtedly his greateet gift to their common church efts was to be found in the men whom for more than fori, years he sent forth as minis- ters of the gospel, traleed under his eye. It was a debt the greatness of white) i: was impossibte to over-estimate that during a pen i od of intellectual upbeaval and religious difli rutty their best minds were thus enabled to feel the influence of the keenest religious thinker of time and to catch some of the tones of his own unshaken trust in the eternal verities of religion. In one other direction his influence was so characteristic and at the same time so pewerful, and by some so little understood, that it most he referred to very briefly. Dr. Martineau was an apostle of religious toleranee and comprehension. He was ever haunted by the vision of a church catholic as the charity of Christ. Re was keenly- alive to the perils of sect life and the narrowness of the sectarian spirit. He believed that the true foundation for the fellowship of worship was secured not by uniformity of belief or opinion, but must have its toot in the common senti- ments and affections of the Christian heart. Staunch and unbending in his individual convictions, he at all times refused to transfer his own theological label to the church or its ministry, or to do anything which might seem to imply that the assembly of worshippers met for the promotion of a peed or for controversy with its neighbours insfead of for nurture of the religious life and prayer to the living God.</p>
        <p>At the Bold-street Wesleyan Chapel, on Sunday morn- ing, the Rev. H. G. Roberts said he need not apo'oeise for referring to Dr. Mertineau in a Methocliet Church for although, intellectually far removed from them as Methodists, yet all churches were indebted to him for the magnificent defence of Theism, and the intuition of the soul. Dr. Martineau held the first place as a Chris- tian philosopher, and he wished to publicly recognise his personal indebtedneis ta him. He could dwell on the glory of his mind. It was acknowledged by some that his boArs had s.Yed England from the ruin of the philosiphy of Spencer, and the materialism of auxley and Tyndall. Glad- stone had said that Dr. Martine iu war the greatest living thinker of the day. They hal only to look at his picture, and they could see the evidence of his lofty spirituality. Martineau had a nohle vision of the divine and wrote and lived as one to w:loen the consciouress of the infinite and the divine were as real as the axioms of Euclid. He heard last night that Ruskin had passed away. He supposed the rop4rt was perfectly true. Their great men were peeling away one after another, and they could not afford to lose such men—two of the greatest philosophers and moralints of the 19th century— especially as there wee such a tendency t7-day, even in literature, to trifle with morality. He was glad to say that Ruskin bowed his head in the nr-s3ace of God. Be- fore the matchless style (-)f the Bible he ackno 'lodged it to he the grandest group of writinge existent in the rational world. Thank God the century had had such men. The greatest leaders of both plitical parties had recognised the teaching of Christ. The greatest poets of the,century,Tennyson end Browning,and the greatest philosophers and moralists, Martineau and Ruskin, had also been disciples of Christ's. There was no fear of the future so long as they had such men, and so long as religious sentiments swayed their greatest intellects.</p>
        <p>Warrington Field Club.</p>
        <p>At the opening meeting of the second half of the winter session, Mr. G. ElliQon of Liverpool. read a paper on " A Trip up the West Coast to the Ork- neys." His descriptions of the various picturesque places on the route indicated were much enjoyed. He illustrated his subjeet with a number of beauti- ful water-colour sketcheQ,and also exhibited samples of Orkney antiquities. Beside:, his artistic and archmllogical faculties. Mr. Ellison is endowed with something of the zeal of the naturalist, and had caught quite a number of spiders during a part of his tour spent in Skye. Thes included representa- tives of ten species,all of which occur in the neigh- bourhood of Warrington. A short sketch of Mr. Ellison's znological captures was given by Mr. L. Greening, F L.S., to whom they had been submitted for examination. In the absence through illness of Mr. J. Shaw Green, the chair was filled by Mr. Madeley, and at the clo,e of the evening a hearty vote of thanks was accorded to Mr. Ellison for his paper.</p>
