      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-01-26_WidnesExaminer_art0050" n="art0050">
        <head>• _ _ 414 . 4 7 – –</head>
        <p>• _ _ 414 . 4 7 - -</p>
        <p>" PuNcil " is right. The Saying the same members of the Governmeut Thing.</p>
        <p>must agree to say the same thing about the war,in accordance with the advice which Lord SALISBURY is depicted as gravely tendering to the Cabinet. The policy hitherto has been a little discordant. The line of policy adopted by Mr. BALFOUR has been one of inevitableness and ignorance. The Government did not know and could not know, we have been told, that the Boers were so well armed, so ready,so skilful in obtaining the assistance of military adventurers, so intent upon surprising us by an advance into British territory, so likely to find us unprepared. If this be the drift of the defence which is to be offered when Parliament meets, it will not be difficult to demolish it, and to discredit the Government. But can this policy of saying the same thing be con.. sistently maintained ? Mr. MURRAY has been challenging his opponent at York to deal with Sir WILLIAM BUTLER'S warnings and his recommenda-, tions, and we hope Mr. FABER will take an oppor- tunity of obtaining some instructions from Con- servative headquarters and his own leaders as to what he is to say in reply. Can the existence of these warnings and suggestions be denied? We shall see. Another count in the indictment will be the ignorance of the War Office. Was it duly informed by the Intelligence Department as to the guns, rifle,s, and military stores which had been accumulated by the Boer Republics? Report has it that for some years past copies of all the way- bills of guns and ammunition passing into the Transvaal have been sent home, either to the Colonial Office or the War Office. Have these copies been r,ceived ? If the Colorlial Office had them, were they duly forwarded to tho Intelligence Department of the War Office? Did Mr. CHAMBERLAIN begin to form his idea that war was " inevitable " from these documents, and did he retain these evidences of warlike intentions in his own custody? These are some of the questions sure to be asked in Parliament, and if Ministers are to take Mr. " Punch's " advice, what is the same thing they are going to say? Mr. BALFOUR has declared that the man in the street knew as much as the Government did. If this is the same thing which is to be said, it is equivalent to a verdict of incapacity being assented to by the Cabinet, in the lace of the country and the world. An Administration of this ignorant nature dees not deserve to remain in power, and it will stand self-condemned, even if the Opposition is merciful and the supporters of Lord SALISBURY are silent and forgiving. Political expediency may require that the men who blundered so strangely, alike in negotiations, in knowledge, and in preparation, should be com- pelled to conduct the war to a successful.termina- tion. What LORD ROSEBERY calls our prestige— at best an ugly word—is at stake ; but when the end has come the country will know how to deal with the bluaderers, and it will not spare them. The whole of the patriots will then be saying the same thing—we cannot trust a Coalition Govern- ment any longer, and it must be swept away. A. feeling of this kind is alrewly taking possessioi of many Unionists,who will find it extremely perilous to say to constituents what may have appeared plausible in Parliament. Had the war been in Europe, had we been caught in the same condition the result would have been damaging to our general position, and our pride would have been terribly shaken by great disasters. To avoid such changes, we mist change a good many things be- sides the personnel of the Government. We must return to the good old policy, the safe old paths of peace and reform.</p>
        <p>THE proposal to regard Railway Servants and railway service as dan- the State.</p>
        <p>gerous, and to place it under the guardianship of a department of the State, is a very serious matter for everybody. The amount of freedom already enjoyed by the railway companies must be very considerably curtailed, and the question arises, what will be the corres- ponding advantages? It is contended that any trade in which seventy-eight per thousand of its members are annually injured must be pro- nounced dangerous, and to that proposition there will probably be general assent. If the proportion of deaths and injuries can be substantially reduced it must ultimately be a benefit to the companies, who have to give compensation, and to the men they employ, who have to work under such dangerous conditions. The force of these considerations is very great, and without them it would be difficult to understand how a Royal Commission, which set out to ascertain</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-01-26_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="1" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-01-26_WidnesExaminer_art0052" n="art0052">
        <p>how shunting and other accidents could best be' reduced, should have recommended that to secure uniformity of action and enforce the best possible regulations it was necessary to suggest the con- stitution of a department of the State "with cer- tain powers for the inspection and regulation of the work done by shanters, goods guards, brakes- men, and platelayers." The advance is remarkable, if it logically follows from the primary decision ' that such work on railways comes under the general designation of dangerous. The railway companies are a powerful body, and we feel sure they will not like the interference proposed, and that legislation on the subject will be very difficult. However, there may be reasons why the com- panies should prefer this method of dealing with the subject to the plan originally proposed, of im- posing upon them stringent and costly methods in the way of automatic couplings. The Commission is unable to decide what form of coupling should be adopted, and it makes a number of minor and very j useful sugcestions as to brakes, levers, cabins, and 'the more effeCoive illumination of shunting yards, Sheds) and sidings. Some persons will regard these minute suggestions as almost sufficient to meet the evils complained of, without any change in present regulations as to the powers of railway companies. The answer to any such contention is that when regulations are made they must be rendered flexiole, to suit the varying conditions of work; and there must be some enforcing authority• The authority proposed is, of course, the Board of Trade, which would have to be legally invested With the new powers, and be strengthened to meet the extra work imposed. It has already a rail- way department, and that would be the authority. The companies, however, would be allowed a right of appeal, either to the Railway and Canal Cournissioners or a single member thereof, sitting in chambers, in minor cases. A special tribunal of arbitration is to be constituted in certain cases. The servants of the various rail- way companies will probably not object to the changes which have been outlined, though as a rule they may prefer to be under private rather than Government rules. It is doubtful whether the general public will raise much objection to the scheme the Commission has advised. The resist- ance is more likely to come from the railway com- panies themselves, who regard with anything but equanimity the general advance of State control. But they will not act hastily, even if they at first decline to be enthusiastic, and the probability is that as they will save money in the end they will accept the new authority with a good grace.</p>
        <p>It is probable that Ladysmith will rank in his- tory as one of the great sieges which a British force has withstood. These sieges have not been numerous. The fortune of war usually wills that the British shall invest places rather than be themselves invested, and our laurels in this one respect, therefore, are not, perhaps, so conspicu- ous as those of our great rivals. The defence of Lucknow was an achievement of which any nation might be proud. But it is a moot point whether We have hitherto done so well as Osman at Plevna or Todleben at Sebastopol. Ladysmith is a splen- did example of what British soldiers can do. Sebastopol had wonderful defences, corapleted by an engineer of remarkable genius; Plevna had natural and artificial defences of great importance. At Ladysmith, however, all the advantage lay with the besiegers, who have had 10,000 men accustomed to an active life, with a large number of townspeople and natives, cooped up in a narrow plain commanded by eminences from which it could be raked. No great military commander, except when compelled by circumstances beyond his control, would have retained Ladysmith as a position where a supreme effort might be made. The circumstances were beyond Sir George White's control. The immense accumulation of stores and ammunition compelled him to keep the place. A supreme effort has been rnade,and in spite of a bad situation,decimation by disease and continual fighting the gallant town has so far successfully defied the military skill of its invaders.</p>
        <p>The subscribers of a Kansas daily newspaper are Probably looking forward with a mixture @f trepi- dation and curiosity to the week beginning March, 13th, when the paper for six days will pass under the supreme control of the Rev. Charles Sheldon, author of "In His Steps.- It was a very happy thought of the wideawake proprietor to advertise his newspaper in this manner. A host of readers Who have heard of and believe in Mr. Sheldon's views on newspaper management will be attracted, While the regular subscribers may be trusted to endure the ordeal patiently, if not with gladness. Mr. Sheldon declares that he hopes to conduct the paper in a way that would commend itself to the Founder of Christianity. Apparently, he Makes light of the task, and does not foresee any _difficulties. What will he do with the police and law reports for instance? The saying," Let him that is with:out sin amongst you, seems to pro- hibit this or any other form of social retaliation upon the sinners against society. There is scarcely a felture in the modern newspapers against which Plausible objections might not be manufactured. Mr. Sheldon bßlieves he can reckon with all these, and yet produce a paper that people will read with interest and advertise in extensively. The experi- ment was worth making, but Mr. Sheldon, who lays claim to some business capacity, is, we fear, quite alone among business men in believing that the task is simple.</p>
        <p>The Warrington Volunteers who are awaiting orders to embark .1 or South Africa have now had a fortnight's experience of real barrack life. No longer can the cynic point to them as" Saturday after- noon" soldiers or cast insinuating remarks as to their weakness to .pleasantly enjoy a week's holi- day in camp at the seaside. By their action in the present National crisis our Volunteers have proved themselves not only " Soldiers of the Queen" in name but in reality. When men are prepared to leave their homes and occupations for suck a rough and thankless task as is before them in South Africa they do at least deserve some credit for their patriotism. The fro3Lionl with which Volunteers throughout the country are responding ,to the call to arms has more than justified the exis- tence of Volunteer corps and will appreciably enhance their value from a military standpoint. " Defence not Defiance has long been the motto of our Volunteers but in future in order to be more strictly correct it will have to read "De- fence and Defiance." We have been accustomed to rely upon the Volunteer corps as an arm of our military system which would be more or less valuable in case of invasion but rarely have we thought of them as a portion of the British Army 'carrying war into the enemy's country. This has been one of the Lessons of the present war and it will not be lest upon our continental rivals who have been wont to reckon on the smallness of the 'British army.</p>
        <p>There is one other feature in connection with the patriotic action of the volunteers which is also of value. We believe it will be the death blow to that retrograde proposal of compulsory service. So long as this country is blessed with men, from the highest to the lowest in the land, imbued with a spirit and love of country such as we have seen during the last few 'weeks we need not fear con- scription under which the fit and unfit would be pressed into the service. Such a demonstration of loyalty as we have recently seen on the part of the Volunteers ought to, and probably will,cause more attention to be paid to Our Volunteer forces, and drive home the necessity of incres.Ring their effici- ency. There are many grievances in connection with the Yeomanry which need redress, and the saDae repaark applies to the Volunteer battalions.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-01-26_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <p>The long-felt need of shootin4 ranges must be met without delay. It is a regrettable if not disgrace- ful faet that our Warrington Volunteers have been obliged to travel as far as Altcar in order to get that shooting practice upon which their value as a fighting force so much depends.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-01-26_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <head>RUNCORN NOTES</head>
        <p>RUNCORN NOTES</p>
        <p>[By A CORRESPONDENT. The state of a large ,:ection of the parapet, and the crossing below the Primitive Nlethodist Chapel, is becoming the subject of public remark. In wet weather, this section of Greenway-road is best des- cribed as an abaminable mud-puddle; and besides being an impediment is undoubtedly a source of UllitiallCO and annoyance to the public u3in4 the road. Surely something of a temporary character can be done, even if the road cannot be perman- ently wade fit.</p>
        <p>Next Wednesday we are treating our old folk and the aged ones will assemble in our Public Hall to be regaled with the once a year treat. It is a praiseworthy spirit that arltua;:es a community of any kind to such generosity as affording" Old people's treats." They are productive of good. How often has not the practice resulted in some poor awed couple being brought before our notice with the result that a lasting intereA has been taken in them by the philanthropic. Of course there is a reverse side to this picture, bat let us hope it does not apply to Runcorn.</p>
        <p>The Conservatives of the town are proceeding with the project of erecting a Constitutional Club. The committee in a circular they have issued state they have offered to them on a lease for 99 years, at a very reasonable cuief rent a valuable site at the junction of Greenway-road with Lowlands-road. This is on laud belonging to the United Alkali Com- pany. Plans have also been prepared, and are now open for inspection. The eomraittee propo,:e to float the Club by means of a Limited Liability Com- pany, issuing £1 shares which can be paid for by easy and small instalments during the progress of the building. In my goings to and fro I gather that the idea of the company is not very favourably en- tertained, and the " rush" for shares has been conspicious by its absence. There are some Tories in Runcorn, of course, and also a few Liberal Unionists whom it is intendel to catch by the title ef "Constitutional," but I have heard of one Unionist, using a big big "D,"saying that never a cent of his should be invested in the Club. No , doubt, the billiard-room, card-room, and the re- freshment bar are depended upon to make the Club pay its way. I wonder though how much dividend Widnes Conservative Club with those adjuncts has , returned to its shareholders? A not very capacious pocket would hold the lot. And Widnes is a stronger centre of Conservatism than Runcorn.</p>
        <p>The movement which is now taking up our im- mediate attention is the endeavour to get "our Volunteers "—(dressed in Khaki this time)---down amongst us for a farewell dinner. It is said they will not be required to leave the country for nearly a fortnight, and in the meantime,their officers could easily spare them half a day for such a good pur- pose, though they ran the risk of their being for ever afterwards dis-satisiied with barrack-rations. I do hope the movement will succeed, and feel sure anyone wishing to help could not do better than call at the Drill llall, with" his card and silver."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-01-26_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
        <head>FOOTBALL.</head>
        <p>FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>I am more than gratified because the unexpected happened last Saturday. There are plenty of people who are good prophets after the event, but it is a few honest critics that give honest expression of their opinions without causing umbrage.</p>
        <p>Runcorn'edefeat of Swinton was no half one, and from beginning to finish "redtails" were to the fore. Indeed at times their performance astonished the Swinton spectators, as much as it gratified the few Runcorn spectators who were present.</p>
        <p>Next Saturday Runcorn are going to enter;ain the Warrington team and crowd at Wilderspool and so far promise that Warrington shall n) longer boast ot a moral victory on December 26th.</p>
        <p>The team most likely to represent Runcorn is as follows :—lloucv,hton, back ; J Butterworth. C lit- terworth, T Warder, J Wilkinson, three-quarters ; J Jolley and J Faulkner, halves ; Farmer, Gaytor, Darlington, Lightfoot, Rees, 0 Hughes, Tomlinson, and Taylor, forwards. _ _</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-01-26_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <head>SENSATION AT RUNCORN POLICE COURT.</head>
        <p>SENSATION AT RUNCORN POLICE COURT.</p>
        <p>Prisoner Faints in the Dock.</p>
        <p>Assaulting a Wife With a Poker,</p>
        <p>Nicholas Johnson, 35 Granville-street, was brought up on remand before Mr. J. Headley, at the Runcorn Police Court, on Wednesday morning, and committed to the next Cheshire Assizee, for unlawfully and maliciously wounding his wife Mary Johnson. The evidence taken was as follows .Johnson, said she was the wife of the prisoner, with whom she resided at 35, Granville-street, Runcorn. On the 20th of January, her husband came home shortly before mid- night, when he appeared to be mad with drink. When he came in she was sitting on the fender by the fire. He had a pair of boots with him, and cams running in, and threw the boots against the pantry dour, saving "I will kill you stone dead if I get hung for it." Witness who remained sitting on the fender said "No you won't. What is that for 2" Prisoner then took up the poker from the side of the fire-place and struck her a violent blow on the side of the head, just as she was in the act of getting up. The blow par- tially sturur d her, and caused her to lose a quantity of blood. A minute or two afterwards she tried to push the table against him so that she could escape by the door. She succeeded in getting to the door and screamed for Mrs. Kennerdy,after which she remembered no more. By the Bench: She had no quarrel with her husband, and was always on friendly terms with him.—By prisoner : She was not in the Sloop Inn, Lock-street, at 10 o'clock that night. At this stage considerable sensation was ceused by the prisoner geing into an hysterical fit. He was subee• quently accommodated with a seat in the dock, and the examination of witnesses continued. Mary Ellen Kennerdy, wife of Henry Kennerdy of Dutton'e-eourt, Granville-street, swore she was in the house of a neighbour (Mrs. Ellison), about midnight on the 20th of January. In con- sequence of hearing Mrs. Johnson scream twice she ran out, and saw the prisoner with one arm around Mrs. IJohnson's neck,and in the other hand he was holding the poker (produced). She rescued Mrs. Johnson, and took the poker off prisoner. , Mrs. Johnson, who was bleed- ing copiously, was then taken to Mrs. Ellison's house, where she became unconscious. The 'Meter came shortly afterwards, and later Inspector Hicks to whom she gave the poker (produced). •ne Eva Ellistn,who reside I with her pareete ah 28. Gre- nville-street, corroborated Mrs Karinerdy. adding Mr Johnson was saturated with blood which was running down her face. Constable John McLeod said he was on duty in Bridge- street when he stopped the prisoner by the fountain, as he was walking quickly and appeared to be excited, ri9 asked Johnson where he was goi riz,and he replied "I hit my wife with the poker and I thought I had better come and tell you. It is no use running away as I know yea will soon find me." Witness noticing a quantity of blood on prisoner's coat pointed to it and asked " Where did that come from 2" Prisoner replied : "It must have come from her head." On the way to the Police Station prisoner said "I do not know what made me do it, for she is a good woman, and never takes a glees of beer." Whilst they were in the office Inspector nicks came in with the poker in his hand, and prisoner said " Yes, that is our poker; and that is what I have done it with." On Sunday witness charted prisoner with the offence, to which he replied "Have you seen her ?" Witness said he had not. Prisoner then said "you might let me know how she is 2" Prisoner was under the influence of drink. The coat produced was the one prisoner wore at the time of arrest. Dr. Findlay MeD Algal! surgeon ,pract'sine at Runcorn swore he was called to the prisoner's house a little put midnight. He found the injured woman to be bleeding very much from a wound on the head. On further ex- amination he found the wound to be a large one Pepe- trating down to the bone on the left temple, There must have been considerable force used in producine the wound, which from his point of view was a very :,erious one, and one whioh might have been attergled with serious results, both to herself and prisoner. If the in- strument which proluced it had net been a blunt one,it would have gone through her skull, for in his opinion ' the woman had been prodded with the poker and not struck with the full force, otherwise her skull would have been inevitably smashed .—Prisoner then illustrated 1 how he had struck his wife, which seemed to agree with the doctor's theory. He made no answer to the charge. tHe was committed to the next Cheshire Assizes.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-01-26_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <head>ACCIDENT TO A WIDNES FOOTBEiLLER.</head>
        <p>ACCIDENT TO A WIDNES FOOTBEiLLER.</p>
        <p>Thomas Handley, aged 29, of 27, Midwood-street, one of the Widnes team who played Broughton Rangers, on Saturday, wai taken to the accident hospital on his re- turn to Widnes. In the early portion of the match a kick from H. James wag charged down, and in the m6lee which followed Handler severely irjured his right knee. He retired from the field and took no further part in the game.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-01-26_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
