      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-01-26_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <head>14AANTINER. FRIDAY, JANUARY 26, 1900. RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</head>
        <p>THE 14AANTINER. FRIDAY, JANUARY 26, 1900. RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</p>
        <p>A WORKMAN SCALDED AT WIDNES. Michael Nolan, aged 48, 8, Major Crosa-street,labourer at the Vine Works, was admitted to the Accilent Eios- - on Saturday, suffPring from sellds of the 13ft leg, caused by slipping into the vitriol tank. He was attended to by Dr. Hutchinson. RUNCORN AND THE 20rn CENTURY FUND. Mr. T. Simister, presiding at a meeting of the Buns- wick Sunday School, en Sunday afternoon, impressed upon all the great interest Mvthodism feit in th- move- ment. Addresses were also delivered by Mr F J Norman and Mr J D Jones, and Mr Edward Taylor. The amount promised from the Runcorn circuit is sail to total over £1,250. Before the conclusion of the meeting the "Roll Gall" was sung by Miss Balding.</p>
        <p>THE OLD FOLK'S TREAT. Rancorn's Old Folk will be accorded their annual treat next 'Wednesday, when the Public Hall will be laid out in the accustomed manner with all kinds of " goodies." The colimittee intimate that donations will be thankfully received in furtherance of the good object, but at the same time they also desire that none ot our old folk should be left out, and applications should at, once be made to the secretaries at the Town Hall.</p>
        <p>SUNDAY SCHOOL THEA.T Tho infants classes of the Mersey Mission Sun-lay Schools, and the teachers and workers, to the number of about 50 were entertained to the annual tea on Wednes- day last. The tables wore preside I over by M:s Evans, Mrs Hughes, and Miss Temple. After tea a meeting was held in the church when a few words were addressed to them by Mr W Shaw (missioner) and Mr W Shaw (licensed lay reader). Considerablo regret was felt at the absence of Mrs Mack who was reported very ill. The remainder of the time was spent in parlour games in the schoolroom, and right heartily the young folk enjoyed themselves.</p>
        <p>NANSEN'S POLML EXPEDITION. I lecture on the above subject was given last Thurs- day, to the students at the Victoria-road evening solnel by MiS3 Robb, head mistress, (aids Department). The lecture which proved Ivry ieteresting to the pupils, was illustrated by a large number of lime-light views. The lantern was manipulated by Mr. J. Pritchard, ths ap- pointed laeternist at the evening shoo!. On Friday evening the same slides were shown to a large audience of day scholars and parents, the proceeds being in aid of the scholars' prize fund. Mr. J. F. Mention, head master presided, and the views were de:•cribei by Mr. J. C. Crum. Songs were given by Miss Wood and Mr. J. Brown, and patriotic songs were sang by the schelars. Mr. A. E. Parr presi,l3l at the piano. BRUNSWICK MUTUAL IMPROvEXIENT CLASS.— ANNUAL MEETING. The annual tea and social of the Brunswick Mutest Tenprereatent Ciess was held on Wedeiesday evening, at the Brunswick-street schootroom,under the preAdency of Mr. T. Sitnioter.—The election of officers reaultsd in the re-election of Mr T Sitnister (president)and Mr J D Jones (vice-president). Mr James Walker was elected secre- tary, and an entirely new ladies' cemmittee was appointed. About 42 members sat down to a capital tea, the arrangements for which were carried out by the ladies, assisted by Mr. Percy Unsworth. Then followed a programme of music and games. Songs were given by Miss Carter, and Messrs. George Johnson are Jack Ruttel. The evening wasla most enjoyable one. FUNERAL OF TUE LATE MR. WALTER GIBBONS. The funeral of the late Mr. Walter Gibbons, a prom- inent member of St. Paul's Young Men's Society Class, took place en Saturday afternoon at Runcora Csmeery. The young man succumbed to phthisis a few days ago. He was in his 221 d year, and had been employed as a fitter at Messrs. Timmins' foundry. His remains were borne shoulder high from his residencs in York-street to the cemetery by his late colleagues in the class, and emong others present were the Revs C W L Chri,stien and J A B Malvern, Messrs George Hayes and John Nan. collie (present and past leaders of the class), D and J D Bisbrown (fellow workmen), and representatives of the Oddfellows' Society. The burial service was impres- sively condected by the circuit ministers. Beautiful wreaths were placed on the coffin by the sorrowing family and friends. LOCAL BILLIARD Runcorn Liberal Club sent a representative team to play a billiard match with the members of the Chester Liberal Club last Wednesday. An enjoyable evening was spent, and capital tefreshment provided. The scores were as follows :</p>
        <p>RVNCORN: CHESTER. J Elliott W B Brimelow B Johnson W Lawton R Jones ~100 TG Hyd~ T Carruthers 1-1 Hughes J Smith J 0 Cragg J Bate J Deakin 56 W Lamb 3 Joyes lOO W Aldies -</p>
        <p>1057 1119 Thus it will be seen that Chester won by 62 points, though Runcorn won 7 games to 5. The most exciting was the game between J Smith and H Thomas, the latter being 99 when Smith required 15. TEA AND ENTERTAINMENT AT DUTTON WORICHIIISE. Toe Ladies' Committee gave their annual treat to the inmates on Friday, when the following ladies, Guar- dians, and friends were present :—Miss Dutton (presi- dent), Mrs Milner (hon. secretary), Mrs Whitley, Mrs Armitage, Mrs Poenott, Mrs Boston, Mrs Linaker, Miss Linaker, Miss Whitley, Miss Baxter, Miss Sloan, Mr Williams (chairman of the Board), Mr A Norman (chair- man of the House Committee), Mr Clarke, Mr Milner, Mr Boston, and Mr Turton. The tea to the inmates was on the tables at four o'clock. and consisted of tea, bread and butter, meat pies, currant and seed bread, and buns. The ladies afterwards entertained the Guardians, friends, and officers to an excellent tea in a room which had been prettily decorated for the occasion, after which an adjournment was made to the dining hall for the annual entertain- ment. The chair was occupied by Mr Williams. and the proceedings were opened by some suitable remarks from the Chairman and a carol b 7 the choir, the latter also contributing patriotic songs, accompanied by Mrs. Cuouth (matron), and condi:acted by Mr. Gunnels (mas- ter). The graneaphone, maninulated by Miss Sloan and a magic lantern, under the able direction of Mr. Boston and Mr. Turten, were much appreciated, the scenes from" The Lifeboat" and constentive readings appeal- ing strongly to the pathetic feelings of the audience. Songs were given by Mrs Boston, Miss Linaker and Miss Sloan. and elicited hearty applause. A dialogue by two little "dots" caused much amusement, and a gymnastic display by a juvenile " strong man" gave evidence that the physical culture of the children was being carefully attended to. The programme, having lasted three hour, had to be curtailed, and a vote of thanks to the Ladies' Committee, Chairman, Master and Matron, and other officers was given, after which the singing of the Natioaal Anthem cloyed the proceedinge. BETHESDA CONGREGATIONAL CHURCH. An unusually interesting social gathering was held in Bethesda Church Schoolroom on Tuesday evening. The attendance was exceptionally good, the room which has recently been beautified, being quite full. The chair was taken by Mr. E Marshall who in a few appropriate words expressed his pleasure at being present on such an interesting occasion. The first item on the pro- gramme was a pianoforte solo, admirably eeecuted, by Miss Bishop of Manchester. Mr. W Shaw then sang "Eileen Alannah," and later in the evening" Mona," both soles being very warmly appreciated. Six little girls, tastefully dressed, contributed two pieces, the first, an action song, "John Brown's Knap- sack" which created much amusement, the ! second, a Scarf Dill, which was performed very gracefully. Miss Stone sang with good effect "The Children's Home." Two violin solos" The Shepherd's Danes " and "Morris Dance" were beautifully renderod by Miss Bishop, accompanied at the piano by Miss M Handley. Miss Christian gave a pianoforte solo with splendid effect, and immediately after Miss Woodward ' and Miis Shaw sang together " Gipsy Countess," an old favourite, and well rendered. The programme was varied by a cornet solo of a very interesting character by Mr T Knight. "The Holy City " was next sung by Mr Koiveton who was in tine form. A very attractive feature -if the evening was Mr. J. Birkett's humorous songs •• Tin Gee Gee" and " Dallp's Mamma," together with a number of amusing stories which were received with much appreciative laughter. Those two songs were accompanied by Mr H W Handley. A vary attractive item was a song by Master J J Lunt. entitled " Never forget the dear ones" which he sang in a beautifully clear treble voice. There was an interval of half-an-hour between the two parts of the programme during which refreshments were served out to those present. During the evening the Rev C W L Christian delivered an address. In his opening remarks Mr Christian said " I congratulate Bethesda Church in having as its Pastor so scholarly and cultured a gentleman as Mr Osborne. It is not every church that can claim as its minister a graduate of two English Universities and an Honours man in the bargain. Moreover Mr. Osborne is a devoted servant of Jesus Christ and his heart and soul are in the work of making his church a power for good and there is no fear that this will he accomplished if you all stand round him 'firmly and sympathetically." Mr. Chrietien dwelt at some length on the point that a strong church must be a praying church and a united church. At the conclusion of the entertainment, the Pastor, Rev J Osborne, B.A.,in proposing a vote of thanks to all who had by their abilities contributed to the enjoyment of the gatherine, said that the encouraging financial Success of the evening was due to the cheerful and self-denying efforts of Mrs. John Coy, Mrs. William Powell and Mr. John Bata, and that the ready and generous way in which the members of the church and congregation has given money and pro- visions was a splendid token of their confidence in the ministry, and their desire that the church should pros- per. Mr. Osborne announced that the present gathering was one of a series that would be held monthly during the winter. Mr. John James Bowyer seconded the vote of thanks and it was carried with hearty applause.</p>
        <p>SHOCKING ACCIDENT TO A RUNCORN MAN.</p>
        <p>John —Walitcr, aged nineteen, of 33. Blantyre-street, Runcorn, was admitted to tAe St. Helens Hospital on Monday afternoon, suffering from serious injuries. It appears that Walker was employed by a firin who are at present decorating the County Asylum, It4inhill, and whilst at work on a scaffAd in the large dining-room fell to the floor, a distance ef 40 feet, with the result that his head was severely injured and both arras broken. The poor tellow was at once attended to by the Asylum doctor, who afterwards ordered him to the Si. Helens Hospital where Dr. Jackson was called in.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-01-26_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <head>RUNCORN LIBERAL CLUB.</head>
        <p>THE RUNCORN LIBERAL CLUB.</p>
        <p>Mr. W. B. Briaielo a' presided at a fairly attended meeting of the members of the Liberal Club,at the Tem- porary Buildings, Forrester's Hall, Runcorn, on Tuesday evening last, when the following nominations were re- calved :—Presldent, Sir John Brunner,Barr., M.P. ; vice- presidents, Me-gra S Taylor, J.P., A R Norman, J.P., R H Posnett, W Ockleston ; treasurer, Mr Richard Moore; secretary, Mr E Riley, auditors (two required), Messrs H Chadwick, C Kniveton. E R Pearson ; committee, Messrs John Bate, W B Brimelow, John Clarke, John Elliott, I hornas Gerrard, Jost.lon Handley,, James Jolley, a Rawnscroft, T Carruthers, W A Millinger, J Rawlinson. Ei Chadwick, E R Parson, E Johnson, James Lea, W Riley, Gorge R Smith, Thomas Wood, Peter Corns, T Yatea, James Green, Alex. Green, C Knivetonii Worrall, I Williams.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-01-26_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
        <head>MERSEY MISSION TO SEAMEN,</head>
        <p>MERSEY MISSION TO SEAMEN,</p>
        <p>Tuesday's weekly concert at the above institute was provided oy the members of the Runcorn Parish Church Band of Hope. Owing to the unavoidable absence of the Rev. W. Scholes (through illness) the duties of Chair- man wer, discharged 'uy the genial Missioner, Mr W Shaw. Mr W Howard opened with " S)ldiers of the Queen" and naturally was encored. A number of girls under the instrultion of Miss Carr received a meritorious applause for their action songs " Ambulance Maids" and ‘• Oh ! Fancy." Miss Easily Clare's recitation "Turning the tables " excited admiration, and considerable amuse- ment:was afforded by Mr W Howard. "Mary had a little lamb." "Joan and Darby,' by Messrs E Capper and W Howard, and the descriptive song " Scarlet and Blue by Messrs El Gibbs and A Hunt, proved two interesting duets. "The ride of Jenny Mactical" was another capital recitation by Miss E Miles. Tile boys contributed the "Chinese Lantern " acioo song, and were loudly applauded. A pianoforte solo and mandoline solo were given by Miss Bate and Mr G Treadwell. Miss G Page was well received for the recitation " A curious com- plaint," and Mr H G Griffiths for his singing of " The An.lhor's Weighed." 'Flee efficient accompanist was Miss Bate. The usual votes of thanks being heartily accorded, the audience rose ea masse and sang " God save the Queen. NATIONAL UNION OT TEACHERS' ORPRIANAGE AND BENEVOLENT FUND. _ _</p>
        <p>The annual meeting of the Runcorn and Widnes Lecal Board of subscribers to these Funds was held in the Vic- toria-road Boys' School, Runcorn, on Saturday evening. Tee chair was occupied by Mr. T. Phillips, 13 resident of the association, and there was a represestative attend- ance of members. Mr. J. F. Moulton. the secretary, presented the annual report, which stated that the con- tributions from the local association for the year ended December 31st, 1899, amounted to £36 16s, made up of the fellewirw, subscriptions :—Sir J T Brunner, Bart., M.P., £5; Messrs H Sutton Timinis, £5 ; J S Gilliatt. M.P., £1 ; H Wade Deacon, £l. is; Alderman J W ‘Vareing, £1 is; F Niel, ss; T Dodd, 5s ; D Lewis, 53 ; teachers' subscriptions, £22 183 ; The above sures were allocated as follows :—Orphan fund, £l5 Os 61 ; benevolent, £2l 15s 61. The total raised by the heal association since its formation now amounted to £239. The report further stated that whi/st it was very gratifying that the local association con- tinued to do so well for these deserving charities, yet it was a matter for regret that out of a membership of 81 only about 30 subscribed 53 or upwards. The committee hoped that the appeal this year would meet with a cordial and generous response from all the menVe,rs. Toe report and balance sheet were adopted, and t.: 3 follow- ing officers were elected to manage the funds during the coming year :—Chairman, Mr J Brown (president of toe association), secretary. Mr J F Moulton ; committee, Misses E J Clayden, A. Naylor, and 0 Stubs ; Messrs. G S Smith and T Pnitlips. Votes of thanks to the workers and those subscribers outside the profession who had so generously assisted brought the meeting to a close.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-01-26_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
        <head>ALLEGED EMBEZZLEMENT AT RUNCORN.</head>
        <p>ALLEGED EMBEZZLEMENT AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>County Court Clerk in the Dock. At Runcorn Police Court on Saturday, before Mr. F. J. Norman, George Pear inn, clerk of Runcorn County Court, was charged with havin.: on the 6:h December embezzled £7 103. 21. trout his edaployer, Mr. William Davies Jolliffe.—Mr. F. A. Lake appeared to prosecute, and said he only proposed to offer evidence sufficient to warrant a remand.—Alfred Torrie, bailiff at Runcorn County Court, said on the 611 December he paid to the prison-r £7 103 21 received on a warrant of execution. —Mr. W. D. Jolliffe said he was registrar and high bailiff of Runcorn °aunty Court. Prisoner had been in his employ as clerk for about four years. It was part of his duty to receive this money from Tornio, and to enter it into the Foreign Executions Commitment Cash Beck. The prisoner (lid not so enter the money nor had he accounted for it in any way.—The prisoner was remanded until to-day Friday.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-01-26_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <head>HOCKEY.</head>
        <p>HOCKEY.</p>
        <p>Latchford v. Broad Green 2nd.</p>
        <p>These clubs met at Broad Green on Saturday to play their only engagement of the season. Owing to the recent heavy rain the ground was not in the best of order, and so fast forward play was severely handicapped. From the start the game was fairly even, the ball travelling from one end to the other. After some give and take play Grounds managed to rt the ball in the circles and notched a good goal for the visitors, Shortly after Albinson scored a second. After this Broad Green rallied, and had extremely hard lines in not scoring, as they had a very easy chance. During the second half the home team tried hard to scare, but could not ma,nage ta break the visitors' de fence. After a run up the field Knight centred and Grounds scored a third goal. Latchford half backs played a splendid game.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-01-26_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <head>DRUNK ON LICENSED PREMISES AT NEWTON-</head>
        <p>DRUNK ON LICENSED PREMISES AT NEWTON-</p>
        <p>Landlord's Wife Assaulted. At Newton Petty Sessions on Thursday, ElWard Jones was charged with being drunk on the licensed promisee of Richard Morris, Newton, on the 20th January, and with refusing to quit when requested to do ss. He Was also charged with assaulting Mary Morris at the same time and place. He pleaded not guilty to each charge. —Mr. A. Browne, Warringtoe, prosecuted, and the defendant was represented by Mr. J. W. Knowles.--Mr. Browne, in opening the case said that the complainant was the licensee of the " Sunbeam "Inn, Wargrave, and had occupied the house for twenty two years. The defendant had not been in the house for five years till last Saturday night when he came in drunk. He asked for two glasses of beer, one for himself and one for a friend, but Mr. Morris said " Not to night, you are not in a fit state" and advised him to go home. Mr. Morris then walked to the bar and defendaEt followed him and again asked to be served. He was refused, and he then commenced to make use of disgust- ing language. Mr. Morris told him that if he did not stop using that language he would nut him out. Defend. ant would not stop and struck at Mr. Morris, who war- ded off the blow. Mr. Morris then proceeded to eject him, and whilst he was putting him out defendant kicked him three times in the stomach. A young teen came te the landlord's assistance, and defendant was ejected. Re, however, returned a few minutes after- wards. Mrs. Morris tad him he must go met, and he kicked her tare s times on the leg. With the assistance of two men, defendant was again put out,but he waited down the road a considerable time and thrashed one of the witnesses. The case was a bad one, and he (Mr. Browne) thought the Bench would agree with him nst. if a licensee did his best to prevent unnecessary drunkenness he should have their pretection. Richard Morris. the complainant, gave evidence in support of this statement. Cross-examined by Mr. Knowles he denied that he told the defendant that a customer who came once in five years was not worth serving. Mrs Morris, and two men named John Sumners and Thomas Unsworth corroborated. Mr Knowles for the defence said that in their evidence neither Mr Morris or his wife ever said they requested the d.fendant to leave the premises. His defence was iret that the defendant was not drunk. No evidence had been given to show that he was staggering about the room or acting like a drunken man. His eecond con- tention was that the defendant was not not asked to leave the premises. Mr. Browne said Mr. Morris had said that he told him to go out. Mr. Knowles : If that were so. the question is was the man drunk. Continuing Mr. Knowles said that the injury to Mil. Morris was caused in the general scuffle and defendant did not kick her deliberately. Defendant gave evidence and said he was not drunk. Mrs. Morris gave him two black eyes. lie was not ordered to leave the premises. H 9 had nine glaseee of beer during the whole day. Arthur Critchley said he was in the peblic house at the same time as the defendant. lie saw Mrs. Morris get hold of Jones, who was grumbling about the land- lord not serving him. Defendant was not " making much bother." He was sat quietly on a seat when Morris came to him. - - -</p>
        <p>_ By Mr. Browne : Defendant made uso of bid language He did not deliberately kick Mrs. Morris. His foot "swung round" and caught her on the shin. The magistrates retired to consider their decision and on their return the Chairman said they had carefully considered the case and were perfectly satisfied that the defendant was drunk and committed ths assault. Ho would foe fined 53 and costs for each offence.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-01-26_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <head>RUNCORN AND DISTRICT TEACHERS' ASSOCIATION.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN AND DISTRICT TEACHERS' ASSOCIATION.</p>
        <p>Annual Meeting.</p>
        <p>The above association hid its annual meeting on Saturday last in the Vietoria-road Board School, Run- corn. The chair was occupied by Mr. T. Philips (presi- d'ent), and in addition there were also present : G S Smith (hon. secretary). J F Moultan,J Brown, G F Parr, Calland, and a few lady members.</p>
        <p>RESIGNATION OF TIM TRELSURER.</p>
        <p>A letter was read from Misi Kilburn thanking the association for giving her a position of trust for so many years, hut she wished t 3 be excused from holding soy office this year. She also intimated that she would not be able to attend the conference this year. The Treasurer's report showed an income of £44 95 id, and after meeting all expenses there was a balance in hand of £5 14s Id.</p>
        <p>Tito report was adopted, on the motion of Mr. Brown, seconded by Mr. Parr. At a later period of the meeting it was decided to ac- cord Miss Kilbutn a hearty vote of thanks, and put her valued services on record in the form of an illuminated address.</p>
        <p>RESUME OF TITIC YEAR'S WORK.</p>
        <p>Mr. G. S. Smith, the hon. secretary, presented the folio wing report :—Your committee in presenting to you their report on the year's work have to state that the year commenced with a membership of 81. There have been eight withdrawals in consequeace of removals, but these have been more than counter-balanced by the admission of eleven new members. The association now numbers 84. It is with great regret that the committee report the death of Mr. Herbert Keighley, who is as a member and represented our association at the Confer- ence of the Voluntary Teachers held at Birmingham. During the past year the following meetings have been oeld :—Taree quarterly, one special general, seven com- mittee, making a total of eleven meetings. In the notice of the annual meeting will be found a tabulated list of Attendances made by each member of the committee. No papers have been read at any of the meetings, but it is hoped that during the coming year the association will make itself of more practical use by meeting occasionally for the discussion of some of the educational problems of the day. The committee regret that some of the meetings have been very badly attended by certain members of the association—notably at the quarterly meeting at Runcorn on November 18th, when only seven members were present, five being from Runcorn. This year had been a very successful one in connection with the N.U.T. TOO Superannuation Act, passed in 1898, came in force on the let January, 1899, and has been accepted by a large number of teachers. The Executive however, regret that there are some 10,000 certified schoolmistreases in the country who so far have not accepted the Act. In the case of many ladies the omission to accept arose from a misunderstanding. Many of them supposed that they must remain in school until 65. They may if in a position to do se, leave school some years before that date without forfeiting their right to a pension i at 65. Ia order that it may not be too late for these members the Executive have now obtained permission for late acceptances of the Superannuation Act to take place. The date is now extended to the 31st March next, after which the door will be absolutely closed to teachers certificated before 30th A pri1,1899. In connection with the Cheiehirellietrict a public meeting was held in Runcorn on September 23rd. In conclusion the Com- mittee tender the heartiest thanks of the association to the managers of those schools in which meetings have been held during the parse year; also to those teachers who have helped to make the meetings a success. The Chairman said the experience of their secretary was only that of his prodeeessors with regard to the attendance at the meetings of the association. One gentleman. had he been present that evening, intended to propose that all the meetings of the association should be held at the West Bank Board Schools, Widnes. The teachers resident in the Widnes district had an enor- mous hill to clienb—(laughter)—after they had crossed the bridge, and besides that many of them had to come two or three miles out of Widnes. Thera were four pre- sent that evening who had to travel three or four miles te get home. Mr. Parr moved the adoption of the report.</p>
        <p>• Mr. Moulton seconded. They did hoe in the coming year there would be a better attendance. He reminded the meeting that the place of naeeting was fixed by one of the standing rules of the association, and that rule could not be altered except by giving notice at that annual meeting. He did not think it would be any remedy to hold two meetings at the West Bank Board School. He did not wish to unduly blame the Widnes teachers—the Runcorn teachers were quite as bad—but he would point out that the Widnes teachers had not attended when the meeting had been held at West Bank. He hoped no such change as had been hinted at would be adopted. The present arrangement, in his opinion, was fair and equitable. He had much pleasure in complimenting Mr. Smith on the report he had submitted.</p>
        <p>The report was adopted. REPORT OF TUE PARLIAMENTARY' SECRETARY</p>
        <p>Mr. J..y. Moulton, the Parliamentary Secretary, re- ported that, as requested, ha wrote to the members for the Widnes and Northwich Parliamentary Divisions in- viting their support in favour of the Half Time Bill. He received favourable replies from Mr. Caidist and Sir John Brunner, Bart. The former said that he would give the matter his careful consideration, and stated that he was in favour of the measure so far as he knew at the time. Sir John Brunner replied that he was heartily in accord with the provisionsef the measure. He (Mr Moulton) also distributed a number of pamphlets on "child tabour," and a petition was drawn up and ex- tensively signed. It was sent to Sir John Brunner And laid before the House of Commons. So he thought the association did its share in assisting in the passing of the Half Time Bill.</p>
        <p>The report was adopted. The CHESHIRE Dtsriucr uptiort. _</p>
        <p>Mr. Moulton reported on the proceedings of the Coun- cil of the Cheshire District Union, observing that the preliminary discusiion of the resolutions of the confer- ence by the Council had been very helpful to the various represe:ltatives in deciding: what altion they shguld take Upon them. They had had the advantage during the year of the public meeting of the Cheshire District Union at Runcorn, which, he thought, would result in lasting good. Mr. Smith said he had derived pleasure and instruction from the meeting. Those who had the privilege of at- tending the meetings would see the great amount of work the Council did. THE GULF BETWEEN THE HEAD TEACHES AND ASSISTANTS.</p>
        <p>The Chairman, in vacating the chair, said he was pleased to observe that during his year of office the mem- bership bad gone up, as it had done several years pre- viously. He hoped that in the cornicg year it would grew stronger and much more active. He regretted the vast gulf which t%ore was between the head teachers and the poor unfortunate assistants. It was considered an anomaly in many associatinns for an assistant teacher to occupy the presidential chair,but he was proud to have occupied it in that capacity, and he</p>
        <p>hoped the position had not suffered at his hands. He hoped the ensuing year would be a prosper4us one, and • that as an association they would gather strength and i usefulness. (Applause.) .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-01-26_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>TRH ELECTION- OF OFFICERS, -PRESIDENTS' ADDRESS.</head>
        <p>TRH ELECTION- OF OFFICERS, -PRESIDENTS' ADDRESS.</p>
        <p>Mr. J. Brown having been elected president tor the ensuing year, on taking the chair thanked the members for the honour. In the course of his address he said a great and goad work was dons by the B.nevolent and Orphanage Fund of the Teachers. But to him it was a matter of great regret that this splendid fund was not in direct legal connection with the Nationa! Union of Teachers. The Executive as representing the N.U.T. had really nothing to ao with this fund, and they found that large numbers of members of the N.U.T. did abso- lutely nothing to help en or stipport this most noble work. As many as 85 assoc'ations in the country did nothing whatever to help on the Fund. He should like to see the two, that is, the N.U.T. and</p>
        <p>the Benevolent and Orphan Fund directly and legally contacted. with an increased subscription which would cover all the work and be commensurate with the great amount of work that lay before them. He was pleased that the Runcorn and Widnes Association did take an active interest in the Benevolent and Orphan work. In- deed considering the size of the association they sup- ported it admirably. Mr. Brown then went on to say that the Size of the classes under the charge of individual teachers is a great hinderance to the work in the elementary schools of the country. How could one man or woman deal adequately with a class of from 50 to 60 and in some cases men more children ! Can he or she educate them ? That the teacher would get them on there was no doubt but to educate in the true sense of the word was impos4ble. This kind of ' thing was unfair to the children as well as to the teacher, for the sharper children were to a certain extent kept marking time whilst the slower ones were pulling no. Then again there were some who were very dull. What happened to these ? Had the teacher with a large class to superintend, time to give them individual help. No ! These poor slow enes got left hepstessly behind with the result that they had to plod on for a couple of years in one s'endard. He sincerely hoped that before long there would be a drastic change in this matter. He contended that 30 children, particularly in the upper easses, was a sufficient number for any one teacher te have under his charge day after day, that is if he was .to be allowed tap opportunity ot finding out and fester- ing the special capabilities of his pupils. (Hear, hear and applause). The election of the other officers resulted as follows: Vice-president, Mr R Devonaki ; treasurer, Miss Nevlor ; secretary, Mr G S Smith ; assistent seeretery, Mr R Glover ; parliamentary secretary, Mr J F Moulton ; press cerrespondent, Mr J Brown ; committee, Misses Brassil, Kirkham, Clayden, Williams and Stubbs and Mr B Parr ; representatives to conference, Messrs J Brown, T Phillips, G S Smith, J F Nloulton. and G F Parr, and Misses Clayden, Stubbs and Naylor; repre- sentatives to Cheshire District Union, Messrs G S Smith and J Brown. Mr. Moulton is also a representative on the Council by virtue of his office as president of the Cheehire District Union.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-01-26_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <p>WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>Warrington Technical institute.—Holiday Lectures.</p>
        <p>At the conclusion of tme lectures on " Beat and steam: how they make the locomotive move" delivered in the Christmas holidays by Mr. J R Ash- worth, 'ALSO., the lecturer invited the juvenile mem- bers at his audience to write an essay describing as much as they could remember of the lectures, and offered a prize for the best paper sent in within ten days. A number of excellent papers were received, and after careful consideration the following "class list" has been arrived at :—Prize, E Gordon Bur- gess ( Viliamarleigh-street) proxims accesit, Greene. Honourable mention is accorded to F Monks, Dorothy Monks, and F Broadhurst. The winner having been consulted, the prize takes the form of a model locomotive.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-01-26_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>Miss _Spinks' Annual Ball.</head>
        <p>Miss _Spinks' Annual Ball.</p>
        <p>The annual ball arranged by Miss Ethel Spinks and attended by her pupils and friends was held in Parr Hall on Friday evening last week, over one hundred couples being present. Everything passed off very successfully and pleasantly, a programme of 20 dances being agreeably inter- spersed with other items at intervals. The Misses Wright. Ewart, Cromptoa, Parson- age, Chantry, and Johnstone d.taced charm- ingly in a " fairy " dance designed by their teacher, Miss Spinks. Miss A Harrison and MissC ton " did "the cake-walk very engagingly later in the evening, while a couple of humorous recitals. by Mr EI M Ashton were also heartily appreciated. The proceedings lasted till half past two, and were enjoyable throughout,Mr W Owen acting efficiently as M.C. Mr. Ralph Johnson had decorated the Hall, and the catering was in the hands of Messrs. Chantry and Son. Excellent music was supplied by a band under the direction of Mr. W. linidrick. Oddfellows (Warrington District).</p>
        <p>A number of the members of the Warrington dis- trict of the N. 1.0. Odd tellows paid a visit to Earles- town on Saturday to participate in celebrating the anniversary of the Prosperity Lodge, belonging to the district. The weather was favouratile, and allowed the new banner of the district to be dis- played. Arriving at the Othlfellowe' Arms Hotel at 4.30 about fifty members sat down to a subs:-,antial dinner. Afterwards a social was held, the chair being taken by the Grand Slaster of till district, Bro J Brown, who was supporterl by D.G.M. E E Pester, T Dickinson, ; J Smith, and J Bramhall. Th., usual toasts were given during the evening, and were well responded to. P.G. 3 Hughes spoke at some length in reference to the lodge being comparatively at a standstill, and hoped that each member would do his utmost to further the interests of their lodge. Addresses were also given by P.P.G.11. J Smith and P.G. Kennedy. The following contributed songs ;—Bros. Smith, Duncan, Johnson, Brown, Naylor, Bothwell, Hughes, Dickinson, and Pester. A hearty vote of thanks was accorded to the host and hostess for their catering. The singing of the National Anthem brought the meeting to a close, and the party reached Warrington about eleven o'clock, after an excellent outing.</p>
        <p>Wrestling:</p>
        <p>Under the auspices of the -Widnes Subscription Band an athletic entertainment of wrestling was held in the Drill Hall, Widnes, on Monday. There was a fair audience and Mr. Sullivan, of Liverpool, was appointed referee. The first exhibition was between Ted Reece, champion of Liverpool, and Bob Faulkner, of Tyldesley. in the Greeco-Roman style. The first bout was to TJkl itteCt3, and lasted six minutes. The second continued for 14 minutes, and was to Faulkner. In the anal Ted Reece put down Faulkner after a five minutes' sharp struggle. The second event was in the "catch- as-catch-can" style, between James Collins, of America, and Parker, of Liverpool. Both rounds were to Parker, the first in 13 minutes, and the second in eight. A display of fencing was then given by Prof. B W Hartley and P McArthur, of Liverpool. The final item was a wrestling handi- cap between Tom Cannon, the world's champion wrestler. and Fair Turner, of Warrington, in 15 minutes wrestling time, Grfe3o-Rorean style. Mr. Preece refereed. The conditions were, if Turner stood the champion off for the allotted time he would he presented with a purse of £4O. At the commencement Turner gripped his opponent from the back, but Cannon got round him before half- time. Cannon exerted his utmost, but failed to I throw Turner, who was acclaimed the winner I amidst loud cheers, "The Ancient Mariner" at the Parr Hall, On Saturday evening, the members of the War- rington Band of Hope Union Choir, assisted by several other artistes, gave a concert, the proceeds of which were in aid of the bazaar fund,in the Parr Hall. There was a fairly large attendance. The first portion of the programme consisted of T. Mee Patticon's popular cantata " The Ancient Mariner." The choruses were admirably rendered by the choir Mr. W. Brough wielding the baton and Mr. E. Wilkes presiding at the organ. The solos were taken by Miss Kathleen Howell (soprano), Miss Hannah Brough (contralto) Mr Levi Richardson (tenor) and Mr Arthur Weber (bas-), who did ample ljustice to the parts allotted to them. The second portion of the programme was of a miscel- laneous character. It was opened with a cleverly executed violin solo " Sarabande," by Miss B. Boachier, who later on gave I. Ritornell " and" Perpetuo " (Bohm) with good effect. Miss Kathleen Howell sang in a manner which gained the hearty applause of the audience. Mr. Arthur Weber has been heard in Warrington many times previously, and on Saturday his reputation was en- chanced. His powerful bass voice was heard to great advantage in the popular song "The boys of the old brigade" for which he was awarded a hearty encore. He sang in response " The diver." The humorous element was in the hands of Mr. C. Clifford Walker who made his debut before a War- rington audience. His efforts to amuse were very successful and for several of his contributions he was encored. Mr. E. Wilkes accompanied with his usual skill. The proceedings were broaght to a close with the singing ot the National Anthem.</p>
        <p>Presentation to Mr. John Griffiths.</p>
        <p>On Saturday evening, at the Cheshire Cheese Inn, Latchford, another presentation was made to Mr,l John Griffiths, who a few weeks ago severed his connection as one of the managers of Beweey Forge to take charge of the above well-known inn. This time the presentation was made by the forge de- partment, and it took the form of a handsome timepiece, with ornaments ;figures of horses) to 1 match.to Mr Griffiths,and a satchel to Mrs Griffiths., A brass plate on the timepiece bore the following inscription :—"Presented to John R Griffiths, as a token of respect on his retirement, by the Puddling Department of Bewsey Ironworks, Warrington, ' 20th January, 1900." A substantial knife-and-fork tea was provided at six o'clock, and thoroughly enjoyed, after which Mr T B Malkin took the chair, and Mr Vir Clayton the viee-chair. Mr Malkin then expressed the pleasure it afforded him to ba present, and alluded to the fact that he had worked with and had known Mr Griffiths for 30 years, and he had never met a more competent man in his particular trade. He bad had a wonderful experience. Of course the position of a manager was a very responsible one, as he had, as it were, to be between two fires. The first call upon him was the interests of his employers, and he had also to use tact with regard to the men. He had often sympathised with him, although he admitted that he had generally exercised great judgment in his work, and he wished him every success in his new sphere of labour. The presentations were then made amid applause. Mr. Griffiths, , who was overcome with emotion, re- plied,and expressed the pleasure it afforded him to know that he was apparently so mach respected by the men. He should treasure the presents as long as he lived. He had been cenneczed with the works nearly 30 years, and it was, as they could well imagine, a great sorrow to him to have to leave them. He hoped work at the forges would go on as Ismoothly as ever, and that they would all take his advice, which was " be reenter and attentive to your work." (Applame.) There were openings at the works for every man who would give his mind to what he had to do. (Applause.)—Mre. Griffiths briefly responded, and a pleesant and varied pro- gramme followed—The committee reeponsible for the arrangements for the preeentation were Messrs Atwell (chairman), T B Malkin (secretary), Thomas Cox (trea,surer),W Thompson, Frank Worrall,Martin Gallagher, J Leigh, Henry Bishop, Samuel Davies, Stephens, and William Atwell, sen.</p>
        <p>' LINRII3D COMPOUND Trade Mark of Comp und Easenee oi Lfnseed, for Conichs an Colis.—Advt</p>
        <p>PUBLIC 13t:NEIT UNTIYER THE CLOCK FOR BIliT BOOTS IN LANOASIAIRE.—A.cIet. LAMPS. Oil "Stoves, Colce Stove=, J. S. GARLA.ND, 5L 53, and 55, Bricige-stNet, Warrinztaa.— Advt. USEFUL PRESENTS of every clegcription, See Win- dow.—J. S. GARLAND Irorrnonirr, Widge-st.—Advt•</p>
