      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-01-26_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <p>OUR</p>
        <p>FOOTBALL COMPETITION</p>
        <p>(COUPON ON PAGE 7.) £5O Offered Weekly—Rloo Aggregate Prize.</p>
        <p>RESULT OF LAST WEEK'S COMPETITION.</p>
        <p>ONE COMPETITOR WINS £5O</p>
        <p>The matches selected for our 21st football compotition fin.; !shed as follows: Wolverhampton Wanderers beat Glossop N. K, Aston Villa boat Blackburn R, Derby County beat Newcastle United, Notts County drew with Manchester City, Linooln City beat Bolton W. Chesterfield beat New Brightont Tottenham El beat Reading, Salford beat Warrington. Wake- field beat Manningham, Gloucester l•eat Llanelly. The fol- lowing competitor gave 10 correct results, and therefore wins the £5O prize -- 11ir 0 Standish, 137, Hih-,.treet, Ilrrac...lrnty, Dev,n. ME CONSOLATION PRIZE.</p>
        <p>The following competitors made one mistake, and therefore share the £lO prize :-0 3 Gunning. Newbury; If A Truscott, Helston ; 0 Lapthorn, Plymouth' G Nightingale. Croydon; H Allen, Wellingborough; F Sawbridge. Islington ; Mackenzie, Nottingham; E Marks, Eastbourne; A R Payne-Smith, Langton; M Collings, Leicester ; S Brown. Laucaster' G Manning, Dunstable; B Maple, Bed.. ford; W Rice, Kettering ; A P Allen. Loughborough; T Adnitt, Edinburgh; C Newton. Comae ; W Ivy, Wakefield; J Ward. Stamford ; D S. Stevens. Ipswich ; Miss Dale, Liverpool ; R Thomas, Enfold; P Bonner, Finedon ; H Con. mote. Bedford; P A Stewart, Manchester; W If Bailey, Cardiff; G Blaekmore. Honiton ; C Whitlock, A.vortraouth ; W Amott, Lichfield; B S Dobson, Handsworth ; S Walters, Henley.</p>
        <p>THE COUPON. PRIZE. Mr B Maple, Oakland, Bushmead Avenue, Bedford, wins the £3 coupon prize with 6550 coupons.</p>
        <p>THE =HECATE PRIZE</p>
        <p>Mr. Maple has scored 24 points for tho aggregate prize Mr Proctor 23 ;Mr Arthur 21; Mr Mann 15; Mr Pridgeon 13 ; dr Pittam (H.R.) 12 ; Messrs Mason, • Pittsni (M ), and Hutchings 11 each; Messrs Wood and Chaplin 9 each ; Messrs Cook and Rodgers 8 erch ; Messrs Astles, Oritehley, Fuanell and Twining 7 each; Messrs Miller, Salt and Owen 6 each; Messrs Bowley, Fletcher, Harris, Price, Stanley, Tugwell, Dean, Ambridge, Lawrence, Nelson, Marshall, Taylor, Tiflre, Brook, Standish and Spencer 5 each.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-01-26_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>WORKINGMEN'S MISSION, BANK QUAY.</head>
        <p>WORKINGMEN'S MISSION, BANK QUAY.</p>
        <p>Annual Tea Meeting and Concert.</p>
        <p>Speech by the Mayor.</p>
        <p>In the new schools, Thewlis-street, on Monday evening the annual coneregatonel tea meeting and ceecert connection with the Workingmen's Mission Chapel, Sank Quay, was held. Tea was perlaken of at FOC o'cleck and the tables were presided over by the following ladies : Mesdames T Hassell, T Taylor, Naden, Joseph Monks, R C Tanner, G Hassell, Burndrod, T Dale, S Hill, .1 Taylot Henry Edwards, J Monks and Miss L Cowap. After the removal of the cloth, the subsequent pro-, ceedings were cothmenced with the singing of the National Anthem. The Mayer (Alderman Roberts) pre. sided and he was supported ou the platform by the chief workers at the mission. The Secretary (Mr. Edwards), read a very oncsuraging report on the pass year's work. The MAYOR made a few remarks in the course of which he said he bad great pleasure in wishing all pre- sent (if it were not too late) a very happy and pros- perous New Year. He woe pleased to be present that night to bear th:ir anntral report read hut regretted very much to hear of the losses they had sustained through death during the year. .Two of the names men- tioned by: the Secretery in hie report (viz.. Mr. Pewees and Mr. Ball) were very well known to him' and in fact were particular friends of his, and therefore, • they would understand that the news of their death wee a blow to him. Their removal, however, as had been very properly pointed out in the report was a stimulus for the young men of the congregation to come out and take their places. Be cculd say without hesitation that no man or woman had ever regretted the time .spent in , Sunday school work. Of course as they got older and other responsibilities came upon them it was not possible for anyone to take:.such active engagement in the work , of the Sunday school, and therefore he urged the young men and women present not to lose any opportunity of doing everything they couldito help on the cause. He could assure them that nothing would give them mere ' complete satisfaction than the knowledge that they had done and were doing their best to teach the young echolars as they themselves had previously been taught by those wire had alr..ady passed away.:He congratulated them on the new building they now occupied which would increase their scope fur usefuloess. He felt assured that they would rese to the occasion and would see to it that the increased facilities would be taken advantage of. He reminded them that they had occupied their position in that district before anybody else and he sincerely hoped that the Mission would still remain the premier religious institution in the district for very many years to come. (Applause.) He could not c'ose his remarks without making a Mich': reference to the war which was at present raging. Of course this Christmas time had brought sorrow to many an English home, hundreds (from the very hiehest homes to the humblest cottage in the land) were mourning for loved ones they had lost on the battlefield. They sincerely sympathised with the sorrowing ones. They had read a great deal as regarded the commencement of the war. Of course he would not like to city who was responsible for the war; that would have to be dealt with leer on, and those responsihle for any blunder—for a b'u -Aar he believed had been made —would have to be brought to book. The time,however, was not yet. Of c nirse he was only speaking his own views. He was quit atisfied that (putting aside all con- ( siderations as to the cause of the war) in the interests of 1 the whole world's tutors it was of the Minos', importance t that the British forces should be succeseful. He believed that our Success would mean greater progress, greater happiness and greater opportunities for the spreading of the Kingdom of Jesus Christ, as success to our arms had previously meant. The British Natien had done great things, hut greater things remained to be dare. Ws had been the pioneers of all that was for the world's general! good (aided and abetted, be admitted, ty other nations), but no nation had taken the stand we had taken, in regard to the things referred to; and therefore it was his hope and prayer that, sooner or later(a.lthoueh the cost might be terrible, both as regarded loss of life and in other respects) in God's good providence, and in His own good time, our armies would be successful in South Africa. (Applause.) He thought the war was doing us some good. In the first place it was taking some of the haughty pride out of us as a nation. We had made ourselves pretty con- EnietlOLlS:lin some quarters of the weed by pride, for things about which we had better not have been se proud. But the war was doing more than that ;it V7Eig teaching us the lesson that there were other people as brave as ourselves. In spite of all that might be said against our enemies in South Africa. they were a brave people, and seine of the illustrations that had been sent over showed that many acts of kindness had been done by the enemy to our men, which equalled anything that could be done by Eaglishmen. Let thorn not forget that in this war they were not •</p>
        <p>fighting it; lot of barbarians, but a prople who had a pride of home an home life just as much as ourselves. (Applause.) In spite of that, however, he thought that it was in the interests of the whole world that Britain should conquer, and he had no doubt that we should do so, relying not upon our money bags and powder maga- zines, but on those qualities of dauntless courage and bravery which had made England what slat was to-day, and what we hoped she would realain, for centuries to come. (Loud applause.) A programme of miscellaneous music was provided. Mr. George Geugh's bass voice was heard tn d van ta g e in the two songs "The King') 07n" and "Out on the Deep." Mr. S. Hassan was also successful in his inter- pretation of "Take a pair of sparkling eyes " and " Ailsa Mine." Mrs. Williams's initial performance was " The Last Dream" which was given will her usual ability. Later in the evening shesO "By the Foun- tain." Miss Wooley is a premising contralto and her singina of "Acne Laurie" and" Sho wore a wreath of roses" vas well recsived. In restiome to an encore she sang7"Tioree Fishers." Two recitations. by Mrs Farrar, "The Piper of Dargai " and "Three, cheers for our Volunteers." were greatly appractiated. The choir under the conductorship of Mr. F Atkinson very creditably sang several glees. Mr. Atkinson also accompanied the soloiats on the piano. The humour of the evening was supplied by Mr. A Grounds whose musical sketch " A Village Concert," and a ventriloquial sketch "music and mimicry " were very popular wi:h the audience. A vote of thanks was tendered to the artistes and chairman on the proposition of Mr. Joseph Monks. A. successful mectlng concludel with the singing of the Doxology. 'els</p>
