      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-01-26_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <head>INFLUENZA INCREASING.</head>
        <p>INFLUENZA INCREASING.</p>
        <p>The Influenza Epidemic is again spreading over the Country. The visitation is of a very prostrating nature, and the diaeaae is certainly on the increase. The origin of the disease or rather the ciresonastance3 which bring it about is as much a mystery ae ever. Yon cinnot trifle with Influenza. It undermines the Ner 'sous Systen to a greater extent than almost any other disease, and it is Pneumonia that kills, and Pneumonia strikes the man who imagines he had only a mild form of Influenza, and can go about the strew; just as usual. It can hardly be doubted that the prevalence ef Chest and Lung Com- plaints must be ascribed to the baneful influence of In- fluenza, whose after effects so frequently take this form. It was a bad day for this Country when this malignant scourge first visited our shores. Year after year it has never since failed to revive at its due time, and to extort its tribute of human life. Having observed its ravap;rs and its dreadful effects upon the constitution on the occasion of its former visits, we cannot but regard its recurrence with much fear, as a great many have not yet, if they ever wilt, regain their former health. As a Preventative, we know of nothing equal to Gwilym Evans' Quinine Bitters. A few strong doses taken in , time have often Droved effectual in wardine off attacks of this Malady, and always succeed in mitigating their severity when a patient is under their influence. Do you suffer from Chills, alternating with Sudden Flushes, Severe Headache, accompanied by difficulty of breath- ing, and symptoms of Catarrh, Pains in the Chest under and between the Shoulders, and a stiffness and soreness of the Muscles, Loss of Appetite and Nervousness. If you suffer from any of these symptoms, know that they are the forerunners of Influenza, and it behoves you to resort at once to a course of Gwilym Evans' Quinine B:t- -ters. By common consent Patients everywhere admit that they feel more depressed and miserable after an at- tack of Influenza than while under its influence, and more than they feel after an attack of any other illness. The best way to banish these miserable feelings is to take Gwilvm Evans' Quinine Bitters, The Vee,etable Tonic• We can strongly and confidently recommend this Preparation, as it has treated severe cases of Influenza, Heavy Colds, Extreme Weakness and Lan- gour, with greater success than any other known Remedy. We may also add that as a remedy for Indiees- tion, Sleeplessness, Low Spirits, Nervousness, and Loss of Appetite, Gwilym Evans' Quinine Bitters stands un- equalled. It is sold in bottles 21 9i and 4s 61., by all Chemists, and Patent Medicine Vendors, or direct car- riage free, for the above prices, by the Sole Froprietors : Quinine Bitters Manufacturing Company, Limited, Llanelly, South Wales. Avoid Imitations. See the name "Gwilym Evers" on Label Stamp, and Bottle.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-01-26_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>BEFORE the MAGISTRATES</head>
        <p>BEFORE the MAGISTRATES</p>
        <p>SCENE AT THE WORRHOUSE.—A " PERFECT TERROR." On Friday at the Warrington Police Court, before the Mayor and other magistrates, Annie Smith was charged with wilfully breaking a plate glass window in the door- way of the porter's lodge at the Workleouse at 10 o'clock the previous morningscomenitting damage to the amount of 3.—She pleaded not guilty.—She was further charged with assaulting Samuel Fernside, the porter, on the way to the Bridewell to which she pleaded guilty.—Mr. B. Shearer prosecuted on behalf of the Board of Guardians. He said the prisoner came to the Workhouse at 10 o'clock in Thursday morning with the obj,ct of gaining admittance. She had been an inmate of the Work- house for several years on and off, and spent the greater part of her time either there or iR gaol. She was allowed to pass through he doorway and she banged the door with such force that she broke the glass to pieces. She was under the influence of drink. The porter took her to the Po'ice Station, and on the way she was very violent and assaulted him.—Mrs. Fernside, the portress at the Workhouse, corroborated this statement.—Samuel Fern- side, the porter, said that the glass was valued at 31.— He toek the prisoner to the Police Statien, and on the way she was very violent and struck him in the face Several times. She was also very violent at the police station.—Mr Shearer : She is a perfect terror.—The Chief Constable said the woman had enede 28 previous appearances. She bad only le-en nut of gaol a couple of days.—Prisoner who was very disorderly in the dock, was committed to gaol for a month for breaking the window and two months for the assault.—Prisoner : Thank you gentlemen. I can do that like a bird.</p>
        <p>PECULIAR CONDUCT OF A WALTON MAN.</p>
        <p>A! Police Constable Carter was proceeding along Borough-road near the river, late on Saturday night he was startled by hearing oomeone, shouting " I'll do it " several times. He immediately rushed to the river bank and found a man who transpired to be Michael Whits,* farm labourer, of Lower Walton, standing at the water's edge flourishing his arms about excitedly. Hig prculiar conduct led the Constab!e to believe that he was about to jump into the river. He had a whiskey bottle in one band and, re use the policeman's words, be was "mad drunk." With the aid of P.C. Cooper the man -,was taken to the Police Station,though not without a struggle. On Monday morning, solwre I, and subdued he appeared before the magistrates on a charge of being drunk and disorderly. He was very penitent and was allowed his liberty on paying a fine of 53 and costs.</p>
        <p>YDITNO THIEVES GET THE BIRCH ROD.</p>
        <p>Although he is only thirteen years of age, George Donoghue seems to be getting quite beyond the control of his parents, most probably due to their own neglect. Per some time past be has continually played truant from school and has been ele,pime out at niehts. A short time ago he was brought before the magiatratee end cae committed to a truant school, but on being medically ex- amined was rejected as being in a delicate state of health. Be now appears to hare become a young thief and vaga- bond, and be is not content with committing depredations himself but induces other boys to follow his example. One of hie latest "disciples" is Thomas Howell, who is about 13 years of age and resides at 13, Albert-street. On Wednesday last week a greengrocer named Mrs. Littler attended the Warrington Market and engaged Donoghue to carry a basket for her to Bank Quay Station. He departed on the errand and she followed later on, but was surprised to find that he had not been near the station. She waited for him to come for a con- siderable time and missed several trains. As there were no signs of the boy appearing information was given to the police. It appears that instead of taking the basket to its rightful owner, Donoghue sold it to a Mrs. Hennesev of Hale-street for the large sum of two- pence. He told her that it was his mother's basket and he had told him to sell it to obtain money with which o buy newspapers. On Saturday the boy was arrested by Detective Forster and when charged he said " Yes I took it and sold it to a woman for 21." On Saturday evening, the boy Howell was apprehended by Superin- tendent Monaghan and Detective Forster in connection with the theft of the baeltet and was found to be wearing a pair of boots which had been stolen from the shop of Mr. William Leslie, secend hand dealer. of Mersey-street. The boots were seen safe by Mr. Leslie's son on the afternoon of the 18th inst., and were missed at seven o'clock. When charged with stealing the boots, Howell admitted the theft.—On Monday morning, both boys appeared before the magistrates, when the above facts were related by the Chief Constable —who said that Howell had been led astray by the other boy. Be had been a good boy up to the time he met Donoghue. Donoghue was neglected by his parent's.— The father of Donoghue said the boy had caused laim a lot of trouble and he could not control him.—The Bench ordered Donoghue to receive twelve strokes with the birch rod for stealing the basket, and for the theft of the boots Howell received six strokes. CHELL AND HIS CAPERING—TWO MosTus FOR ASSAULT-</p>
        <p>ING AN OLD MAN.</p>
        <p>At the Runcorn Police Court. on Wedneselay morning, before Messrs. James Handley and A. R. Norman, Frederick Chell, greengrocer, etc., Runcora, a well- known character, was charged, on remand, with being drunk and disorderly on Nunday evening, and with assaulting an old man named James Bradbury, on Satur- day night—Both cases were tak9n together, and Chell pleaded not guilty to each.—Constable T. S. Bowyer said about quarter to eight on Sunday night, he was in Greenway-road, when he saw Chell reeling about the street " mad drunk." He was flinging his arms about and creating a great commotion. He stepped several young men, and wanted them to pay for a drink. Upon them refusing, Chell would look up to the sky and say The dewdrops of Heaven are falling upon you." (Laughter.) On witness taking Chell into custody, Chell broke away and raced down Victoria-road,and it was with great difficulty that he was recaptured and taken to the station.—Chell said tbo Constable came up to him, and wanted him to go and beg several people's pardon, but " he did not court any man as he was a man all over himself." James Bradbury, said he was 63 years of age, a delph labourer, and resided at 99, Halton-road. About half past ten, on Saturday last, he was returning borce.when las saw the man Chell soming out of Mr. John Salt's shop. Chell had a jug with bier, which he put down on the floor, and commenced capering about in the road, saying witnesa was just the man he was looking for.' Witness said" hold on ; what do you want me for ?'</p>
        <p>But Chell replied by striking him two or three times,and kickiag him on the arm. Witness was knocked over, and whilst on the floor Chell kicked him, making his nose and mouth bleed. Chell took a runnin, kick at wi'ness. In consequence of the assault, witnass had suffer:d very much !rom pain in the 131 k, and, on Sun- day and Monday, he lost a lot of blood.—Cross-exam- ined : Ile never spoke to prisoner, nor was hie wife with him at the time.—Cnell. addre•ising the Bench said,"One could have nothing without a battle. It is the same thing over again so far as lam concerned. The man only got what I often get. I have been knocked down:many a time as far as that goes."—Superintendent D. Pearson said he was of opinion that the man was mad, e.nd anyone seeing the way he acted sometimes would be of that opinion tee. Chell had, however, been under medical surveillawie time after time, but on each occasion he had been certified sane.—Mr. Johnson bore cut: this statement.—The Bench sentenced Chell to one month hard labour for being drunk and disorderly ; and two months' hard labour for assaulting the old man. Saiatenc,,s t 3 run consecutively.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-01-26_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>WORKHOUSE INMATE SENT TO GAOL.</head>
        <p>A WORKHOUSE INMATE SENT TO GAOL.</p>
        <p>Before Messrs. G. L. Davies, and F. J. Norman, at the Runcorn Police Court on Thursday morning, Annie Al- bington, was charged whilst being a paup.r inmate of the Runcorn Workhouse, she had on the 23rd day of January ran away from that institution taking with her 153 worth of clothing the property of the Runcorn Guardians.—Evidence was given by John Roberts, as- sistant master, that the woman ran away after Foaling a wall above 10ft. high. She had been an inmate of the Workhouse ever since she wai a girl, and was continu- ally,falling out with the other female inrnat,s.—Prisoner who admittel runni, g away, asierted the " boot was on the other leg." It was the other women who annoyed and assaulted her.—Committed for 21 days.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-01-26_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>MINOR CASES.</head>
        <p>MINOR CASES.</p>
        <p>Warrngton Borough Court.</p>
        <p>FRlDAY.—Before the Mayor (in the chair), Alderman Wright, Councillor Smethurst, Dr, Ciornall, and Messrs. John White, and J. Hepherd. LIEF r OF A SACK OF FLOUR.—Peter LOAgShAW was charged with stealing from Messrs. James Fairclough and saws mill, Bank Quay, at 1-30 on Wednesday afternoon a bag containing 14,21bs of flour valued at 14s their property. The Chief Constable said that prisoner had been in Messrs. Fairclough's employ for six months as a carter. Information reaehei the police on Thursday which led Sneer- intendant Monaghan to visit the man'a house after ten o'clock at night in company with a Mr. Jones, a traveller for the firm. They saw the prisoner and Superin- tendent Monaghan told him that they had reason to believe that he had something in the house belonging to Messrs. Fairclough. The man became very indignant and said he had nothing belonging to the firm except a coat which was allowed to all the carters. The Superintendent then put the matter more plainly and said " I know that you have a sack of flour here that von have no right to. and if you can give an account of it, well and good." The man became still more virtuously Indignant, and said they could not seareh the house without thy had a warrant. On being told that his house would be watched all night by the police till the warrant was obtained the following morning he "caved in " somewhat and said be had a sack of dour upstairs. but it was his own. He was asked to account for having it in his possession, ant he then said " It is no nee telling any further lies about it. I took it myself." He further stated that he took it when he went on a journey to Lowten St. Mary's, and left it at his house. The value of the flour wes 14s. He (Mr. Talbot), was instructed to say that 'Messrs. F.irclough felt very strongly in the matter. They had suspeeted that that kind of thing had been going on for a considerable time. It was, however, very difficult to detect the theft and pilfering in such a large werke.—ln answer to the Mayor, Mr Jones, the representative of the firm, said that before the prisoner set out the number of sacks on the tart was bo deed and the load was weighed. The seek that was stolen must have been put on the cart afterwards.—The prisoner No sir it was not. It was put on before it was weighed.—&amp; fine of 40s with the alternative of a month's imprisonment was imposed. SATURDAY.—Before Messrs. Birchall (in the chair), It T Fairclough. Geddes, and Hayward.</p>
        <p>BEGGING.—James Andrews was sent to gaol for seven days on the evidenco of P.C. Cloulh for begging in Marshhouse- lane at 12.10 the previous day. SLEEPING Otrr.—Thomas Murray was charged with being found sleeping on the premises of Messrs. Monks Hall and Co., on Friday night without visible mean. of subsistence.— William Kenny, a watchman in the employ of Messrs. Monks Hall and Ca., said that at 8 o'olock th, previous night he found the prisoner going to sleep in the mills. He turned him out, but at 11 o'clock be found him In a labourer's cabin, He caller! in P.C. Muskett and they tnrned him out ag on and cautions:! him. Later on, he again found him lying down in the cabin, and sent for an officer and gave him into custody.—Prisoner had been found sleeping on the premises three nights in succession. He was formerly in the employ of the Aral but was dis- charged as it was thought he was not in his righ mind. After consultation the magistrates decided that the charge should be altered to that of being ft lunatic at large and remanded the prisoner to the Workhouse for 14 days in order that he could be medically examined. MONDAY.—Before Mr. F. Monks (in the chair). Councillor Smethurst, and Messrs. Birchall, S. Wright and J. W.</p>
        <p>Hayward. A. dUsPiciouS CHARACTER.—Bdward Watson alias Dick- son, last from Manchester, %Via charged that he, being a sus- picious character, W4B found on the enclosed prom isea of the dwelling hones of James Phillips, 73, Mersey-street. at 10.15 on Sunday night with intent to commit a felony— He pleaded not guilty—John Phillips, a wire weaver. residing at 5, Derby-street, said that at 10.15 he was in the house of his sister-in law, Mrs. Phillips 7C.', Mersey-street They were sitting in the kitchen when they heard the front door, which opened directly on to the street, open very softly. The front room was in darkness, He went into the room, but could not see anyone there. He was aboet to light the gas when his hand came in contact with the prisoner, who was standing in the centre of the room. He asked him what he wanted and h said he was looking for a lodging-house. Mrs. Phillips wa a very nervous and deaf person and had she been in the house alone the consequences might have been serious.— Prisoner was, handed over to the police.—The prisoner said be thought that the house was a lodging-house. —He was sent to gaol for a month with hart labour. ASSAULT orr A CONSTABLE.—Donstnic O'Connor. Meadow Green Farm, High Leigh, was charged with being drank and disorderly in Bridge-street at 11.411 on Sunday night.and with assaulting P.C.Carter at the same time.—P.C. Carter .stated that at Cab time in question he was on duty in Bridge- street when he h,-ard a noise as though someoae was trying a door in Mr. Mainwaring's yard. On going in the yard he saw the prisoner kicking at a door and trying to force it open. He asked him what he was doing and he made use of filthy language. Witness took him into custody and he be- came very violent and struck him in the chest knocking him to the groend. Witness managed to put the handcuffs on and took him to the Police Station.—P.S. Williams said he was in the Police Office when the prisoner was brought in. He was "mad " with drink and threatened P.C. Carter several times. He was very violent in the cells—Prisoner was fined ss. for drunkenness and 53. and costs for the assault.</p>
        <p>WEDNESD &amp;Y.—Before Alderman Sutton (in the chair) and</p>
        <p>Mr. T. Birehall.</p>
        <p>BEGGlNG.—Patrick Murphy. last from Liverpool. was fined 5s and casts for begging in Outtermarket-street at 8.15 on Tuesday evening. SROP DOOR THEFT.—James Kaney; a stranger, was charged with stealing from outside the shop of Messrs. Unsworth's Bros.. Lyme-street. a youth's jersey valued at Is 6d at seven o'clock on Tuesday evening. lits pleaded guilty.—The Chief Constable said that at the time stated a fish salesman named Balshaw was in Lyme street when he saw the prisoner go up to the do-sr of the shop and take a youth's jersey and then walk aw ty. He informed 'dr Buck. an assistant in the shop, about the matter. The prisoner was followed and given into custody. When charged, prisoner admitted the offence. Prisoner was committed to gaol for fourteen days with har labour.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-01-26_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>Newton Petty Sessions</head>
        <p>Newton Petty Sessions</p>
        <p>THURSDAY—Before Messrs. T. J. Gillespie (in tha chair), C,</p>
        <p>C. F. Borron and John Jackson.</p>
        <p>LICENSING.—The li.ense of the " aeneral Elliott "Inn, Croft, was transferred from Ralph G. Green to James Lodge. A "CONSCIENTIOUS OBSECTOR."—Harry Pearson of Mercer- street, Earlestown, applied for a certificate of exemption from vaccination for his child. He said he believed vaccination was prejucihial to the health of the child. He had seen many cases of suffering from it and on that ground alone he had a strong objection.—The Chairman said that if there wer6 many like the applicant in the country there would be an outbreak of smallpox and then they would be" running in" fast, trying to get vaccinated.—The application was granted. ALLEGED AsSAULT AT GOLBORNE.—JAbez Ingle was summoned for assaulting Edith Inca at Golborne on the Bth January.—He pleaded not guilty.—Complainant said that about one o'clock on the morning of the Bth January she went out to look for her husband, as ahe suspected he bad been in a" common place." She met him in company with the defendant and two women and renaoastr ited with him for being out at that hour. The defendant thereupon struck at her and tore her jacket sleeve. He then tried to choke her and followed her to her house. She was packing up her things in order to leave the house, and the defendant pitched her clothes across the room. lie then knocked her against the" squeezers." Evidence was giver, in support of this statement.—Defendant denied the assault and evidence was called to show that there was a general quarrel and Mrs. lace threatened to throw a paraffin lamp at Ingle.—The case was dismissed.</p>
        <p>D hUNICENNEB3.—The following were fined for this offence: —Margaret J Burgess, William Bailey, Arthur Worsley, and Henry Mains. 2s 6d and costs ; Michael Cunneriv, 5s and coats; Daniel Conroy, lOs and costs; Thomas Thompson, 2s 6d. A LOWTON SIAN SUMMONED TOR TRESPASSING. —William Litherland of Lowton, was summoned for having on the 25th December at Newton, trespass, d on land in the occupation of Lord Newton in search of partridges.—He pleaded not guilty. —Mr H J Longton prosecuted on behalf of Lord Newton and Mr Wid •ows, of Leigh, appeared on behalf of the defendant. —Mr Longton said that at 3.15 on Bank Holiday the 26,11 December, David Jax, a gantekeepr on the Kenyon estate of Mr G Kenyon, which adjoined the estate of Lord Newton, Highfield Moss, was watching game belonging to his own master. He was in concealment about 19 yards from Lord Nevrton's boundary when he saw the defendant and another man pass Wong the footpath on Lord Newton's land. The defendant was carrying a gun and there Was also a retriever dog, but it was not known to whom it belonged. The gamekeeper did not Interfere with the men so long as they were walking down the footpath. Th 3 men p nosed them for a d!stanca of between eighty and ninety yards, when, instead of proceed- ing in a line along the path they deviated and went on the Moss for a distance of forty or fifty yards. Jax was within nineteen yards of them and saw them rouse a cevey of partridges. Defendant shot at the partridges and hit a bird which dropped on the Moss. Jax could not see whether they picked up the bird or not. The men then pro- ceeded in the direction of Lowton. —David Jax gave evidence bearing out this statenaent.—Samuel Watkins head gamekeeper to Lord Newton said that Highfield Moss belonged to Lord Newton. He had seen partridges on the land recently. The land was in the township of Newton.—</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-01-26_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>EX A MINER, FRI DAY, JANUARY 26, 1900.</head>
        <p>THE EX A MINER, FRI DAY, JANUARY 26, 1900.</p>
        <p>Mr. Widdows for the defence, said that the defendant denied being on the Highfield Mos at all. The gamekeeper had made a mistake. At the time ia question defendant was at his father's house, three and a halt miles away.—Defendant gay evidence and said he was at Wargrave, the whole of ths day i 9 question.—Cross-examined Ths latt time he was at the Moss was a month or five weeks ago, on a Saturday after- noon. His brother had a dog but it was a collie—Walter Litherland, defendant's brother, corr .borated,—The Bench considered there was a doubt in the matter and gave the defendant the benefit of it.—The case was dismissed. ARREARS IN MAINTENINCE.—Thomas Gliliard WaS 8111:11 • mooed for neglectina to comply with an order made on the 30th November for him to contribute 10s a week to the main- tenance of his wife and pay the Court costs al 13s 6d.—He was ordered to pay the money be the next Court day or go to gaol for a month with hard labour.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-01-26_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>Runcorn Petty Sessions.</head>
        <p>Runcorn Petty Sessions.</p>
        <p>MoNnaY-7Before Alessrs. F. J. Norman (presiding), and T,</p>
        <p>Wiewall.</p>
        <p>TH% COST OF A CHIMNEY FlRE.—Thomas Frain, Mersey- street, who was represented by his wife, was fined 43 6.1 for allowing his chimney to be on fire. notwithita-iding the fact that his represent,ttive asserted the chimney had never been en fire before, during the 20 years she resided there.—The case was Droved by Acting sergeant White. and Mr. J. Percival, inspector of nuisances, represented the Urban Dis- trict Council.</p>
        <p>_ . OBSCENE LANGUIGE.—For indulging in this vile practice, within hearing of the public, Tho -aas Newton, junior, King- street. was fined 52.</p>
        <p>THE POOR LAW GUARDI.LNB CLIIH FOR MAINTENANCE.— George Dunbavin and Charles Henry Dunbavin, labourers, were summoned at the instance of the Runcorn Board:of Guardians, for £; 12s 6d and £2 16s Od respectively, bei g chiefly arrears due on orders made on the 241,4 May, that they contribute Is per week towards the maintenance of their ifather, who was chargeable to the Runcorn Union.—George Dunbavin attributed his inability to pay to having bren out of work for varying periods.—Mr Johnson (who appeared for Mr John Frodsham, collector to , the Union) intimated that those periods had been taken into , Consideration and £1 was taken off the original amount for lost time. Charles Henry Dunbavin'schief objection to paying the money was because he knew of other people who ought to contribute towar-Is the maintenance of some of their rela- tives, and did not. —The Bench ordered both to pay 5s a week regularly to the collector until the amount was reduced to its proper proportion. Failing which are order would be signed for their removal to Knutsford gaol for one month.- -An order was made on Joseph Twig, collier, to contribute 6d a week towards the maintenance of his motner.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-01-26_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <head>COUGH MIXTURE WHICH GIVES SATISFACTION.</head>
        <p>A COUGH MIXTURE WHICH GIVES SATISFACTION.</p>
        <p>That is the very medicine you want—something that will cure your cold, and at once relieve you of th.t wretched tickling and soreness in your throat. Well, then, try La vans Lung Linctus. Is a cough mixture it is unrivalled. The rose will Jiff yuu ic:ief. A few doses will cure the most painful cough or sore throat, Bronchitis, A4hma, Colds in the aead, etc. In bottles, is. 1-.12-d., 2i. 91., 44. 63„ post free, from the Proprietors, Green and Co., Chemists, Bolton. All Chemists and Co- op. Stores sell L'IVATI9 Lu,;fz Linctus. Mts. B. Ralphs, of 135, Eves Lane, Chorley, says : " Having used your Laval's Lung. Lanctut for a period of many years. I have to say that I havo found it to be absolutely the best and most certain cure for all com- plaints of the ches: and throat I have ever tried. I re- commend all who suffer from catarrh, sore throats, or cold on the chest, to give it a trial."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-01-26_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>ASSOCIATION FOOTBALL. Wigan Cup.—Third Round. Warrington v. Wigan County.</head>
        <p>ASSOCIATION FOOTBALL. Wigan Cup.—Third Round. Warrington v. Wigan County.</p>
        <p>Warrington, after defeating G Aherne and Castle iii in the initial rounds of this competition, were drawn against Ow strone county team of Wigan. The War- rington executive placed a st,rong team in the fi-ld, and had every confidence in them giving a good account of themselves. There was only a meagre attendance of spectators when Pierpoiat started on behalf of the visi- tors, who quickly invaded the home quarters,hut relieved with fine kicking. Aspinall's charge was now assailed, and he saved some good shots from Evenson and Kelly. Warrington next took up the attack, good passing by the left wing giving Plerpoint a chance to teat Garvey, wh proved himself quite safe. End-toend play now ensir d, both teams striving bard to score, but neither succeeding,. The score was nil at half time. On the re-start Warrington attacked in grand style, Connor shooting over. From the goal kick Hunter gained possessien and gave to Murray, who tricked Evens and gave to Pierpoint, who made no mistake, and found the net with a grand shot amid the hearty ap- plause of the Warrinetan spectators. Tinge was now closely approaching, and the home teem attacked vigor- ously, Evans, Evenson and Kelly testing Aspinell with splendid shots, all of which he cleared ia fine style,until Evenson, meeting one of his clearanees, shot into the net, thus cqulliz'ng the scores three minutes from the call of time. Time was now signalled, but the referee ordered ten minutes extra to be played both ways. The first ten minutes arrived without any alteration but in the last stage Kelly managed to give Wigan the lead, a grand game thus ending by the Warringtenians losing by the bare margin of a goal, the sceres being—Wigan County, two goals ; Warrington, one goal. Lancashire League. Earlestown v. Southport Central.</p>
        <p>These clubs brought off their return League fixture en Saturday on the Eerlestown ground bafore a poor gate. It will be remembered that early in the season Southport defeated Eaelestown on the tormer's ground by 5 goals to 1, but on Saturday this decision was rd versed, much to toe surprise of Seuthport. Earlestown lost the spin of the coin and had to kick off down hill. Some lively ex- changes took place, and the pace becanse fast the South- pert min le•qng evidently bent on forcing matters. Baxendale was in good form but Johnson clune tenaciously to him, and through,ut the game never allowed him to become dangerous. A rush by Hampson and Whitehouse jeopardised the Southport goal,and Cue. tiffs in attempting to clear, sent the ball into his own goal, thus sco-isig early on for Earleatown. From the kick off even m spirit was infused into the ganae,and Chew was troubled on several oceasions, but he cleverly warded off danger. Heyee and Cottam played a sterling game and ably assiseed Chew in keeping his charge intact. Ellis, who was playing centre terward vice Linton, a'aewed excellent form in the field, and though his play was not of the brilliant order, his touches were of the kind likely to bear fruit. Frequent visits to each goal ultimately resulted in Her/mien and Whitehouse again becoming daneereus, and some long passing enabled Ellis to get down in a good positiou,and securing the possession he sent the ball into the net. No other point was scored to half time. On turning round Esrlestoern bad the hill agaiast teem, but they corn• plately ovrplayed Southport and succeeied in notching two more teals. Play afterwards became rather locus and Southport availing themselves of every opening. managed to score twice in quick succession. At the call of time the game stooi Earlestown 4, Southport Central 2.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-01-26_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <head>BRITAIN v. THE WORLD IN SHIP-BUILDING.</head>
        <p>BRITAIN v. THE WORLD IN SHIP-BUILDING.</p>
        <p>According to Eagineering., we in Great Britain built last year two tons of shippin for every one ton built by all the countries of the world cemhined. Thus last year there were built in this country 726 sea-geing steamers, totalling 1,416,791 tons ; while in the celonies and foreign countries 543 vessels, of 704,947 tons were built, making the ad iition to the world's tonnage 2,121,738 tons, much more than double the wastage due to deca y and disaster. It is remarkable that while we build no sailing fillips, foreign countries! constructed 174,002 tons of such craft. Among foreign countries,the three leading places are held by the United States of America (225,000 tons), Germany (211,000 tens), and Franca (90,000 tons). Of the mercantile ton- nage built in the United States, a considerable pro- pertion does not effect tee general commerce of tke world, being intended for servicA on the Great Lakes. As showing the size of vessels employed in that trade, it may be mentioned that 11 steamers have been built for it during 1899 of upwards of 4,000 tons each, besides two sailing barees of 5.000 tons each. Germany has launched the Patricia, of 13,293 tens, and tile Grossor Kurthrst, of 12,500 tons, besides six other steamers of 10,000 tons and over, In France, the construction of large sailing vessels, almost atianioned elsewhere, has continued to flourish under the influence of the bounties granted by the State. Twenty-four such vessels, of 2,000 tons and upwards, have been launched, during the year under review. The largeet of these, and likewi=e the largest sea-going &amp;tiling vessels built in the world during the year, are the Ville de Mulhouse and Ville du Havre, of 3214 tons each, Both have been built at Havre. No great expansion is noticeable in the construction of steamers in France. In Italy, the mercantile output of the year has increased to 49,500 tons. The returns of vessels now gin progress amount to abeue 107,000 tons, as compared with only 34,000 tons at the end of 1897, and with 90,000 tons at the end of 1898. Allowing for wastage, the sailing ton- nage of the world has been reduced by about 206,000 tone durine 1899 ; the stem] tonnage has increased by about 1.601,000 tons • The net increase of the world's mercantile tonnage is, therefore, 1,395,000 tons. Britaines net increase of 313,000 tons is equivalent to 22.5 per cent. of the total. In the net increase of the world's steam tonnage, viz.,1,601,000 tone. the United Kingdom hag shared to the extent of 498,000 tout; nr 31 per cent. Of the new tonnage launched during 1899, the United Kingdom has acquired 54 per cent.</p>
        <p>Sold under a to sci • 01"-- aclb Hea Tbm DR 0 PS Arne, mont frj.t. 7,, • moni •13 Sale Er 4. • • mAR4 eszck</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-01-26_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>RUGBY FOOTBALL.</head>
        <p>RUGBY FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>N view of he approaching Football Season, it may be in teres:ing to-state ths.t the world-farned manuficturers Geo. G. BUSSEY tCo , of London, are prepa7ing a stock o Bads, *Weil for cputli'6y, uniformity, an i durability, will h unique. These bills will be distinguished by the appearanc thereon of their well known trade mark &lt;a/KC. Ask th local Sports Dealer for their Catalogue. I: not procurable^ apply to 36 tic 38, Queen Victoria-street. London. Factorie —rec%ham, London; anl Jlmswell, uffolk.—Advt.</p>
        <p>*** Secretaries of Football Clubs are requested to note that reports of matches piaved on Saturday must be sent to the Head Offic,., Bewsey-street. Warrington, nat later than 10 o'clock on Thursday morning or they cannot be inserted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-01-26_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
        <head>NORTHERN UNION. LANCASHIRE. SENIOR COMPETITION.</head>
        <p>THE NORTHERN UNION. LANCASHIRE. SENIOR COMPETITION.</p>
        <p>Warrington Beaten Again.</p>
        <p>re'.urn snatch betwsen Salford and Warrington was played on the ground of the latter in the presence of about 4,000 --_,peatators last Saturday. Salford were without Harter and Hadwen. good substitutes however being found in Thomas and Hoskins. Warrington were anxious to avenge the defeat sustained at New Barns earlier in the season and played a fairly strong team, Morrison and Eden being prominent absentees from the forwards. Their pla-ses were taken by Sudlow and W Ashton, and a trial at half-back was also given to a you,h named Burgess, from Flixton. The weather turned out very ia•rourab!e for the game, and though the ground was on the soft side i was far from being heavy. Mr. F. Kennedy (Broughton) was the referee, and the teams lined up as follows : Salford :—Smith, back ; Hoskins, Williams, Thomas, and Pearson. threequartora ; Grey and Phillips, half backs ; Tunney, Woodhead, lihappa, Shaw, Shore, Heath, J Williams, and "riv:•,maa forwards. Warrington : Boscow, full back ; Fish, Isherwood, Smith and Jones, threequarters ; Duckwerth and Burgess, half hacks ; Taylor. Sufilow, Mereweather, Schnitze, Fiirhurst, Boardman, Hatton, and W Ashton forwards.</p>
        <p>Salford won the toss and Jones started against the wind and sun, tlie ball rolling in touch. Smith punted well into the W:rrington half, T Williams following suit, only t) be repelled by a clever mark on the part of Fish. Even play follewed in the Salford hail, Duckworth gaining ap- plaese by a clever break-away from the scrimmage which was spoiled by Phillips. Salford got down into the home half, Hoskins attempting to round Fish, but finding the task a bit beyond him. Thomai got possession on the opposite wing, on'y to be grassed by Jones. S..nith picked up and ran well into the Salford half, tut the visitors' custodian was equal to the emergency. There were numerous penalties against Grey and Burgess for peeing tire ball in wrong,and the game dtieericrated a lot. Passing was attempted with poor results on both sides. Person nearly scored by an individual effort, dashing, over the line just too late to prevent BOMOW touching down. Warrington rushed back to centre, Duckworth feedine Fish from the scrim- mage. Toe wing man cleeerly eluded Hoskins, but was tackled by Williams from behind, the latter just seizing Fish by the shirt as he was spriatiug clear. A lofty punt by Jones caused Smith to mull on the Salford line, where Hattoa was within an ace of scoring. Warrington at tacked for a time, but there was little spirit in the play, and beyond a clever dribble by Burgess Warring- ton appared rarely dangerous. Uninteresting play fol- lowed to the interval, when there was no score. Salford restarted, and the home forwards getting off side they had to defend. Williams and Husking got away on the right, but after covering some ground they ware pulled up by Fish and Isherwood. Both sides showed poor form all round, but the heavier Salford forwards began to assume the aggressive. Fish impeded Boseow, and Salford attacked near the line. Several efforts to score failed miserably, hut at last Grey dodged over from a scrimmage, and Phillips added a goal. The game was as good as over, for Warrington never looked like scoring. Salford maintained the attack and Phillips added to the score with a well-placed penalty red. Five minutes from time Warrington rushed the bAll to the Salvor] rend, where Burgess got possession and dropped a cell from amidst a awarm of opponents. The final came with the score reading :—Salford 2 goals 1 try (7 points); Warrington 1 goal (2 poiata).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-01-26_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <head>Runcorn Swamp Swinton.</head>
        <p>Runcorn Swamp Swinton.</p>
        <p>The crowd at Swinton en Saturday furnished ample proof th.t the Northern Union game in and around Man- cheater is in anything that a parlous state. It is a long time since the enclosure at the place netted was so ex- tensively patronised, and an attendance of 14,000 is evidence that with genial weather, and the prospects of a coed match there is plenty of support for Northern Union clube in tee busy districts. Saturday's crowd included a fair number of Oldhariers, who had doubt- less made the journeyeto see their most dangerous rivals disposed of in which case their anticipations were rudely diepolled. 01 form Swinton certainly seemed to have a chine?, but as things worked out they were hopelessly beaten, their opponents thus taking four points from them in two matches. Regarding the teams Swinton had their strongest availanle lot up, Jim Valentine, who has recovered from his illness, being a spectator. As thins turned out, his presence and weight would have been invaluable on Saturday. Runcorn missed J Butter- worth as three-quarter, that player having a badly bruised toe, whilst Farmer, one of their leading forwards managed to miss the train. The visitors started, and smart footwork by their for- wards enabled them to press early on. A few scrum- mages were fought in Swinton quarters, and from the break-ep of one of teem the bLtl wont to Goodman. Ile had his kick charged drewn,but his forwards fortunately relieved the danger, and Hampson and Taylor made a few yards of ground. A huge kick by Houghton sent the ball close to Swinton lines, and on to of this Jolley was conspicious with a smart effort. Then LeW:S was badly mauled in trying to get the ball away, but he had not to be taken off, and the next item was a smart tackle of Davies by Jolley, a dead bell accruing to the visitors a minute later. After dropping out 6winton were again pressed, but Morgan ultimately managed to reach half way with a useful run, Up to now Runcorn bad been clearly sup4ior, and Messer drew some ap- plause when he ran through one or two of the visiting backs- The game was raging at a fast pace, but noth- ing eventful happened until near half-time, when Jolley landed the first point by placing a goal from c. Penalty after Houghton had missed from a similar attempt. This was the only score before the interval, and in the secend half play was much in favour of Runcorn. They kept the Swinton defence well en the move, and in time a splendid piece of play between Hughes and Tayler had the result of the ball reaching Faulkner, who scored, no goal resulting from Jolley's attempt. Alter this Swint an were again penned, but ere the close they opened up the only real chance they had during the afternoon. This was by means of a fine run by Hampson, who almost scored,and on the top of this effort Morgan hit the cross-bar with a drop at goal. Hereabouts Runcern were very lucky in keeping their opponents out, but this they did, and making one final rush the visitors again attacked, and Warder scored. Jolley failed at this attempt, and Run- corn eventually woe by One goal and two tries to nil. _NOTES ON PLAY.</p>
        <p>me game. which was expected to produce such a capital F•trug-7,1e, was quite a disappointing affair, and with the exception of one chance Swinton were never in it. Although Runcorn won se easily it cannot be claimed that the contest wes a brilliant one. The visitors kept the play too close for that, and it was very seldom indeed that their halves passed out to their three-quarters. Tho result was that the Runcorn .forwards were usually on top of the home halvee and three-quarters, and the four placers in the latter pesition presented a very sorry spectacle. They could see very well that their opponents were keepin them close so as to double them up, and yet they never once adopted a change of tactics. A man like Jim Valenl-ine would have been more serviceable on Saturday, for with his old head he would have devised some means of making the visitors open out. It was not for want of chances that the Swinton blcke did so badly, for their forwards played exceedingly well, and they had pos- session oftener than the Rnncorn backs. On the matck the visitors were certainly superior, and won pretty comfortably. Individually Houghton was the biggest success of the day, and it need only be said he played as well as ever be did. The threeqearters did well, with Warder as the leader. Jolley on Saturday was clearly in front ef Faulkner, and forward Hughes, Taylor and Lightfoot were the pick. On behalf of Swinton,Goodman, at full-back, was a failure, and his kicking was so weak as to be of nu asvisiance to his forwards. One or two repetitions of Saturday's form and he will lose his place. It would be useless to criticise Hampson, Taylor,Messer, and Lewis. at three-querters. They seldom stuck to the ball when it reached them, and when they did never travelled above a few yards. They gave a wretched display. Davies and Morgan were outclassed at half- back, and the forwards were all good, being quite a match for the Runcorn men at anything like close play LANCASHIRE "A" TEAMS COMPETITION.</p>
        <p>Salford v. Warrington. This match was played at Salford on Saturday. The home team held the tipper hand throughout, Hignett, Norris, and Thomas scoring trips in the first half. Brown Griffiths and Hignett each failed with their goal kicks. At the interval Salford led by three tries to nothing. In the second half Smith, Crocker and Norris got tries for Salford and the final score read :—Salford, six tries (18 points) ; Warrington, nil.</p>
        <p>ASK for CUNNINGHAM'S iIIGH-CLASS MINERAL</p>
        <p>RITCHPN RA.NGES, Tile Grat,s,and Chimney Pieces Large Stock—.J. S. GARLsTvD,l.onmonger,Bridge-st. Adv</p>
        <p>MOURNING.—W. T. WILLIAMS makes a Speciality of the Mourning Department : Choice Selection of Materials always in Stock. Smart Dressmaking; Chugs moderate. 74, SANKEY-STREET, Warrington.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-01-26_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <head>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS. Rugby—Fo—o—tball. [By "Oval ist."]</head>
        <p>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS. Rugby—Fo—o—tball. [By "Oval ist."]</p>
        <p>The three Illactieatrir cluoa have etch tv,u at War- rington this season, a thing they have t.:o.ver done before in any one season. Swinton and [ford both won for the first time in fact, and as neither was expected to do, the blow was a hard one to bear. Salford followed Swinton's example on Saturday, and have now got the full complement of four points from Warrington. I am afraid prophesying, so far as the Warrington team is concerned, must be regarded as play,-!d au% I ventured to predict the defeat of Salford, but the result was re- versed, and for the future I shall not attempt to prophesy until I know. Most prophets would find this a wise plan to adopt.</p>
        <p>There is something particuLtriy annoying in Warring- ton's fixtures against Swinton and Salford. We had hoped 0- six poin's out of the three successive borne matches in January, and to ! tour of 'em have vanished already and the remaining two are fluttering their wings. Fr= the way the Warrington men have played at various times they appeared well able to hold their own at Wilderspool against any team, but when al pinch ctme they col- lapsed like punctured tyres.</p>
        <p>Before Christmas I told one of the committee men that Warrington wouki Lave all their work cut out to equal last season's record. Being a committee man he laughed at the idea, but a study of the facts show that Warrington will have to play very much better than they are doing if they expect to obtain the 23 noints they got last seAson. The record then was won 11, ,ost 14, drawn 1 ;16 goals 34 tries for ; and 32 goals, 51 tries, against ; points 23. Now it reads, played 19 ; won 8, lost 10. drawn 1, 13 goals, 30 tries for ; 11 goals 22 tries against; points 17. There are seven more league matches to play, and it is by no means certain that they will yield six points. Wigan and Roctodaie (home), and Millnm (away) are the easiest of the succeeding en- gagements, so that the chances do not appear difficult to reckon up. It is worth while noting too that Millem swamped Leigh on Saturday, and are by no means as poor a team as their rt cord appears to indicate. So far the only improvement on last year's performances is in the defence of the team, which has stood the racket fairly well. The scoring power however appears to be exactly the same, and it is here where improvement is so sorely needed.</p>
        <p>Saturday's game was one of the poorest seen on the Wilderspool ground for many weeks. Neither the win- ners nor the losers ever displayed form that would have done credit to their own " A" teams, and for the greater portion of the time a pointless draw appeared probable. Salford possessed a considerable advantage forward,and it was their preponderance of weight hero that won them the rnatcl7. The Warrington front rank entirely lacked dash or spirit and cave nothing like the display against Swinton. Their failure cannot be put down entirely to the absence of Eden and Morrison. There were one or two " passengers," and it was their lack of energy which crippled the team. The Salford eight pushed them about in a ridiculous fashinn,and the Vicki could hardly be expected to shine. Yet even they ought to have done better, and the fault was again at half back and centre. The wings are plenty good enough hut their chances are generally spoiled by poor combination. Apart from the weakness of the halves, Isherwood and Smith are both displaying a lack of dash and enterprise unusual for them. During the last two matches they have done little beyond field the ball, kick, and tackle. That is the sort of centre play whica has ruined War- rington for years, and it will have to he altered. In the Bull and Leigh matches they showed what they could .do, and we want to see it oftener.</p>
        <p>Salford had a very strong team pap,r, but t!.ey were little superior to Warrington. The backs dis- played the same want of dash and enternrise,and it was only the stirling pla7 of the forwards that retrieved them from utter failure. There was no score at all in the first half, hardly anything but shady play in the scrimmage, conseuent penalties, and frequent punt- ing. The second half was nearly as bad, in falc on the home side the disp ay worsened. Then the reds took to rushing matters and were finally rewarded by a try, augmented by a goal. A fortunate penalty kick gave them an extra two points. Warrington managed to escape being nilied,Burgess, the new half ;:ack,dropping a goal close on time.</p>
        <p>On the home side Fish and Jones were tho best. But- gesi played in promising style, and while he is reckless in putting the ball in and a little afraid of dodging into the open, he can pass like an old hand. The general opinion was that he had the makings of a smart player in him. Duckworth is in want of a rest, but the bck of a substitute makes it awkward for him to stand down. Grey and Phillips played a good game behind the Salford pack, but the three-quarter line was erratic and unreliable. Tom Williams and Hoskins are merely shadows of the once famous right wing. Smith, at full baak was fairly reliable, his kicking being especially well-judged. Boscow found touch much too often.</p>
        <p>The League table given below shows Warrington to be in just the same place. They are on a level with Widnes and Leigh, and povess the prior place on point average. Whether they will keen in front is doubtful. Even Stockport have climbed over the heads of Warrington, which was apparentir a very improbable thing wben Warrington visited Eigeley Park.</p>
        <p>The League table!up-to-date is R 9 follows : For. Against. P. W. L. D. G. T. P. G. T. P. P. Runcorn 18..16. 1. 1...25 36-158 5 4... 22...33 St. Helens 21..15.. 4. 2...24 50-198... 7 17... 65...32 Oldham 18...15... 2... 1...22 65-239... 7 13... 63...31 Swinton 18..13.. 4. 1...12 33-123...17 12... 70-27 Salford 20...10... 9... 0...24 43-177...24 26...128...22 Stockport l9 8. 9. 1...15 21— 93...13 27...107...18 Warrington 19.. 8...10... 1...13 30-116 ..11 22... 88...17 Widnes 19.. 7. 9. 3...13 29-113...14 31...121...17 Leigh 19... 7... 9... 3...18 24-108...20 41...163...17 *Brought% Rag'r'sl3... 8.., 9... 1...12 21— 87...14 19... 85...15 *Rochdale H'netsl9... 7...11... 1...15 14 72...12 36...132...13 Wigan 19. 5..14. 0. 6 18— 66...18 45...171...10 Millom 20.. 3...16.., 1...15 19— 87...22 53...203... 7 Tyidesley 2l. 2..18.. 1. 7 15— 69 ..37 72...290... 5 *Broughton Rangers and Rochdale Hornets have each had two points deductel for breach of rules.</p>
        <p>The "grate fite " with Runcorn comes off to-day, and the prospective champions should draw a big crewd to Wilderspool, where they are always popular visiters. As the first meeting between the clubs on Boxing Day only resulted in favour of the " Linnets by a try to nil, the game at Warrington should prove a very close affair. As a matter of fact it generally does, though the Run- cornians come out on the right side almost invariably. Warrington's poor displays of late have induced the Com- m ttee to try a little re-org,anisation of the forces, and to-day Jones, who has played exceedingly well on the wing will operate at full back in piace of Bogoow, whose kicking has been more use to the opponents than to War- rington of late. Duckworth is also dropped—l hope the rest veil! bring him back to form—and in his place Allen figures alongside Burgess. Harris comes into the team once more, and should improve the scoring powers of the three-quarter line.</p>
        <p>The changes are no doubt justified. but I ds not fancy they will make it any ths harder for Runcorn to win. At half back the two inexperienced players selected, can hardly combat successfully such a strong pair as Jolley and Faulkner, and it is here probably, that the team will be let down. I hope else centres will play with more dash or Warrington's fate will soon be sealed. One is naturally anxious for Warrington to inflict that long- expected licking on Runcorn, and at the same time it would bs a pity if such a gallant lo", as the "Linnets should lase the championship. To tell the truth, for' once I shall not be sorry te see Warrington low, though I could feel pleased if they won on their merits. Any- how a great game is sure to take place at Wilderspool to-day (Saturday).</p>
        <p>The committee have decided to resume the worke competition this season, and their decision has saved me the trouble of repeating the anathemas I hurled against them when it was abandoned. Too much cannot be done in the way of cultivating local talent. Runcorn's enccess is almost sntirely due te the fostering and train- ing of local juniors wbo have shown promise. A big town like Warrington,taking great interest in the game, should have no difficulty iti keeping up the supply of local footballers,if junior clubs were properly supported and encouraged. The wo-ks competition is an excellent method of bringing out what Wel talent there is, and as it will be conducted solely tor the benefit of those works not running a regular team, some good contests for the medals should 1213 forthcoming.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-01-26_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <head>Harriers.</head>
        <p>Harriers.</p>
        <p>The fixture for the Sankey Harriers Club on Saturday was an inter-club run with the Bakenbead Gymnasium Harriers, from their headquarters, the Y.M.C.A.. 22 turning out, composed of 10 of Birkenhead and 12 of Sankey. Trail was laid by Messrs. Jones and Whitfield. over some exceedingly heavy cluntry (including jumps over ditches and hedges In anticipation of the Northern) in the direction of Birkenhead Park, through the Golf Links, and home again, the distance being about 711 miles. The team finished in the following order :—San- key : 1, Jack Riley ;2. R Thressol ;3, E Hilton ; 4 Joe Riley; 5. T Devlin; 6, J Wilding; 21 points .Birkenhead: 7, J W Smiley ; 8, R Meery ; 11, F Speed ; 12, F W Mad- dock ; 14, T Scott; 15, W Campboll ; 67 points, the majority tor S nkey heing 46 points. The day being an ideal one for cross-country running, the run was thoroughly enjoyed. Afterwards the" Gyms " enter- tained Sankey to tea.</p>
