      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-01-26_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <head>DORIS IRENE RICHARDSON.</head>
        <p>DORIS IRENE RICHARDSON.</p>
        <p>We wish to again emphasize the beneficial effects of Scott's Emulsion in babies when cutting their teeth. It is difficult to explain all of the favourable influences of this preparation without entering into an exhaustive scientific discussion, nor do we think it is necessary. Every parent knows what a trying time this is for children, and also the serinus.pliacies of it. The body is feverish, digestion is upset. gems are sore, and often the children seem to be nothing but a bundle of nerves. Teething babies need help, and the bc.ist help they can have is Scott's Emulsion. You will be as much convinced of this fact, if you try this prepara- tion, as was the lady who wrote the following letter :</p>
        <p>"56, Lanesett Road, " Eillsbro', SheffiAld, " Aprii 12':.h, 1899.</p>
        <p>"Dear Sirs,—l am writing to tell you how much good Scott's Emulsion has done my husband and child. My husband always had a cold or was ailing some way, and had tried everything anyone recom- mended. Nothing did him much good, however, until lie tried Scott's Emulsion, and the first large bottle did him such a great amount or good that he was much improved in health. My baby was suffer- ing from spasms through teething. She was a fine child, but she went dreadfully thin and looked very weak. I thought I would try Scott's Emulsion f9r her. The first few doses did her good, and after a week or two she was looking better than ever. She</p>
        <p>(From a photogripL)</p>
        <p>has cut nearly all her teoth. She is only one year and seven months, avid a liner child it would be hard to find. I think there would ant be so many children suffering through teething if their parents would give them Scott's Env;!sloe. It is a splendid pre- watlon. Wishing it all the success it deserves,— Yours truly,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-01-26_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>(Signed)</head>
        <p>(Signed)</p>
        <p>"N. RICHARDSON."</p>
        <p>Here you see a practical demonstration of the scope of Scott's Ernukion. The father needed some- thing to strengthen the system and raise the stan- dard of health, while the baby also needed the properties v. hich Scott's Emulsion contains. Scott's Emulsion is cod-liver oil made palatable and easy of digestion, combined with hynophosp- hites of lime and soda, and glycerine. The oil has a favourable effrot upon the whole system, while the bypophosphites are food for the nerves, an aid to digestion, stimulant for the brain, and material for the formation of healthy bones. When given to children during teething, Scott's Emulsion has a soothing effect upon the digestive system and nerves, and belpi thorn in many ways to go through W. period without distress. You can obtain a sample of Scott's Emul- sion by sending threepence to cover postage to Scott and Bonne, Limited, Manufacturing Chemists, 95, Great Saffron Hill, London, E.C:, and mentioning this papsr. All chemists sell Scott's Emulsion.</p>
