      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-01-26_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
        <head>WAR.</head>
        <p>THE WAR.</p>
        <p>LATEST TELEGRAMS.</p>
        <p>SPION KOP CAPTURED.</p>
        <p>Boer Garrison Surprised. General Woodgate Wounded.</p>
        <p>The following despatch was issued by the War Office yesterday morning, : From General Buller to the Secretary of State for War. January 25, 12 10 a.m. (midnight). Warren's troops last night occupied Spion Kopf surprising the small garrison, who fled. It has been held by us all day, although we were 'heavily attacked, and especially by a very annoying shell fire.</p>
        <p>• I hear that our casualties are considerable, and bave to inform you with regret that General Woodgate is dangerously wounded.</p>
        <p>Warren is of opinion that he hss rendered the enemy's</p>
        <p>position untenable. The men are splendid.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN VOLUNTEERS, AND</p>
        <p>MILITIA.</p>
        <p>Enthusiastic Scenes at the Railway</p>
        <p>Station.</p>
        <p>A Mother Faints at Parting with Her</p>
        <p>Two Sons.</p>
        <p>Runcora V 73.9 in a state of great excitement last Satur- day, and Tuesday, the occasion being the departure of theo local members of the 2nd Earl Chester Volunteer Battalion, and Militia units of the 3rd Cheshire Regi- ment respectively. On both occasions large crowds of enthusiastic townspeople assembled to wish the men "God Speed," and shower upon them evidence of appre- ciation of their srlf-sacrifice.</p>
        <p>The Volunteers finally selected to represent the Runcorn Detachment were :—Lance-Sergeant F J Jeecock,Lance- Corporal E J Speakman, Private A C Timmins,Private L Mack,Frivate F Fl tat,FrivateD Chadwick. Though asked to report themselves early on the Saturday mnrning, it was ultimately decided that the men should go down to Chester by the 2A p.m. train so that as many of their comrades as could make it Convenient should be afforded the opportunity ot escorting them as a "guard of honour." Naturally information of such an idea spread biro r;ildfire, and from aboat 1.30 large crowds congre- gated along the route of march from the Drill Hall to the Railway Station, and soon the scene was a most impressive and enthusiastic one. Assembled at the Drill Hall, the Volunteers were lined up, with the chosen six immediately behind the band, and Captain Ashton addrcssing them said the two companies forming the Runcorn Detachment ef the 2nd Eart Chester Volunteers felt proud of being called upon to send six god men to represent them in South Africa. (Applause.) He hoped before leaving England, whilst at headquarters, those men would do their duty so thoroughly in every way as to gain good conduct marks from their Captain. As to how they would accomplish their duty in South Africa he weuld say nothing for ho felt they would all fight courageously and well. (Applause.) They did not expect each of them to obtain a "V.C. ' but if called to the front, todo their duty in the fighting line—though they did net expect six ** V..',"—they did hope there would he at last one Victoria Cross to come back to Runcorn when the war was over. (Loud applause.) The company then filed out of the Drill Hall in the following order :—Band ; The South African Contingent with rifles and accoutre- ments ; followed by the members of the conpany. The appearance of the Corr; in the open' air was the signal for an outburst of enthusiasm from the younger portion of the large cencouree assembled, and they valiantly assisted the band in rendering "Soldiers of the Queen.' On arrival at the station Captain Ashton dis- missed hi a men, and instantly they availed themselves of the opportunity of bidding a final farewell to their comrades. Collection/4 were made and gift, of all kinds shower, d up/ n thern,useful woollen caps,tobacco,rnoney, pipes, field glasses, and many othf-r useful articles being handed to them. And in those few minutes they under- went an experience as pleasing as unique.</p>
        <p>When the train elidei; into the statiia the band struck up "Auld Lang Syue " a tune they kept up till the de- parture, which was 'accompanied by a ringing cheer. The "Guard of lionour " then returned to the Drill Hall, and the band ciecluded its programme of music with" God Save the Queen.</p>
        <p>We understand that there is a movement on foot to get the:men down frnm headquarters for the purpose of giv- ing them a farewel: dinner, bofere their actual departure for the front. Such an idea is a most praiseworthy orp., and it Ist t 0..• 110131.4 tht our Iccal gentry will st” !tat the idea ie fittingly carried out. The officers on parade were :—CAptain, George F 10hron (in ce=mand) ;Liout., W Linaker. Quart.r- Master, W Hopkinson, L S. ; Sergeant Instructor Catiagh4n, Bandrnastrr, Outt3waite Colour-Serf:Pants Morr,and Harvey, S-rgeante. Woodland, L.S. ;Rushton, Bohnss, Edwards. Cooper, Matthews, Pncenis. Platt, and Lance Sergeant Edwards.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-01-26_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
        <head>Departure of the Militia.</head>
        <p>Departure of the Militia.</p>
        <p>Perhaps there is no branch of our National Defence, which is, more dezpised than that of our Miltia by those people who consider themselves a "cut above." But it was more than gratifying to see the" turn out" of such an enthusiastic crowd as occupied the streets on Toes• day, when the units of the 3rd Cheshire Regiment, who resided in Runcorn departed for their headquarters (Chester). True, there was not much of a band to lead them—but there was a band, and a decidedly loyal one, the St. Edward's Catholic Mission Band, and after all that was the great thine. The men were fine. hardy sons of toil ; straight, tall, wiry fellows ; Irish " every mother's son of them," and con- sciously proud of their nationality. And though we all cannot agree with the" wettiness " of their "send- off," all who were present on Tuesday, conferred that it was such men as those who supplied the finest metal which ever shone in England's battles, great and small. Ihe volunteers we packed off en Saturday were truly fine fellows, but the four men who entrained for Chester on Tuesday, whatever their faults were " foiner.” Their names and stations are as follows :—Private J. Hannon (sirgle), 2, Back Mersey-street, the sole support of his mother, aged 56 years. Private Patrick Lovelle (single), 11, Water-street, sole support of his mother, aged 50 years. Private William Burke (married). Bridgewater- street), wife and four children (aged 13, 10, 5 and 2) dependent upon him; Private James Sharkey (single), 4, Water-street, only support of his mother and little brother, aged 9 years. When the final partings were taken, some of them most pathetic the men entrained, and as the train (2 4 p.m.) steamed out eif the station Mrs. Sharkey fell down in a faint, the sheck of two of her sons departing for the front proving too much for her. After tieing at- tended to by the Police and Other ambulance men pre • sent the old lady recovered and was comfortingly escorted home by her many neighbours. Mrs. Sharkey'a other son Need his regiment from Ellesmere Pert,—The gifts to the men were not so numerous perhaps as to "our voluntetrs," but they were as hearty. It was decidedly pleasing to note that Mr. W. Shaw (of the llfirsey Micsion) was present as usual, and eeciaibited a eptrit that more professing evangelists might do well to copy. The cheers raised by the crowd as the train left the platform were deeidedly the best that Runcorn has raised ever the present war, and pns- eased the "British Ring."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-01-26_WidnesExaminer_art0127" n="art0127">
        <head>Frodsham's Contribution for The Front.</head>
        <p>Frodsham's Contribution for The Front.</p>
        <p>There was no mistaking the heartinees of the "send off " accorded the Frodsham Volunteers, selected for the front, and the scene in this most picturesque village last Saturday was one worthy and befitting the great occa- sion. The man were all privates of the "I " Company of tho 2nd Che3bire Volunteer Battalion, every one of them first-class rifle men, and they looked fit for any- thing when they teek their position with rifles and accoutrements in front of their less fortunate comrades en route for Frodsharn Station, headquarters, and the Cape. Their tame and stations in the village are well-known,but never have the names Evans. Carter, and Derlingten cot:email:lde groatnr respect than was ac- corded the representatives et those names last Saturday. They were truly the heroes of the hour, and when they bad departed the large crowd assembled seemed to realise that already the troubles of England were com- ing to an end. The men were escorted to the station leo their comrades of the "I" Company and the band, under the command of Major Harrison, and Lieutenant Vri E Burton. Arrived at the station the short time be- fore and during entrainment was taken up for expre.- pions of good feeling. tte men were liter:olr glowered with rreeents. A. the train moved nut, three hearty cheers formed a firting climax to a capital out- burst of enthueiesm.</p>
        <p>Meeting of Warrington Volunteers. A meeting of tho e.rn he rs of the lst V.B. South I,•9acashirso Regiment was held at the Drill Hall on Mon- day evening and was welt attended. Volunteers to form a reserve half co-rny' wAre asked for and a largP num- ber responeed. Colonel R:d.sewav- explained the pro. vig7ons which had been made for the wives and children of ,he Company who have been selected for aclve ser- vo, and said they would he very woll looked after,both rirritrentallr and by the Government and local funds. 11.-the conclusion et the meeting a collection was made en behalf of the war fund.</p>
        <p>Private R. by of Warrington. Killed, at Ladysmith.</p>
        <p>The above is taken from a p ntrait of Private R. Bibby of the Ist Manchester Regiment, who is re- ported killed during the desperate engagement at Ladysmith on January 6th. ite is the son of the late Mr. J. Bibby, of Warringron, 3nd is the first Warrington man to fall in the present war. His mother resides at 14, Eldon•street, and she feels the loss keenly. He was 21 years of age, and joined the Manchester Regiment about three years ago. Prior to the South African war the regiment was stationed at Gibraltar from whence it was sent to Ladysmith. The regiment has borne the brunt of the fighting right from the outset of the war and it is the more regrettable that he ahould meet with his death when the trials and 3ufferings of the men in be- leagured Ladysmith are, it is to be hoped, nearing the end.</p>
        <p>..,_ • ~e 1 (1111 /0' I - 1 I, 11.(1 V )/ , , I.p i l' . , i Bib' ' ' •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-01-26_WidnesExaminer_art0128" n="art0128">
        <head>South Lancashire Man at Estcourt.</head>
        <p>A South Lancashire Man at Estcourt.</p>
        <p>Private W . Farrington of the Ist South Lancashire Regiment writirg to his &amp;ster at Buckley-street, War. rington, front Estcourt, on December 28, says he is pleased to let them know that Iv has landed after a long journey of 23 thys. Whea he left Warrington people were sayir.g that the war would be nearly over by the time the Sloth Lancashires got there but on arrival to,l found the British no nearer the fiish than when they started. There was no doubt that their regiment was " fairly in for it "and there was a great deal of hard fighting before them. When they had relieved Lady- smith they would be smiling but it would cost a lot of lives. It was no use writing about the regiment's rnove - ments because those at home got the news before those in camp.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-01-26_WidnesExaminer_art0129" n="art0129">
        <head>Latchford Man Wounded.</head>
        <p>A Latchford Man Wounded.</p>
        <p>Mr. J. El . Chappell, of 7,Grosvenor-terrace, Wash-lane, has received a letter from his son, Corporal James Alex- ander Chappell,who was wounded in the thigh at Orange River on December 13',h. He says a rifle bullet caused a compound fracture, but the bullct was extracted after two operations. He is now progressing as favourably as possible, although 1:0 expects he will have to lie quiet in bed for two or three months before be is fit to get about Then he expects to be sent home to the Netley Hospital. He thinks he has had " quite enough of his share of the battle." He is very grateful for the kindness extended t 3 him, and adds" We get everything we wish fcr that adds to our comfort, as well as fruit, wines, &amp;c. On Christmas Day the ladies of Wynberg gave us a splendid tea, and waited on us at our bedsides."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-01-26_WidnesExaminer_art0130" n="art0130">
        <head>Warrington Royal Engineer and the Gifts to Soldiers.</head>
        <p>A Warrington Royal Engineer and the Gifts to Soldiers.</p>
        <p>The following is a copy of a letter weth ha q been re- ceived from Private Charles Smith (23,784, 28:h Com- pany Royal Engineers), who3o homeig 12, Welifield-st., Warrington :—lt is dated "Arundel, 27:h December. I am now in Arundel with the Cavalry Brigade under General French,3o miles south of the Orange Free States and 17 miles from Colesberg, the place where we expect to make our next assault. The Boers pay us a visit almost daily, but our Lancers and New Zealanders are much too smart for them and easily drivn them off. We are living very well, having fresh meat and bread every day. We have commar.deered 3,000 sheep from the enemy, so that it is mutton in galore,. I hope the gift</p>
        <p>of tobacco from Messrs. Crosfield's will come to us all ccrrect, as we have neither seen the Queen's chccolate, nor any of the 10,000 puddings. There are lots of other dainties toe numerous to mention referred to in the English newspapers, but we have received none of them yet. Pezhaps the Army authorities are waiting until the 10 h and 11th divisions come out and all the present divisions are dead. When I left for Arundel we had a very narrow escape, but the engine driver it:Ana:led to pull the train up in front of a mull! iron bridge which had been blown up by the Boers. We had to get out and sleep alon.q. the line till nexc morning."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-01-26_WidnesExaminer_art0131" n="art0131">
        <head>Warrington Policemen Engaged in</head>
        <p>Warrington Policemen Engaged in</p>
        <p>Four Battles.</p>
        <p>In the course of a letter to Mr. W. W. Whittle, P.C. Hurst, a member of the Warringou Police Force,who is with Lord Methuen's column, says :—" I was on out- post duty about one mile from the Boers, and they bad been firiog tor about four hours, but their shells went over the trenches, so you can tell what sort of a time we are having out here. W 6 have had four battles already, and we shall have a lot more yet. Our first was at Bel- mont, and the next at Graspan. The battle at Modder River was won with great losses on both sides, but the Boers lost more than we did. The fourth battle was at Magersfont_in. We were fighting from 2.30 a.m. on the •Menday, until sunset next day, but we could not get them out of the hills, for there were too many for us—two to one. However, they will have to corn. out before long. We are only awaiting reinforcements, and then we shall advance to Kimberley and if we get through I don't think we shall go any further. You can tell everyone that Heward (another member of the Warrington Police Force) and myself are not dead or wounded yet, but alive and kick- ing, and live in hopes of being in Warrington next sucerner,and the war all over and settled. We are getting tired of this country, for we have not been undressed since we have been out, with the exception of when we ge to the dirty river,which we have to drink. You could cut it with a knife. We have got one blanket to sleep in at night, and when we wake up we are half starved with the cold,or the rain, for it is very hot during the day and very cold et night. We cannot get any news,and you practically know more than we de." Hurst con- cludes by wishing to be remembered to all the members of the Police Force.</p>
        <p>The Slaughtered Connaughts. Buller's Brows Puckered up.</p>
        <p>Sergeant H. Holt, of the King's Rlyal Rifles,who was in the battle of Colenso, has sent the following letter to his parents in St. Austin's-lane, Warrington :—I am safe and sound after the bard-fought battle of Coleaso,where we lost 1,150 killed and woonded, and would have lost a groat many more if their shells had burst, for we had over 40 droppel among us in the first four hours. Vs only lost one man in our battalion ,and he was blown almost in half. The Scottish, Dublins, and Connaught Rangers were almost all slaughtered, especially the Con- naughts,who formed our left flank guard. Br some over- sight they were left unprotected by artillery, and the Boers managed to get a Maxim gun trained upon them. Instead of the Colonel retiring he went forward at a run and got within 300 yards of the position. The Tugela stopped further progress, and the men were shot down like dogs. Out of 1,200 officers, nan-commissioned officers, and men who went like lambs to the slaughter, only 410 returned, but we gave the Dutch devils "beans," killing according to the estimate 2 500, though their loss most have been greater, for they were burying them in hundreds,and the trenches were filled with dead. It was a sickening sight. Old Butler sat on his horse with his brows puckered up, ordering by a wave of his hand the different positions our brigades had to take up. He manoeuvred them very well. We were fighting for nine and a half hours under a burning sun, when the firino suddenly ceased and our general retired to our former camping ground, and then marched by night to Frere camp.</p>
        <p>A Burtonwood Man at Colenso. "Fell Like the Leaves of a Tree."</p>
        <p>Bin Henry Taylor, Police Court Mnsinnary. and In- quiry Officer to the Transvaal Relief Committee has reeeived the following letter from Private No. 2076, W. Baker, "E" Company, Royal Welsh Fusiliers who is serv- ing with General Bullet's forces in South Africa and pre- viously resided at Burtenwood .—Colenso, 27. 12, 99, A happy New Year to von all. Dear friend, just a few words to you in reply to your mcst kind letter which I received from you. I must say that I was very p4eased to hear that you were doing your bast for my wife and family. I was very uneasy abeut them and how they were going to live in my absencs but lam pleased to say that I feel more easy new that I know that your kind committee is doing what they can for them. I am very pleased to tell you that I am in the best of health at present which I must thank God for as we are having some very hard times of it out here. We are very short of water and out here the heat is terrible, but we are not having much fight- ing to do at the present time. I have been in two battles up to the prreent, the last one being at the place that lam now stationed at called Coins°. I damey you saw in the papers about our side losing 1,047 be- tween killed, wounded and prisoners and I must state that it was awful to see our dear comrades fall in the way that they did as the Boers wore hid in the hills so that we could not cos them until it was too late. When our men got close to thrm they fired on them and RS our men advanced up to them they fell like the leaves of a tree. I hope and trust that it won't last much longer so that I may COMA home and see you and Inv dear borne and family. Hoping your workings will e I</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-01-26_WidnesExaminer_art0132" n="art0132">
        <head>EXAMINER, FRIDAY, JANUARY 26, 1900.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, FRIDAY, JANUARY 26, 1900.</p>
        <p>rewarded in times to come. Believe me, dear sir, yours faithfully, W. Baker, one of the British serving in South Africa.</p>
        <p>A "Fighting Son" at the Front. The flowing letter wt 3 recei7ed by Mr. snd Mrs. Wiiliana Beasley, Princes-street, Runcorn, from their sou Lance-Corporal Caulfield : Honey Nest Kloof, • 1212,-99</p>
        <p>Dear Mother and Father,—f write these few lines to you wishing you are all in good health I am sorry to tell you that this will be the last letter I can I send you for a while, for we are in a place where we cannot get any paper. and God only knows when we will i get out of it—sues dismal holes you never did see in all your life. It is called Honey Nest Kloef. It is not far from the free. We have been fighting for the last two days, and had great losses on our side. The Black Watch were all cut up. The Boers are in their " last hills" and have got all their guns up. There are 20,000 Boers, aid our men can't get at them at all. Our Artillery are firing lyddite shells on thorn, and are deinr great work. We have the, Beers penned in without water or food—which they ;an% stand lnng. They have been without these things for two days. but still keep up fire, and are killing and wounding our men. The General sent a dispatch to them asking them to surrender. They are fighting madly for water, which they will not get until they give No doubt, by this gets home Kimberley will be relieved. The column here is 21,000 strong, and the Kimberley garrison are thrust- ing the Boers on the column. Australian and Canadian troops arrived here this week, and are " mid ' to get at the Boers, but I told one or two not to be in such a hurry to get at them. So no more at present from your fighting son Peter. I think I will be eels to show the other red-coat soldiers up They say a man " 'lists" because be is tired of work, but they would not say that if they were here. We do more work in one day than a workingman does in a week. . . . The letter concludes with the remark that Lance-Corporal Caulfield is not sure of his address as they are moved so often, but that Honey Nest Kloof, South Africa, or elsewhere will find him."</p>
        <p>Letter from one of Messrs. Rylands Bros'. Workmen. Cruelty of the Boers, The following is a copy of a letter which has b!en re- ceived from Sergeant D. Hough'em, 3,634 (of the Ist South Lencashire Regiment, sth division), who wae etn. ployed by Messrs Rylands 'Bros., Church-street. It is addressed to Mr. S. Baldry :—" Estcourt, Natal, 27-12-99, Dear Sam,—Just a few lines to let you know we have got to Capetown on the 20% of this month after a very good voyage, but were not allowed to land. We were sent on to Durban and got there on the 23rd. We marched up csuntry and made Est-court on Clarief one night, and got a fine reception. Outposts as soon as we got in, and a fine ducking we got. It rained, thundf red and lightened all night, and it has done so every night since we got into the country. I am very sorry to let you know wo are losing a lot of men up to now. The Boers have splendid positions in the hills round Coleus°, and they are ploughing our men down like chaff with a gun called the Maxim, and we can't find out where it is. I ans pleased to let you know that they are getting tired of fighting. an we had 1,005 gave themselves up to us. They say they c in't get anything to eat or drink, and we have another 2,500 hemmed in close to no, simply living on their horses, and as we can't get them out we shell them every morning from five te eight for their breakfast, and same in the evening for dinner and tea, EO I eon't think they will last long. lam very pleased to let you know our artillery blew the Colenso bridge up yester.lay, and to-day the Boers were crossing with a convoy, and as soon as they got in the middle of the river the Naval Brigade opened fire, making every shot tell, and blew the convoy to bits. We lost ten guns and three companies of the Dublin Fusiliers last Friday. They are net much better than a lot of savages (the Boers). They are stripping the dead of their clothes and cutting their fingers off if they have any rings on. It is pitiful to see the csrpses brought in naked, frizzled up with the sun. They (the Boers) have robbed the natives of all they had and they are knocking abaut without a mouthful to eat. We have got Sir Charles Warren in command of the Lancashire Brigade, and we are going to make a bold bid for Coleus° next Friday, 29th. lam in the best of health." How the Soldiers wash at Modder River.—A Comical 'Sight. Private W. Langley, of the_3rd__Grenadier Guards, in</p>
        <p>a letter to his unPle -Consteble Wilson, of the Runcorn Division' describes the hardship; endured at Modder River. He says : " As I am writing this the Boers are shelling us . . . . My Christmas Day's diet consis- ted of: Breakfast : A piece of dry bread and coffee, very little dinner, and tea same as breakfast. I slept those nights (whilst on out-post duty) with the earth for my bed, 150 rounds of ammunition for my pillow, and my rifle for my bed chum. Give my love to little Harry and Dorie and tell them I will bring them a piece of Kruger's whiskers . . . . You might send me a few cigarette;, I often crave for a smoke and cannot get one. We don't often get a wash, we wait a few days until the dirt gets hard,then we laugh, and it cracks and drops off. Writing to his mother and father from Modder River, 22nd December, 1899, Private Langley says :—"I write these few lines to you from the battlefield here, to say that I am all right up to the present. I am on outpo3t duty to-day, and I am writing this letter on my mess tin,with the ground for my chair. There are some exciting scenes here. We were shelled by the enemy this morning soon after breakfast. The first shell come unpleasantly close but they did no damage. Oar "long torn "soon silenced them. . . . I have made my will out for you mother, but I hopa there will be no need for that, and with God's help I shill pull through. Give my love to my father, and to Sallie, Sam and Bessie ; You must remember mother that if I don't come back, I shall die doing my duty. There has been a few heavy losses in our Battalion at Balmont,Graspan, and Madder River. Our battalion and the Yorks. were escort for the artillery and big guns last Friday morn- ing. We started from camp at 2.30, and poured shot and shell into them for about an hoar, but as the enemy did not respond to our fire we retired to camp. (Langley then request; that tbis letter might be shown to his friends as he cannot get paper to write to them, having had to give 61 for the single sheet he could ob- tain). . . . " You would have laughed if you could have seen us the other day. We have only one suit of khaki up here, the other suits being at Cape Town, and the battalion had orders to wash their suits in the Modder River ; after we washed them, we had to sit on the banks of the river and wait for them to dry. It did look a comical sight all with blankets on like a kilted regi- Jaunt." The letter concludes with affectionate remem- brances. The Secret of The Boers' SUccessl Interesting Letter From a Cape Mounted Rifleman. The following letter, which is from Mr. J. W. Outh- waite, (nephew of Mr. Oathwaite, bandmaster, Run- corn), who has been 19 years in South Africa is particu- larly interesting, as after so lone a residence at Kokstad his opinions are worthy of respect. The letter is a copy of one witten to his parents : Kokstad. December 15th, 1899, , Dear Mother and Father.—Although I have scarcely a moment, I can call my own, I determined to send you a letter, by this mail, no matter what oecurred. I write this in the camp of the Cape Mounted Rifise,where I am doing duty as a Velunteer. You will wonder at this, after what I said in my last letter, but the on- ward march of the troops has brn checked in a manner beyond all our expectations; and even our borders have been threatened by the ignorant Boer marauder 3. But before going further t must explain how it comes about that I am doing duty In the C.M.R- camp. At the end of last month when the Boers were at work invading the Colony border, coming in from the Free State side, information was brought in and wired from Metebele and other plac, s near the East Griqualand border, that parties of Boers had been seen jult the other side of the mountain (Drakensburg) which forms the bor-er. This natur- ally made the people in these districts clamour for the C.M.R. and East Grivaland Mounted Rifles being put on the border to protect the passes through the mountain. Civilians volunteer d (that is members of the East Griqualand Mounted Rifles, of which I am a member), anti t:o3p. of C.M.R. and E.G.M..R. were gent out within three days. This left Keksted and the C.M.R. camp unprotected and my troop, No. 23, at once volunteered to provide men for guard in the camp itself, which is eituete about a quarter of a mile out of town, on it Enorth side. The reason for this step was that there are cannon and rifles, ammunition and other ordnance stores to the value of about £90,000 which we are not inclined to see fall into the hand; of the Boers to be used egainst loyal colonise;. Acording to the news you will 3ee in the Kokstad Advertiser of to-day's date. under the heading of the 3 u t Fetcher news, it would appear that our prompt action prevented this territory from being invaded. The nearest point from K Astad to the border is about 50 mites. Just fancy a lot of peaceful loyal citizens having to turn out to protect their homes from destruction by a lot of discontented, ignorant robbers ; who are so cunning and viedictive that they would deceive their own mothers if it suited their selfish purpose. Here am I, in this important month of December, so busy with Iry office work that I scarcely know how to turn, and yet I molt turn out daily for drill in the camp, and take my turn for 24 hours at a stretch for, guard duty, carrying a bandolier filled with 50 cartridges and my rifle in a blezine sun (during the day time) which has been at time; 100 degrees in the Shade. Whew ! How it peels one's hands, face and neck in the Fun. We have had no rain for 9 months—a seriouo state of affairs, lean secure you. Some people think it is the cannonading which is going on in various carts of the country which draws the rain from here. You can imagine that I feel pretty well knocked up, what with my outside duties, office work, and the camp guard. There are a number</p>
        <p>of Dutch people up here, and it is not at all pleasant to know for a positive fact that amongst them are a num- ber of rebels who would like nothing better than to put a bullet through a lot of us Britishers—that feeling 13 reciprocated to the full by one and all of us. I would put a bullet through the treacherous brutes with as little compunction as I kill the vermin in my garden. Let them have a few successes and we should find things hum in our little corner of the colony. This we do not for a moment anticipate, although the Beers are at present holding their own fairly well in some parts. 11380 ride into Kokstad daily for news, as we have a daily service of telegrams here, for which we pay 103 each per month co the Elitor of the "Advertiser.' Do not be at all uneasy about me. We do not anticipate an invasion, as our volunteers are joining the b)zder troops daily ; and I think ere long the Boers will have their hands full in their efforts to check the ouwtrd march of the troops to the Free State and Transvaal. One thing I must warn you against, and that is taking any notice of the " Diggers News " reports. That rag belongs to the Transvaal Government, hence the con- tinued lies which appear about the small number of men they lose, etc. The fact is the Transvaal Government dare not let it be known how many men they lose. The men would desert in thousands if they knew the true state of affairs as regards losses. Therefore the policy of the" Diggers News" is to buoy the men up with glowing accounts of victeries, and as long as they can do this the po ,r ignorant Boers will fight. The secrst of their present stand against our army is that they have hundreds of good officers from Europe who plan out the best positions on the lines of route, always choosing al- most inaccessible mountains, well knowing that the British will follow and endeavour to force them out,thus giving the Boers the opportunity they so delight in of cutting the soldiers down whilst crossing the flats to reach the enemy who are snug and safe behind large rocks and ridges. This is the reason so many valuable liv,s are lost, and will continue to be lost as the army moves forward.—J. W. Ovrnwairn.</p>
        <p>EPT`' lire crowd had assembled, and aftor detraining the oeta.chinent, was led to the barracks by toe bugle nd of the depo. During their progress for the short distance from the siding to the barracks they were greAeU with enthusiastic cheers. On their arrival at the barracks they were inspected, and subsequently were dismissed. 'fht-:ir quarters during their stay at the bar- racks wiil be in the Princiss block.</p>
        <p>Volunteers "Send ofP' at Earlestown.</p>
        <p>On Saturday evening the shop-mates of those mem- bers of the let South Lancashire Volunteers who were employed at tee Viaduct Works, and who have offered their services for the front, Viz: Privates Singleton and Thoth, took oceason to give them a hearty send off. In honour of the occasian they organised a knife and fork tea which took place at the Viaduct Hotel, Earlastown. The smiths: also invited seven other local Volunteers who are also going out to South Africa, and altogether about 50 persons partook of the excellent meal provided by Host Tunstall. The names of the other Volunteers are P.ivates Bevan, Moors, Dean, Sharcross, and Houghtou. After tea a pleasant social evening was spent, Captain Dr. Patterson beine present. Several patriotic speeches were made, interspersed with songs, and the merriment ran high. On behalf of the bolt shop, Mr J Francis pre- sented Private Sing:etoa with a silver mounted pips and case, silver matchbox, and half a pound of tobacco, and on behalf of the smithy, Captain Patterson pre- welted Private Booth with half a pound of cigarettes, silver mounted cigarette holder, cigarette case, silver match box, silk muffler, and box of stationery. The other seven Volunteers were each presented by Mrs Tunstall with a pair of home knitted soeks,and a packet of stationery. Later on the Volunteers were accom- panied to the station,and they by .ral :or Warring ton amid the cheers and hearty good wishes of their friends.</p>
        <p>The Rer. E. R. Tarbuck and the War-</p>
        <p>rington Volunteer Contingent.</p>
        <p>Alluding to the war in his sermon on Sunday evening at St. Paul's Church the Vicar (the Rev. E. R. Tarbuck) said they were just now passing through a very severe trial, a trial so severe that the French writers expressed their astonishment at the coolness exhibited in the daily life of the English people. They confessed qui'e openly that had the Preece nation received such reverses to her arms as we have received the Government would have been forced by the people to make a sacrifice after each reverse of some scapegoat, and that in all probability there would have been a revdluticin. But the English were made of sterner stuff than that. The English char- acter was the result, he believed, more or less, of a steady progress during the last 1,000 years, and he felt proud that just now we were not found wanting in patience, faith and grit. In Warrington there was not as yet a case of a family robbed of father, brothee or husband, lover or son through this war. Within tha next few months, however, many families might be robbed. Daring the past week the character of events had changed considerably. They lied all teen very anxious, and had carefully watched every item of news from headquarters, but there was now a possi- bility of the war being brought to an end, and it was satisfactory to kno es that one of our generals who had sustained a reverse had been permitted by God to turn that tide of misfortune. )we ' not speakieg politically, at any rate not party pelitieelly, but the people would have the power in their trends later on to criticizs, and be hoped fully as well as fairy, but as patriots they could thank Goi most heartily for the turning tide, and as non-combatants pray that the issue of the war mieht be a right one. Perhaps they would think it cruel of him to say so, but the coat of the war would be more than repaid to the nation by the fact that there had been a necessity to call out the Voluntei?rs and Yeomanry. They had an immense power behind them in the volunteer branches of the army, but it had only been during the past week or twe that that power had been eecognized by the nation They would send from Warrington a very fine half company of men—some of their best men—and it would be shown they were some of the best by the !act that there would be no disgraceful offering of drink to the citizen soldiers as they wished them farewell. It was well know that the English volunteer would not lack courage and that with regard to drill he would take his place as the equal in every respect of the regular soldier. Be felt very grateful to God that the opportun- ity which 110S7 presented itself had given proof to the world at large what we had behind our regular army end navy—volunteers that they need not be ashamed of. I'hey hoped the war would soon be brought to an enl with as little mischief as possible.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-01-26_WidnesExaminer_art0133" n="art0133">
        <head>Tugela Battle—Account of a Runcorn Eyewitness.</head>
        <p>The Tugela Battle—Account of a Runcorn Eyewitness.</p>
        <p>Th, following is an extract from a letter to Mr. T. Cooke from Mr. J. Smith:—</p>
        <p>"December ].4th.—At four o'clock this marling every- body in the camp was astir and by half-past nine a.m. the whole column had moved out of camp. A train of mules and bullock waggons miles in length, cavalry, ar- tillery, mounted infantry, and infantry in variees for- mations, all moving out in such precision was a sight which will always live in my memory. After all had cleared out (except a few hundred Scottish Fusiliers to keep the way of communication open) evereone became eager and enpectant, and on Friday morning the 15:h about 5.30 the loud booming of heavy guns proclaimed the commencement of battle. The ernes continued to shell the enemy's no3itioa,until about 11.30 without intermission. After which some of tbe artillery and infantry got into action with terrible reeults. In a very short time about 1,100 of our men had been put out of action—either killed or wounded. It anpear3 that during the bombardment of the Dutch pesitien not a single Boer was to be seen. It was only when our men, who by some unaccountable blunder were ordered to re- tire, that the awful carnage commenced. Such a storm of shell and bullets that had never before been experienced in any campaign was poured into our men from every conceivable place. So hot was the fire that ten of our guns had to be abandoned. I suppose such valour and heroism was never displayed before, but all to no purpose. Lord Roberts' son—Lieutenant Roberts—was killed wiles', attempting to brine in one of the guns. Some of oir men had got within 300 yards of the enemy's trenches, and some had even erossed the deep river, and bad got up to a few yards of the enemy when they were ordered to retire. The men all say if they had been allowed to go on and were properly supported, they could have taken the position and so turned a terrible defeat into a brilliant victory. Of course, you will read all particu- lars in the papers of the battle and its results, but I can assure you that some of the brave fellows cried with vexation beeause they were led into such a trap, and then recalled at such a critical juncture. They say they have been badly officered. Whatever may have been the cause of the disaster the sacrifice has been terrible, an,' the advantage gained nil. The Dutch say they had 2 killed and 4 wounded. But other reports from doctors and several why have come in from the enemy say that the 1053 inlkilled atom° must be at least 2,000, as some of the trenches were heaped up with dead, and they (the Boers) asked for 24 hours armistice in which to bury their dead. The first train load of our wounded came in the station here on Friday alterneon,aad they continued _ _ _ _</p>
        <p>to come in load after load until Sunday afternoon. It appears that the Boers fired on the ambu!ance men when they were gathering up the wounded, and several of the volunteers got killed. Many of our men had to be out on the battlefield all night, as they could dot be brought in owing to the fire of the enemy, who seem to pee- scant respect to the Red Cross., Jack, and I were at the station when the various train loads of wounded came in, and were engaged like many others fetching bottles of water for the poor fellows, for which they seemee very grateful. To all of them it seemed like nectar ! Of course, all that were sent up from the front on their way to Maritzburgh and Durban had been attended to by the doctors, and bandaged up as well as time and circum- stances would allow. Yet, after all the doctors could do for them it was a terrible sight to see 600 men ail bandaged up and more or less knocked to pieces in various ways. I will not try to describe them it is toe painful, I can, however, safely say that a braver let of men never went en a battlefield,for,from all the 600 men that stopped at the station not a single murmur was heard. Their only regret was—and that was very keen —that they had not been able to take the enemy's position. On Friday mnrning about 9 30, when the bombardment had been incessant for four hours, Jack and I got permission to go to a high Kopje, labout three miles from camp and watch tee battle. We had not gone more than a couple It miles ere a mounted trooper came galloping up and demanded our pass. We told him we had not got one, but after a short parley and explanation he said we might go to the first hill, and no further or we might get into trouble. When we bad gained the top we found that there was another hill further on from which we could obtain a better view, and on the top of which we could see a few mounted men. So we decided to cross the valley and chance being sent back. We did so and on reaching the top saw they were five mounted officers, and each had a splendid field glass which they very kindly offered us and never asked us a question. We remained on the hill top for one and a half hours—just whilst the battle was at 49 fierceet. Through the glasses we could see the shells bursting all over the valley, and on the sides and front of the hills. In some places where they dropped nothing but a cloud of Mot could be seen. In other caspa where some of our lyddite shells had buret, a terrific explosion occurred followed by an upheaval of rock and earth, accompanied by a bluish flame and smoke,but these lyddite shells as a rule were so far away that we could not see the actual result. In other case. we could see a clump of men and horses when a shell would come and burst amongst them and when the dug, and smoke bad cleared away, nothing but a heap of debris seemed to remain. The infantry and artillery who made tee attack were too far down the valley for us to see distinctly what was taking place, but to us it appeared to be a mass of confusion. We looked on the traerde being enacted below with an eagerness we could not describe. We were only sorry we could no get another mile nearer the fighting line."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-01-26_WidnesExaminer_art0134" n="art0134">
        <head>3rd South Lancashires Detained at Gibraltar.</head>
        <p>The 3rd South Lancashires Detained at Gibraltar.</p>
        <p>The transport City of Rome which has on board the 3rd Suuth Lanes4hira (Militia) R.giment, has arrived at Gibraltar front Queenxtnwn, and landed the 32nd Corn- Panv of the Southern Division Roy&amp; Garrison Artillery for duty. She has embarked the 2nd Cornoanv of th. Southern Divipion under the commtnd of Major Powle3, and awaits the arrival of the sth Company of th, East( rn Division from Malta, when sbe will sail for the Cape with both companies.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-01-26_WidnesExaminer_art0135" n="art0135">
        <head>3rd Kings Liverpool (Militia) Regiment.</head>
        <p>3rd Kings Liverpool (Militia) Regiment.</p>
        <p>On Tuesday afternoon five hundrel men belonging to the 3rd Kin ga (Liverpool) Militia Regiment were em- bodied at Oiford Barrack, Warrington. They were quickly equipped and departed the same afternoon for Preston to talv.e up garriaon duty. Very few of t'ie men failed to put in an appearance but the conditioes of some as they presented thernrelves at the Barracks' gate was not entirely creditable. The officers in charge of the regiment are the following :—Colocel J. M. Batten,Majnr C. E. Terri., Capt Ans itinney, Bedinfirld, filleted ; Lieutenants Chaffer, Wilmer, Whittle, and Gape ; 2nd Lieutenant Robinson, Withsrington, Wheeler, Hay ; Captlins B roes and FiUtFOn ; Captain and Adjutant Hartley ; Lieut. and I. M. 0. ; attachad-officers, Capzains °ram, and Walker, and Lieutenant Fritche. A large crowd witnessed the departure of the men,and at several points en route from the Barracks to the Station hearty cheers were raised.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-01-26_WidnesExaminer_art0136" n="art0136">
        <head>Volunteers.</head>
        <p>The Volunteers.</p>
        <p>Arrival of the sth Liverpool Irish Brigade.</p>
        <p>Amid Acnnes of unprecedented enthusiasm. the Special Service Company ot the sth LiverpoA Irish Brigade left Liverpool for Warrington on Mondsy afternoon. The battalion to the number of 400, paraded at headquarters in Everton-brow,and proceeded to S. George's Hall, fol- lowed Atli preceded by an immense concourse of specta- tors: The Service Company were dressed in khaki uni- form with their great costs. At st- Geor:7,e'e Hall, in tho pre3enoe of a large and brilliant assembly, the swearing- in ceremony was completed by the Lord Mayor, grear, enthusiasm prevailing. The Special Service men left Liverpool by specie' 'train for Warring- ton about two ()Volt, amid ringing cfpers. The company, which numbered 116, arrivod at Warzing- ton at a quarter to three, and wro to the siding at the waggon-works at Orfor2-lane pear the fisrreck3.</p>
      </div>
