      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-01-26_WidnesExaminer_art0138" n="art0138">
        <head>Another Warrington Policeman for</head>
        <p>Another Warrington Policeman for</p>
        <p>The Front.</p>
        <p>P.C. Muskett of the Warrington Police Force, who is a first class Reservist of the Royal Artillery, received his " papers "on Saturday to rejoin his Regiment to-day, (27:,h inst.)</p>
        <p>"Send-off" to P.C. Bradley. '</p>
        <p>Several friends of P. C. Bradley, who is about to re- join his regiment and proceed to the front, accorded him a very loyal send-off on Monday evening at the Ring o' Bells Inn. Witderspool-road. A supper was partaken of ebeut 30 persons, after which a smoking concert was held, and presentations of an address,writing materials, tobacco, ice., were made by Mr. Joseph Holloday, who, in doing s.), said it afforded them one and all great pleasure to be present that evening. At the same time they felt mime paugi of regret and remorse at having to part with such a worthy friend as Mr. Bradley. How- ever, his loyalty and patriotism in joining the colours and going to the front somew4at mollified these feelings of regret. They assured him at his sociability,urbanity and straightforwardness had endeared to Lim many kind hearts and loving friends whose good wishes would accompany him to every part of the world. Without unduly flattering him,he might say be had been a credit to the Police Force of Warrington and they firmly be- lieved that if England had had a few more men at the front of the same stamina and grit the conflict weuld have been brought to an issue long ago. He hoped to have for many years to come the pleasure of his genial company at the close of tho war.—P.C. Bradley briefly tkanked those present for their kindness.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-01-26_WidnesExaminer_art0139" n="art0139">
        <head>Help for those Left Behind.</head>
        <p>Help for those Left Behind.</p>
        <p>Juvenile Entertainment at Stockton Heath.</p>
        <p>A juvenile entertainment, arranged by Miss Ethel Dun, for the purp )se of raising a contribution to the Mayor's Transvaal Fund,was held at Mr. England's house Stockton fleath,on Friday last week. The juveniles and their adult friends, to the number of 64, entered heartily in the entertainment provided for them. A lantern ex- hibition was the first item on the programme, being fol- lowed afterwards by a dramatje sketch," The Sleeping Beauty." This was well retdered, in character and costume, by the following juveniles :—Master Richard Co3telloe. king ; Miss Pattie Woodcock, queen ; Miss Marian England, prince and wicked fairy; Miss Mary Bennie, nurse ; Miss Frances Corney, sleeping beauty ; Miss Ruby Costellce, Miss B?lEle Lee, and Miss Mary Caldwell, grad fairies. Miss Pattie Woodcock recited a portion of " The Absent-minded Beggar," following which the fairies with their magic wands and baskets skipped about and gathered in 17a in aid of the fund. The next item was a humorous dialogue, entitled " One or Two," given by Mrs. England and Miss Bertha Bouchier, which afforded considerable amusement. A vote of thanks was accorded to all those who had con- tributed to the evening's enjoyment, Miss Ethel Dun being specially sinelei out and given credit to for the able manner in which she had bad such a capital pro- gramme carried out.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-01-26_WidnesExaminer_art0140" n="art0140">
        <head>Wanted to go to the Front.</head>
        <p>Wanted to go to the Front.</p>
        <p>At the Borough Police Court on Monday, before Mr. F. Monks and other magistrates, John Cusick, 27, Hall- srPet, was charged with deserting from the 2nd Battalion Marchcstn Regiment, stationed at the Royal Barracks, Dublin, on the 19:.1a Dee.mber, 1899.—P.C. Monks said that at 9 o'clock on Saturday night he was on duty in Marshhouse-lane when be was called to the Barracks to receive the prisoner into custcdy. He had given himself up. The Chairman: He wants to go to the front does he ?—Tht, Chief Constable: I don't know, sir. He belongs to the Man- chester Regiment which is at the front at the present time —Prisoner said he was a reservist, and thought his regiment nad gone to the front. He had hearo, how- ever, that another portion of it was being embodied at Aldershot, so he gave himself up in order that he might rekir to go to the front. He was remanded to await an escort.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-01-26_WidnesExaminer_art0141" n="art0141">
        <head>Local Firm's Order for Jam.</head>
        <p>A Local Firm's Order for Jam.</p>
        <p>Meq•srsi. Kydd and Kydd Limited, preserve manufac turors, Frodsharn, have been succe!sful in securing a large order from the War Office, consisting of apwards of a quarter of a millon pounds of preserves of various kinds for transport to the soldiers in South A.frlca. The orefer is to b completed in nine weeks, at the rate of 30,0001b5, pr week, which will of necessity entail tt e taking on of irsarly one hundred additional hands.</p>
        <p>Presentation and Send-off to a Co- operative Employee. _</p>
        <p>_ On Tuesday there was a social gathering of the War- rington C.),:perative employes when a presentatioin was made to Mr. Max Nixdorf,one of:the employes,on his leav- ing for the seat of war. in excellent knife and fork tea had been provided after which an entertainment was held, and songs of a patriotic character were ably rendered by several of the employes. The chair was occupied by one of the committee, Mr. Jos.,ph Albinsop. who in introduc- ing Mr Crighton, the senior baker, to make the preNentation to Mr Max Nixdorf, referred to the present as being handsome and very suitable, which consisted of three pipes, 21bs, of tobacco, Balaclava cap, khaki jemv, socks, writing cage and purse of mency.—Mr. Crighton, in making the presentation. spoke of the pli?.asor...l i gave him to see so many of his feillw workers at such a ,s,rat.liering and briefly referred :to the p:easure it gave him to make the present, and also his regret, through parting with a rood workman. He ale° spoke. athe kindreli v;:th which he had treated him and</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-01-26_WidnesExaminer_art0142" n="art0142">
        <head>EARLESTOWN &amp; NEWTON.</head>
        <p>EARLESTOWN &amp; NEWTON.</p>
        <p>On Sunday last (the 21st) sermons were preached in aid of the funds of the Diocesan Church of England Temperance Society by the Secretary (the Rev. J. Hirst), in the morning and afternoon at St. Mary's Church, Lowton, and in the evening at War- grave Church, Earlestown.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-01-26_WidnesExaminer_art0143" n="art0143">
        <head>P. S. A .</head>
        <p>P. S. A .</p>
        <p>The weekly meeting of -the Earlestown and Dis- trict P.S.A. Society, was held in the Co-operative Hall, Earlestown on Sundtry,when there was a large attendance. Mr G Clarke presided, and a stirriug address was given by the Rev F E Miller. Recita- tions were given by Mr J Astill, and vocal items by Miss E Sirett. Mrs Latimer presided at the piano.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-01-26_WidnesExaminer_art0144" n="art0144">
        <head>GOOD TENCPLARY.</head>
        <p>GOOD TENCPLARY.</p>
        <p>The Hope ot Earlestown Lodge weekly meeting was held in the Co-operative Hall, Wargrave-road, on Monday evening,Bro Cutler C.T., presiding. After the opening services, the secretary read the notice of district council to be held in Liverpool, and also the recommendations for L.D., E.S., S.J.T., A.T.J.T. Three candidates were then introduced for initia- tion. Bro Woods, L.D., read the G.C.T. circular also a letter from Bro Lee, D.C.T. which were referred to the business committee. It was resolved to ask the district council to hold its next session in Earles- town. The programme was "A night with the Penny Illustrated Magazine." iteadings were given by Bros Clarke, Woods, Williams, Newman, Leather- barrow, and Sister Warburton.</p>
        <p>TENNIS BALL AT NEWTON</p>
        <p>On Friday evening the annual ball in connection with the Newton-le-Willows Tennis Club took place in the Town Hall, Newton, and was a most success- ful affair. The hall presented a very cheerful and tasteful appearance, the decorations having been carried out by Mr E. T. Evans with the assistance of several lady members of the club. There were about 40 couples present,and dancing was spiritedly carried on to music supplied by Mr. Pearson's (Wigan) band. Messrs. H. Hibbert and J. Bowyer officiated as M.C's. The refreshments were under the charge of Miss King, and the dancing was con- tinued until 4 o'clock on Saturday morning.</p>
        <p>ROBBERY FROM THE EARLESTOWN CO-OPERATIVE SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>At eight o'clock on Wednesday morning, when the manager of the Crow-lane branch of the Earles- town Co-operative Society opened the shop, he found that the premises had been forcibly entered, and provisions to the value of about .E 4 abstracted. The premises were left secure the previous night, and P.C. Clarke in whose district the shop is, states that everything was in order when he passed at half past twelve. It was found that an entry had been made by breaking a window at the rear, and forcing back the catch. The matter is in the bands of the police, but at present there is no clue to the thieves. This is the second time the shop has been broken into, the previous occasion being in August last year.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-01-26_WidnesExaminer_art0145" n="art0145">
        <head>ST. HELENS.</head>
        <p>ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>YOUTHFUL COAL STEALERS.</p>
        <p>At St. Helens, on Saturday, before Alderman J. C. Gamble and Councillor Leach, seven lelys and girls were charged with stealing 2 cwts. of coal, value is 6d, from a waggon at the sidings of Messrs. Pilkington's Plate Glace Works, and were each fined 53.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-01-26_WidnesExaminer_art0146" n="art0146">
        <head>ACCIDENT TO A ST. HELENS COLLIER</head>
        <p>ACCIDENT TO A ST. HELENS COLLIER</p>
        <p>Michael M'Cormick, aged fortv-four, Parr-street, St. Helens, was on SAturea7 morninz admitted to tbe Providence Hospital, suffering from injuries to the foot and head, caused by a fall of rod f at the Bold Colliery, where he was working.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-01-26_WidnesExaminer_art0147" n="art0147">
        <head>STRANGE SUICIDE AT ST. HELENS.</head>
        <p>STRANGE SUICIDE AT ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>On Saturday night a man wag seen to jansp into the canal near the Pocket Nook Bridge, St. Helens. Mary Begley, a ycung weman, witnessed the occurrence, and gave the alarm. Constable Whalley proceeded to the spot, but the man had sunk. The body was recovered, and the man identified as John Macderrnott, a Bolton weaver, who had lodged three nights at 13, Atlas-st.</p>
        <p>STRANGE DEATH OF A CHILD.</p>
        <p>Mr. S. Brighouse, county coroner, held an inquest at the Engine Inn, Parr, St. Helenq, on Tuesgay, on the body of Margaret Ann Lee, the four months old daughter of Richard Lee, a collier, of 285. Derbyshire, Bill-road, who was found dead on the bedroom floor on Sunday morning. Evidence of identification having been given by the mother of deceased, Dr. Bates said that he had made a post-mortein examination of the body, which was that of a well-developed child. In his opinion death was due to dislocation of the spine—namely a broken neck. The mattress of the bed was very springy, and it might have had some effect in the child being thrown out on the floor.—The jury returned a verdict of " Accidental death."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-01-26_WidnesExaminer_art0148" n="art0148">
        <head>GILCHRIMLECTURES AT EARLESTOWN• --- Breathing and Fresh Air.</head>
        <p>GILCHRIMLECTURES AT EARLESTOWN• --- Breathing and Fresh Air.</p>
        <p>The first of the course of five lectures arranged to be given by the Newton-in-MakerfieldLectures Committso in connection with the Gilchrist Educational Trust took place in the Town Hall, Earlestown on Tuesday evening. The Bight Hon. Lord Newton presided,and the hall was crowded to its utmost capacity. The subject of the lee. tare was "Breathing and Fresh air," and was given by Professor W. Stirling, M.D., Professor of Physiology, Owens College, Manchester, and was illustrated by the ox-hydrogen lantern, and numeraus experiments. Lord Newton confessed his lamentable ignorance of the subject of" Breathing and Fresh Air." The first of these two things was generally done in the wrong way, and the latter was not obtained by a majority of the nopylation. His scientific observation had led him to notice that whereas the proper method of breathing was through the nostrils, there were many people who at- tempted to breathe through their mouths. He had often observed that a large proportion of our fellow creatures spent a greater part of the day time with their mouths open, and they followed the same practice at niglat,with results extremely unpleasant to their neighbours. (Laughter.) There were many societies useful and use- less in this country, and Ise should not be sorry to see a new society with a new mission, that of inducing people to keep their mouths shut, and he would not except politicians from this category. (Laughter.) He thought he might take it upon himself to express satisfaction at the large audience. He was sure that it was a great satisfaction to those public spirited people of Earlestown who made it a great part of their,. lives to improve the culture of their fellow creatures in Earlestown. It was a great pleasure to think that many people of both sexes were devoting a great portion of their leisure in coming there to obtain solid and valuable information. (Ap- plause.) Professor Stirling said that breathing was the first act that marked the independent existence of the infant, and its advent into • this work a day world. The cessation of that act was the cause of our exit from this world, from 3 physical, and to a certain extene, vital point of view. The act of breathing is not confined to animals. Plants and animals alike breathe. They do not breathe precisely by the same mechanism, but the end and object, which the animal and plant has, is precisely the same. The broad. principle was a simple one with plants and animals alike. As long as they are() living animals and plants muet continually consum a gas oxygen, and as the result of the consumption of this gas in the tissues in the plant and animal, they had a substance produced, caTled carbonic acid. By means of the lantern he had thrown on to the sheet illustra- tions of animal life showing the method of breathing on the part of worms, frogs and human beings. The illustrations embraced " Water breathers," and " Air breathers," and he said it was in reality toe living tissues of our bodies which breathe', and the gases concerned in the respiration were carried to and {rem the tissues by the blood. The proper inlet for air was through the nostrils, and he proceeded to give illustrations of the nose, the windpipe, lunge, and air cells. With the exposed body of a dead rabbit he showed the action of the luthls under the influence of on air pump, and as explaining the elasticity of the lum,s be continued the experiment with those oreans of a pig. Ira a human t. eing the lungs contained 150 millions of air vessels, and he explained in detail the movements of inspiration and expiration: aed the action of the diaphragm in inspiration. i speaking of the trechea he ehowed how t was kept open for a free pasaage of air by means of a series of rings of griscle. Passing on to the quantity of air Inspired and expired he said a evon fates in 25 cubic inchme of air per hour, and 2000, gallons of air passed through the</p>
