        <p>IDLE FOLKS. Like as Semi and Jumbo were very much alike especially Jumbo, so the Idle and Conceited Folk are very much alike, especially the Idle. Idle folk look down with disgust on calloused hand,corduroy and homespun; and having no character of their own they go to their tailor and buy one. 'Tis use- less trying to reform a confirmed Idler You might as well try to measure the moon tor a suit of clothes, or wash black-a-moor white. Idle folk are always unfortunate—so thev say. The Rev. Peter Mackenzie, talking about these unfortunate People, says' They're so dreadfully unfortunate that if. they happen to fall on their hack, they're sure to break the bridge of their nose." An idle man has only one reason for wishing to get to Heaven, and that is because he baieves that all the people on the other side of Jordan are for ever singing "We've got ne work to do." The idlest man we can think about just now is the man who, having received immense benefit froth taking Page Woodcock's Wind Pills, the finest remedy in the World for the cure of Indigestion, Wind on the Stemach, Sick Headache, Liver Complaints, Costiveness, Nervous Debility, Palpitation of the Heart, Biliousness, Sze., neglects to tell his wife, neighbours, friends, and 613C11:1109, of the great good received. Mrs. Job Norman writes from Chateaubelair, St. Vincent, 8.W.1. :—I tried one box of your Wind Pills, and find them to be excellent. Kindly send me your best terms for further supply.'" We might add, so many ladies, not only in the West Indies, but the world over. find Page Woodcock's Wind Pills excellent.—P.W. The Wind Pills being purely Vegetable, Tasteless and Mild and Tonic in theiraction, may be taken with perfect safety by the most delicate of either sex. Every hard-driven business man, every tired, weary house-wife, every working man and woman often prevented by their working conditions trom taking sufficient exercise, should take Page Wood- cock's Wind Pills. Page Woodcock's Wind Pills are sold by all Medicine Vendors at Is. lid. and 23. 9d. ; post free for price by Page Woodcock, Lincoln.</p>
