        <p>expressed his wish for a safe return from the war.—Mr. Barnes, ths departing soldier's foreman, referred to his loss at partin,4 with such a within?. worker, and her,cd whoa be iec...ve,-1 urders t the irout, tie ould be as faith fe otuie,,,: there, as he ha been at his work. Be also expressed the pleasure it would be to welcome him home.—Mr. W. Ford spoke of the pleisure it gave him to have the opp9rtunity of assisting in the send off. Whatever their opinions were as to the war, it was gratifying to find our volunteers willing to aid their country in the present crisis. He hoped Mr Nixdorf would be able to use the pre9ent on the bittle field, when otherwise he would have been deprived ofthe privilege.He wished for him a gale voyage and speedy return—Messrs F Eaton and W Sandford also spoke expressing the hope fora safe return.—M Nixdorf in replying said he was very pleased with the present and very thankfnl, he found it difficult to express his thanks in words but he was ex- ceedingly obliged for their kindness.</p>
